WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.250
ANDREW HUBERMAN: Welcome to
the Huberman Lab podcast,

00:00:02.250 --> 00:00:04.920
where we discuss science
and science-based tools

00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:05.700
for everyday life.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:09.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.830
I'm Andrew Huberman,
and I'm a professor

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.930
of neurobiology
and ophthalmology

00:00:12.930 --> 00:00:14.700
at Stanford School of Medicine.

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:16.568
Today, we are
discussing breathing.

00:00:16.568 --> 00:00:18.360
Now, breathing is
something that we are all

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:21.900
familiar with because, frankly,
we are all doing it right now.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:26.040
And we do it during our waking
states and while we are asleep.

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.140
And most of us have probably
heard that breathing

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:29.580
is essential to life.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.040
We hear that we can survive
without food for some period

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:34.320
of time, maybe even
up to a month or more,

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:36.460
that we can't survive
that long without water,

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.040
but we could survive a
few days without water,

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:40.457
depending on how well hydrated
we are when we go into that

00:00:40.457 --> 00:00:43.440
water deprivation and the heat
of the environment we happen

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.220
to be in, but that we cannot
survive without breathing

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.340
for more than a few minutes and
that if we cease to breathe,

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.290
that our brain and our
bodily tissues will die.

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:54.550
And, in fact, that is true.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.330
However, despite
everybody's knowledge

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:59.227
that breathing is
essential to life,

00:00:59.227 --> 00:01:00.810
I don't think that
most people realize

00:01:00.810 --> 00:01:02.880
just how important
how we breathe

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.890
is to our quality of life.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:07.853
And that includes our mental
health, our physical health,

00:01:07.853 --> 00:01:09.270
and what we call
performance, that

00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:11.250
is, our ability to
tap into skills,

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:14.310
either physical or cognitive,
in ways that we would not

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:17.552
be able to otherwise if we
are not breathing correctly.

00:01:17.552 --> 00:01:19.260
So today, we are going
to talk about what

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:24.510
it is to breathe correctly, both
at rest, during sleep, in order

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:28.380
to reduce our levels of
stress, in order to wake up

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.020
or to become more
alert deliberately,

00:01:31.020 --> 00:01:32.850
and many, many other
things, including

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:33.840
how to stop hiccuping.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.420
This is one of the most searched
for topics on the internet.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.400
Today, I will teach
you the one method that

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:40.560
is actually linked to science.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:43.110
No, it does not involve
drinking a glass of water

00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.270
backwards from the
opposite side of the cup

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.900
or holding your breath in
any kind of esoteric way.

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.840
It actually relates to the
neural mechanisms, that is,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.900
the brain to body connections
that cause the hiccup.

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.420
Hiccup is a spasm of
that neural circuit,

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.510
and I'll teach you how to turn
off that neural circuit in one

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:01.170
try.

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.948
And that's not a
technique I developed.

00:02:02.948 --> 00:02:04.740
It's a technique that's
actually been known

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:07.140
about for several centuries.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.669
And we now know the
underlying mechanism.

00:02:09.669 --> 00:02:11.610
So today's discussion
will give to you

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:14.460
many tools that you can apply.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:17.520
All of these tools are, of
course, behavioral tools.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:18.810
They're completely zero cost.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.420
And in telling you
how those tools work,

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:24.210
you'll learn a lot about
how the breathing, a.k.a.

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:26.850
the respiratory, system,
works and how it interfaces

00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:29.040
with the other organs
and tissues of the body,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.362
in particular the brain.

00:02:30.362 --> 00:02:32.070
In fact, one of the
most important things

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:34.860
to understand about breathing
right here at the outset

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:37.800
is that breathing is unique
among brain and bodily

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.640
functions in that it lies at the
interface between our conscious

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:43.590
and our subconscious behavior.

00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:45.720
And it represents
a bridge literally

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.410
in the brain between the
conscious and the subconscious.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:50.950
What do I mean by that?

00:02:50.950 --> 00:02:53.670
Well, breathing does not
require that we pay attention

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:55.170
to our breathing
or that we are even

00:02:55.170 --> 00:02:56.337
aware that we are breathing.

00:02:56.337 --> 00:02:58.985
It will just carry on in the
background either normally

00:02:58.985 --> 00:03:00.360
or abnormally,
and I'll teach you

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:03.460
what normal and abnormal
breathing is in a little bit.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.060
However, breathing is unique
among brain and bodily

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.600
functions in that at any
moment, we can consciously

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.800
take control of how we breathe.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:14.850
This is an absolutely
spectacular and highly unusual

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.380
feature of brain function.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:18.620
For instance, your
digestion is carrying on

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.370
in the background right
now whether or not

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:22.500
you've had food recently or not.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:24.900
But you can't simply
control your digestion

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.680
by thinking about it
in a particular way.

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:29.340
In fact, most people can't
even control their thinking

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:30.840
by trying to control
their thinking.

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:32.257
That actually takes
some practice.

00:03:32.257 --> 00:03:34.420
It can be done-- a topic
for a future episode.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:36.360
However, breathing is unique.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.200
Breathing will carry on
involuntarily, subconsciously

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.940
in the background,
as I said before.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.000
But if, at any moment, you want
to hold your breath or inhale

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:48.780
more deeply or vigorously or
exhale longer than you inhale,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:49.770
you can do that.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:54.330
Very few, if any, other neural
circuits in your brain and body

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.160
allow that level of control.

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:59.680
And it turns out that level
of control is not an accident.

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.960
It has been hypothesized that
by controlling breathing,

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:06.690
the brain is actually
attempting to control

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.060
its own state of mind.

00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:13.185
Now, the way this was originally
stated in a scientific research

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:14.560
paper was a little
bit different.

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:15.990
It was a little
bit physiological.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:19.260
The statement was, "The brain,
by regulating breathing,

00:04:19.260 --> 00:04:21.420
controls its own excitability."

00:04:21.420 --> 00:04:23.850
Excitability in the
context of neurobiology

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:28.590
is how able the brain is to
take in new information or not,

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:33.840
how able the brain is or not to
turn itself off to go to sleep

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:38.580
and to regulate its own levels
of anxiety, focus, et cetera.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.800
If that seems a
little bit abstract,

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:42.060
I'll make it simple for you.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.400
By changing your
pattern of breathing,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:48.665
you can very quickly change what
your brain is capable of doing.

00:04:48.665 --> 00:04:50.040
In fact, a little
bit later, I'll

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.620
tell you that while
you inhale, you

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.590
are far better at learning
and remembering information

00:04:55.590 --> 00:04:56.640
than during an exhale.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:04:58.782
And it is a very
significant difference.

00:04:58.782 --> 00:05:00.990
Does that mean you should
only inhale and not exhale?

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:02.070
No, of course not.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.440
I'll teach you how to breathe
for the sake of learning

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:07.410
and memory as well as
for physical performance

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:08.760
and a number of other things.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:10.760
So hopefully I've been
able to highlight for you

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.040
the importance of breathing
not just for life,

00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:15.780
because, yes, breathing
is essential for life,

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:18.350
but that the subtleties
of how we breathe,

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.560
the duration and
intensity of our inhales

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:25.820
and our exhales, how long we
hold our breath between inhales

00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:30.340
and exhales, very critically
defines our state of mind

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:32.710
and our state of body,
what we are able to do

00:05:32.710 --> 00:05:34.030
and what we are not able to do.

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:36.790
And the great news is we
can control our breathing

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:39.790
and, in doing so, control
our mental health, physical

00:05:39.790 --> 00:05:41.140
health, and performance.

