00:09:23.740 --> 00:09:25.360
Let's talk about breathing.

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.190
And, of course, we
breathe in order

00:09:27.190 --> 00:09:29.260
to bring oxygen into the body.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:32.260
But we also breathe to remove
certain things from our body,

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:33.980
in particular carbon dioxide.

00:09:33.980 --> 00:09:36.070
So the main players
in today's discussion

00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:38.690
are going to be oxygen
and carbon dioxide.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:41.920
Now, a common misconception
is that oxygen is good

00:09:41.920 --> 00:09:43.780
and carbon dioxide is bad.

00:09:43.780 --> 00:09:45.640
That's simply not the case.

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:47.950
Let's just take a step
back from that statement,

00:09:47.950 --> 00:09:49.420
and let's think about this.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:52.000
When we breathe
in, we are largely

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:55.662
breathing in air in order to
bring oxygen into our body.

00:09:55.662 --> 00:09:57.370
And we can just stop
right there and say,

00:09:57.370 --> 00:09:58.760
why do we breathe at all?

00:09:58.760 --> 00:10:01.210
Why can't we just get oxygen
from the world around us?

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:04.990
Well, it's because oxygen
can't diffuse through our skin

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:06.850
into the deeper
cells of our body.

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:10.510
Other single cell and
very simple organisms

00:10:10.510 --> 00:10:12.400
can actually bring
oxygen into their system

00:10:12.400 --> 00:10:13.840
without the need to breathe.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:16.750
But we have to breathe
in order to bring oxygen

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:18.730
to the cells that
reside deep in our body.

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:20.283
In particular, our
brain cells, which

00:10:20.283 --> 00:10:22.450
are the most metabolically
active cells in our body,

00:10:22.450 --> 00:10:25.450
require a lot of oxygen.
And those brain cells

00:10:25.450 --> 00:10:27.950
are sitting, of course,
in the brain, which

00:10:27.950 --> 00:10:30.750
is encased in the
cranial vault, the skull.

00:10:30.750 --> 00:10:33.090
And so oxygen can't simply
pass to those cells.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:36.170
So we need to have a system
that will deliver oxygen

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:37.320
to those cells.

00:10:37.320 --> 00:10:39.470
We also need a system,
which turns out

00:10:39.470 --> 00:10:41.960
to be the breathing or
respiratory system, that

00:10:41.960 --> 00:10:45.710
can offload or remove the
gas that we call carbon

00:10:45.710 --> 00:10:48.230
dioxide, not because
carbon dioxide is bad

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:50.792
but because too much of it
in our system is not good.

00:10:50.792 --> 00:10:52.250
In fact, much of
today's discussion

00:10:52.250 --> 00:10:55.580
will also center around
the common misconception

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:58.550
that carbon dioxide is something
that we want to get rid of.

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:02.090
You don't want to get rid of
too much carbon dioxide or else

00:11:02.090 --> 00:11:05.240
you can't actually get oxygen
to the cells and tissues

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:07.230
of your body in
an efficient way.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:11.250
So you need oxygen and you need
carbon dioxide in your body.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:13.880
You also need to be able
to offload or remove

00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:18.360
carbon dioxide and bring in
oxygen in the correct ratios

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:20.720
so that you can perform the
kind of mental functions

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:23.220
and physical functions
that you want to.

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.820
So if we just dial
out even further,

00:11:25.820 --> 00:11:29.240
we say, what are the key
components of breathing?

00:11:29.240 --> 00:11:32.600
What are the elements
within the body that

00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:35.900
allow us to bring oxygen
to the tissues and cells

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:39.170
as is required and remove
carbon dioxide from the body

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:41.960
as is required and yet keep
enough carbon dioxide around

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:44.880
in order to allow
oxygen to do its thing?

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.800
Well, that breathing or
respiratory apparatus

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:50.570
has two major
components, and I'm

00:11:50.570 --> 00:11:52.160
going to just briefly
describe those.

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:54.830
And as I do this, I really
want to highlight the fact

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:57.877
that any time you're thinking
about biology and physiology

00:11:57.877 --> 00:11:59.960
in particular, whether or
not it's about the brain

00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.090
or the liver or
the gut microbiome,

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:08.290
it's useful to categorize things
either as mechanical mechanisms

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:10.120
or chemical mechanisms.

00:12:10.120 --> 00:12:11.150
What do I mean by that?

00:12:11.150 --> 00:12:13.390
Well, let's just take
the analogy of hunger.

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:16.000
There are mechanical
mechanisms that

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.440
tell us when we should eat.

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:20.110
For instance, you have neurons,
nerve cells in your gut

00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:24.640
that signal how
stretched or nonstretched

00:12:24.640 --> 00:12:27.700
the walls of your
stomach are, how full

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:30.490
or how empty your gut is,
and send that information

00:12:30.490 --> 00:12:34.420
to the brain to make you feel
to some extent hungry or not

00:12:34.420 --> 00:12:35.110
hungry.

00:12:35.110 --> 00:12:37.780
In general, when our
stomach is very full

00:12:37.780 --> 00:12:40.960
and especially if it's very
distended, even with liquid,

00:12:40.960 --> 00:12:42.190
it suppresses our hunger.

00:12:42.190 --> 00:12:45.430
Whereas when our stomach is
devoid of that mechanical

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:47.770
pressure, especially
for a number of hours,

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:49.990
it tends to trigger
hunger by signaling

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:52.160
via neurons to the brain.

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:55.420
In addition, there are chemical
signals that go from the gut

00:12:55.420 --> 00:12:56.210
to the brain.

00:12:56.210 --> 00:12:58.000
For instance, we have
neurons in our gut

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.610
that can detect the
presence of amino acids

00:13:00.610 --> 00:13:04.360
from proteins that we eat,
fatty acids from the foods

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:08.080
that we eat, the lipids,
and sugars, different forms

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:09.280
of carbohydrate.

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.130
The neurons in our gut
are paying attention to

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:16.480
or respond to how much
amino acid, fatty acid,

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:19.090
and carbohydrate is in
our gut and sends signals

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:21.730
to the brain to either
stimulate or suppress hunger.

00:13:21.730 --> 00:13:23.722
So those are
chemical signals that

00:13:23.722 --> 00:13:25.180
are being passed
from gut to brain,

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.660
and they work in parallel
with the mechanical signals.

00:13:28.660 --> 00:13:30.400
And this idea of
"in parallel with,"

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:32.420
again, is a very common
theme in biology,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:33.580
especially neuroscience.

00:13:33.580 --> 00:13:35.800
The term parallel pathways
refers to the fact

00:13:35.800 --> 00:13:38.860
that any time there's a
critical bodily function,

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:41.860
it's very unlikely that just
one type of information,

00:13:41.860 --> 00:13:43.540
like just mechanical
information,

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:46.030
is going to be used.
/ Almost always,

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:49.060
it's going to be mechanical
and chemical information.

00:13:49.060 --> 00:13:51.040
I could pick a number
of other examples.

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:53.950
For instance, if
you want to avoid

00:13:53.950 --> 00:13:56.620
damaging your skin or other
tissues of your body, which

00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:00.730
is essential to life, well, then
you have mechanical information

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:02.800
about, for instance,
whether or not something

00:14:02.800 --> 00:14:05.530
is pinching or ready
to pierce your skin.

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:06.850
That's mechanical information.

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.940
It's sent via specific
neurons up to the brain

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:12.340
to signal a retraction
reflex if you

00:14:12.340 --> 00:14:14.680
move your limb away from
wherever that intense pressure

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:15.490
is coming.

00:14:15.490 --> 00:14:17.680
You also have chemical
sensing in your skin,

00:14:17.680 --> 00:14:21.400
the presence of things
that elicit a burn

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:25.120
or that elicit itch or
that elicit extreme cold.

00:14:25.120 --> 00:14:27.250
All of that chemical
information is

00:14:27.250 --> 00:14:29.380
being signaled up to the
brain as well in parallel.

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:31.250
So parallel pathways
is a common theme.

00:14:31.250 --> 00:14:33.550
So when we're thinking about
the respiration, a.k.a.

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:35.835
the breathing,
system, we also need

00:14:35.835 --> 00:14:37.210
to look at the
mechanical system.

00:14:37.210 --> 00:14:38.585
What are the
different components

00:14:38.585 --> 00:14:40.990
of the nose, the mouth,
the lungs, et cetera, that

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:43.030
allow oxygen to be
brought in and carbon

00:14:43.030 --> 00:14:46.300
dioxide to be removed from the
body but not too much carbon

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:48.580
dioxide removed
to allow breathing

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:52.640
to work as efficiently and
as optimally as possible?

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:54.880
And then we also need to
look at the chemical systems

00:14:54.880 --> 00:14:59.470
of the lungs, the bloodstream,
and how different cells use

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:01.570
oxygen and carbon
dioxide in order

00:15:01.570 --> 00:15:03.610
to understand that as well.

00:15:03.610 --> 00:15:06.640
If you can understand the
mechanical and chemical aspects

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:09.670
of breathing, even
just at a top contour,

00:15:09.670 --> 00:15:11.470
well, then the
various tools that I

00:15:11.470 --> 00:15:14.770
discuss during today's
episode, such as the ability

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:17.770
to calm yourself down most
quickly by doing what's

00:15:17.770 --> 00:15:19.248
called a physiological sigh--

00:15:19.248 --> 00:15:21.290
I'll go into this in more
detail in a little bit,

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:24.800
but this is two very deep
inhales through the nose.

00:15:24.800 --> 00:15:27.658
So the first one is a long
inhale [INHALES DEEPLY],,

00:15:27.658 --> 00:15:29.950
and then the second one after
that is [INHALES SHARPLY]

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:33.100
a quick, sharp inhale to
maximally inflate your lungs,

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:37.180
followed by a full exhale
through the mouth to lungs

00:15:37.180 --> 00:15:38.210
completely empty.

00:15:38.210 --> 00:15:40.300
So it's big inhale
through the nose,

00:15:40.300 --> 00:15:43.000
then short inhale
through the nose

00:15:43.000 --> 00:15:46.030
immediately after that in order
to maximally inflate the lungs,

00:15:46.030 --> 00:15:48.910
and then a long exhale
through the mouth

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.070
until your lungs are empty.

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:54.430
You will understand why that
particular pattern of breathing

00:15:54.430 --> 00:15:57.110
and not simply one
inhale or not simply

00:15:57.110 --> 00:15:59.980
an inhale through the nose
and an exhale through the nose

00:15:59.980 --> 00:16:04.300
as well is optimal for
reducing your stress quickly.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:06.970
That double inhale through the
nose followed by a long exhale

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:11.590
through the mouth works to
reduce your levels of stress

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:14.590
and lower your levels of
so-called autonomic arousal

00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:16.390
very fast in real time.

00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:19.700
And it works better than
any other known approach.

00:16:19.700 --> 00:16:20.590
It's not a hack.

00:16:20.590 --> 00:16:22.510
This is actually something
that your body has

00:16:22.510 --> 00:16:24.670
specific neural circuits
to do, and it actually

00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:27.310
performs during sleep on
a regular basis and even

00:16:27.310 --> 00:16:30.440
throughout the day, and that
you can perform voluntarily.

00:16:30.440 --> 00:16:32.590
And it works so well
to reduce stress

00:16:32.590 --> 00:16:35.260
very quickly not
because it brings

00:16:35.260 --> 00:16:37.212
in the maximum amount
of oxygen and removes

00:16:37.212 --> 00:16:38.920
the maximum amount of
carbon dioxide but,

00:16:38.920 --> 00:16:42.550
rather, because it optimally
balances oxygen and carbon

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:43.370
dioxide.

00:16:43.370 --> 00:16:45.790
If you understand the
mechanical and chemical aspects

00:16:45.790 --> 00:16:48.400
of breathing, then you
will understand exactly why

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:49.990
that particular
pattern of breathing,

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:51.730
the so-called
physiological sigh,

00:16:51.730 --> 00:16:54.610
is the most efficient
way to rapidly reduce

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:55.805
stress in real time.

00:16:55.805 --> 00:16:58.180
If you can understand the
mechanical and chemical aspects

00:16:58.180 --> 00:17:01.060
of breathing, you will
also understand why

00:17:01.060 --> 00:17:03.970
most people are overbreathing.

00:17:03.970 --> 00:17:06.700
That is, they're breathing
too often, even if they're

00:17:06.700 --> 00:17:08.470
breathing in a shallow manner.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:10.329
They're breathing too often.

00:17:10.329 --> 00:17:13.540
And they are blowing off
or removing too much carbon

00:17:13.540 --> 00:17:14.390
dioxide.

00:17:14.390 --> 00:17:16.329
And if you understand
that carbon dioxide is

00:17:16.329 --> 00:17:18.670
critical for the way
that oxygen is delivered

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:21.079
from the bloodstream to
the tissues of the body,

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:22.750
including the
brain, well, then it

00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:24.819
will make very good
sense as to why

00:17:24.819 --> 00:17:26.859
people who are
breathing too much

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:30.850
don't actually experience all
the effects of elevated oxygen,

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:33.190
but, rather, they're putting
their body into what's

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:34.510
called a hypoxic state.

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:36.340
They're not getting
enough oxygen

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:38.770
to the tissues of their body,
in particular their brain.

00:17:38.770 --> 00:17:41.080
And this is true not just
for people who are obese

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:44.050
or who suffer from sleep apnea,
although that's certainly

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:47.050
the case, but for people
that have, believe it or not,

00:17:47.050 --> 00:17:48.632
certain personality types.

00:17:48.632 --> 00:17:50.590
We'll talk about breathing
and personality type

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:52.048
and actually how
breathing has been

00:17:52.048 --> 00:17:54.010
shown to alter personality.

00:17:54.010 --> 00:17:54.610
That's right.

00:17:54.610 --> 00:17:57.580
Breathing can alter personality
in positive ways that

00:17:57.580 --> 00:18:01.840
allow anyone to show up to the
various social and nonsocial

00:18:01.840 --> 00:18:04.270
endeavors of their life
with more calm, more focus,

00:18:04.270 --> 00:18:06.670
alertness, and improve
their overall health.

00:18:06.670 --> 00:18:08.980
OK, so let's talk about
the mechanical components

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:09.770
of breathing.

00:18:09.770 --> 00:18:11.260
It's really quite simple.

00:18:11.260 --> 00:18:14.493
You've got your nose, obviously,
and you've got your mouth.

00:18:14.493 --> 00:18:15.910
And a little bit
later, we'll talk

00:18:15.910 --> 00:18:19.990
about the incredible advantages
of being a nasal breather

00:18:19.990 --> 00:18:23.740
most of the time but also
the incredible advantages

00:18:23.740 --> 00:18:27.250
of using your mouth to breathe
both for inhales and exhales

00:18:27.250 --> 00:18:30.430
during particular
types of endeavors.

00:18:30.430 --> 00:18:32.690
And we'll get back to
that a little later.

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:35.530
But for the meantime,
the only two ways

00:18:35.530 --> 00:18:38.590
to bring air into your
system are through your nose

00:18:38.590 --> 00:18:40.630
and through your mouth.

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:45.370
We also have the larynx, which
is a rigid tissue or pipe that

00:18:45.370 --> 00:18:49.220
brings the air from the nose
and mouth down to the lungs.

00:18:49.220 --> 00:18:51.940
Now, that word rigid is
really important here

00:18:51.940 --> 00:18:56.050
because what we will soon learn
is that your lungs basically

00:18:56.050 --> 00:18:57.470
act like a pump.

00:18:57.470 --> 00:18:58.720
You sort of know this already.

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:00.730
But these are two
big bags basically

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:05.840
that can fill with air or
that can squeeze air out.

00:19:05.840 --> 00:19:07.390
Now, what most
people don't realize

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:10.270
is that the lungs are not
just too big bags of air.

00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:13.690
Your lungs are actually too big
bags of air that inside of them

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:15.550
have hundreds of
millions of little sacs

00:19:15.550 --> 00:19:17.258
that are called the
alveoli of the lungs.

00:19:17.258 --> 00:19:19.600
And by having those hundreds
of millions of little sacs,

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:22.870
you increase the surface
area of the lungs.

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:24.730
And by increasing
the surface area,

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:28.540
you allow more oxygen to pass
from the air in your lungs

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:32.650
into the bloodstream than if
you didn't have those sacs.

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:35.440
And you allow more
carbon dioxide

00:19:35.440 --> 00:19:38.410
to move from the bloodstream
into those sacs of the lungs,

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:41.230
and then when you exhale, the
carbon dioxide can be removed.

00:19:41.230 --> 00:19:43.510
So those little sacs we
call alveoli of the lungs

00:19:43.510 --> 00:19:46.180
are an important part
of the mechanical aspect

00:19:46.180 --> 00:19:49.040
of breathing we'll get
to a little bit later.

00:19:49.040 --> 00:19:52.480
So at a first pass, the
mechanical aspects of breathing

00:19:52.480 --> 00:19:53.690
are really straightforward.

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:54.620
You can breathe
through your nose.

00:19:54.620 --> 00:19:55.480
You can through your mouth.

00:19:55.480 --> 00:19:56.813
It goes down through the larynx.

00:19:56.813 --> 00:19:58.720
I told you the larynx
is a rigid pipe.

00:19:58.720 --> 00:19:59.950
The lungs are not rigid.

00:19:59.950 --> 00:20:01.780
They can expand and
they can contract

00:20:01.780 --> 00:20:06.340
like a pump to bring
in air or to expel air.

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:08.740
Keep in mind that
the lungs do not

00:20:08.740 --> 00:20:10.490
have any muscles themselves.

00:20:10.490 --> 00:20:14.770
So we need muscles that can
either squeeze the lungs

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:17.380
or that will allow
the lungs to expand.

00:20:17.380 --> 00:20:20.020
And there are two general
groups of muscles that do that,

00:20:20.020 --> 00:20:23.320
and they are the diaphragm
and the so-called intercostal

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:23.950
muscles.

00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:27.010
The diaphragm is a thin muscle
that sits below the lungs

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:28.850
and above the liver.

00:20:28.850 --> 00:20:31.810
And when we inhale,
provided that we

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:35.080
are using what's called
diaphragmatic breathing, that

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:37.030
diaphragm contracts.

00:20:37.030 --> 00:20:39.140
And when it contracts,
it moves down,

00:20:39.140 --> 00:20:43.600
which allows more space for
the lungs to inflate with air.

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:47.292
Now, the intercostal muscles are
the muscles between our ribs.

00:20:47.292 --> 00:20:49.250
A number of people probably
don't realize this.

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:50.920
But your ribs, of
course, are bone,

00:20:50.920 --> 00:20:52.900
but in between those
bones, you have muscles.

00:20:52.900 --> 00:20:55.750
And the intercostal
muscles, when you inhale,

00:20:55.750 --> 00:20:59.200
contract, and that allows
your rib cage to move up

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:00.772
and to expand a bit.

00:21:00.772 --> 00:21:02.230
And I think, again,
people probably

00:21:02.230 --> 00:21:04.397
don't realize that your
ribs are not fixed in place.

00:21:04.397 --> 00:21:07.120
They can actually get
further and closer apart

00:21:07.120 --> 00:21:08.090
from one another.

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:11.170
So when you inhale, your
rib cage actually moves up.

00:21:11.170 --> 00:21:13.760
Sometimes the shoulders
will move up as well.

00:21:13.760 --> 00:21:15.730
And that's because those
intercostal muscles

00:21:15.730 --> 00:21:17.980
are contracting.

00:21:17.980 --> 00:21:20.980
Now, muscles can't
move on their own.

00:21:20.980 --> 00:21:22.540
They are controlled by nerves.

00:21:22.540 --> 00:21:25.750
So we've got the nose,
the mouth, the larynx,

00:21:25.750 --> 00:21:26.450
and the lungs.

00:21:26.450 --> 00:21:28.450
The lungs have all those
little alveoli in them.

00:21:28.450 --> 00:21:31.030
And as I told you,
we've got the diaphragm

00:21:31.030 --> 00:21:33.250
as a muscle to move
the lungs, and we

00:21:33.250 --> 00:21:36.130
have the intercostal muscles
to move the ribs, which

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:37.603
can allow the lungs to expand.

00:21:37.603 --> 00:21:39.520
Again, we're just on the
mechanical components

00:21:39.520 --> 00:21:40.062
of breathing.

00:21:40.062 --> 00:21:42.170
But because muscles
can't move themselves,

00:21:42.170 --> 00:21:44.200
you should be asking,
what moves the muscles?

00:21:44.200 --> 00:21:48.370
And it's really nerves
that control muscles.

00:21:48.370 --> 00:21:51.040
So whether or not you're
contracting your biceps

00:21:51.040 --> 00:21:53.620
or you're walking and you're
contracting your quadriceps

00:21:53.620 --> 00:21:55.660
and your hamstrings
and your calf muscles,

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:59.290
it's neurons, nerve
cells that control that.

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:02.140
There's a specialized nerve
called the phrenic nerve,

00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:08.433
P-H-R-E-N-I-C, phrenic nerve,
that comes out of the neck.

00:22:08.433 --> 00:22:10.600
And when I say it comes out
of the neck, what I mean

00:22:10.600 --> 00:22:14.110
is that there are little neurons
that reside in the brainstem,

00:22:14.110 --> 00:22:16.540
in the back of your brain,
and they send little wires

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:20.810
that we call axons down
and out of the neck.

00:22:20.810 --> 00:22:24.550
They go close to the heart
and a little bit behind it.

00:22:24.550 --> 00:22:27.010
And they go down, and
they form synapses.

00:22:27.010 --> 00:22:30.070
That is, they form connections
with the diaphragm.

00:22:30.070 --> 00:22:32.863
And when those neurons release
neurotransmitters, which

00:22:32.863 --> 00:22:34.780
are little chemicals,
the diaphragm contracts,

00:22:34.780 --> 00:22:36.350
and it moves down.

00:22:36.350 --> 00:22:38.860
So we say that the phrenic
nerve is a motor nerve.

00:22:38.860 --> 00:22:40.900
It's designed to move muscle.

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:44.530
However, the phrenic nerve, like
a few other nerves in the body,

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:48.790
is interesting in that it has
not just motor nerves in there,

00:22:48.790 --> 00:22:51.320
neurons that control the
contraction of muscles.

00:22:51.320 --> 00:22:56.210
It also can sense things,
has sensory neurons.

00:22:56.210 --> 00:22:59.320
So it also sends connections
down to the diaphragm

00:22:59.320 --> 00:23:01.660
and actually down deep
into the diaphragm

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:03.130
and close to the liver.

00:23:03.130 --> 00:23:05.112
And note that I said
liver twice now already,

00:23:05.112 --> 00:23:06.820
and we're going to
get back to this later

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:08.590
when we talk about
physical movement

00:23:08.590 --> 00:23:11.050
and cramps of the body.

00:23:11.050 --> 00:23:14.350
Those sensory neurons dive
deep into the diaphragm.

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:16.270
And then they go
back up to the brain,

00:23:16.270 --> 00:23:19.390
and they allow you to sense
where the diaphragm is.

00:23:19.390 --> 00:23:21.490
So they're giving
information about where

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:22.780
the diaphragm is in your body.

00:23:22.780 --> 00:23:24.700
Now, most of the time, you're
not paying attention to this.

00:23:24.700 --> 00:23:26.360
But right now, you
can actually try this.

00:23:26.360 --> 00:23:27.902
And I would encourage
you to do this.

00:23:27.902 --> 00:23:30.820
Diaphragmatic breathing
is, in many ways,

00:23:30.820 --> 00:23:33.795
the ideal way to breathe and
that it's the most efficient

00:23:33.795 --> 00:23:34.420
way to breathe.

00:23:34.420 --> 00:23:36.340
We'll talk about what
we mean exactly when we

00:23:36.340 --> 00:23:38.590
say breathing efficiency later.

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:42.340
But the diaphragm
is designed to allow

00:23:42.340 --> 00:23:44.920
the lungs to expand or
to contract the lungs,

00:23:44.920 --> 00:23:47.020
to bring air into
the body or to remove

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:48.980
carbon dioxide from the body.

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:51.250
And if you want to
know whether or not

00:23:51.250 --> 00:23:53.980
you're using diaphragmatic
breathing, it's very simple.

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:56.757
If you inhale-- probably best
to do this through the nose,

00:23:56.757 --> 00:23:58.340
but you could do it
through the mouth.

00:23:58.340 --> 00:24:02.230
If you inhale and your belly
moves outward on the inhale,

00:24:02.230 --> 00:24:05.170
well, then that phrenic nerve
is controlling your diaphragm

00:24:05.170 --> 00:24:05.860
properly.

00:24:05.860 --> 00:24:07.780
And then when you
exhale, your belly

00:24:07.780 --> 00:24:09.410
should go in just a little bit.

00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:11.650
That's diaphragmatic breathing.

00:24:11.650 --> 00:24:14.050
Now, diaphragmatic
breathing is talked

00:24:14.050 --> 00:24:15.890
about in the context of yoga.

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:18.880
It's often talked about as a
way to calm down and so on.

00:24:18.880 --> 00:24:20.740
But diaphragmatic
breathing is just one mode

00:24:20.740 --> 00:24:22.990
by which your brain
and the phrenic nerve

00:24:22.990 --> 00:24:24.670
can control muscle,
the diaphragm,

00:24:24.670 --> 00:24:27.520
to control the mechanical
aspects of the lungs

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:29.860
to bring in air and expel air.

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:31.360
As I mentioned
before, you also have

00:24:31.360 --> 00:24:34.840
these muscles between your ribs
or the intercostal muscles.

00:24:34.840 --> 00:24:36.700
And there's a
separate set of nerves

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:39.280
that allow those muscles to
contract and for your rib cage

00:24:39.280 --> 00:24:42.700
to expand in order to create
more room for your lungs

00:24:42.700 --> 00:24:45.940
to get larger and fill with
air or for your rib cage

00:24:45.940 --> 00:24:50.050
to contract a bit when
those muscles relax in order

00:24:50.050 --> 00:24:51.820
to expel air.

00:24:51.820 --> 00:24:53.560
I'd like to go on
record by saying

00:24:53.560 --> 00:24:57.400
that there is no rule that
diaphragmatic breathing is

00:24:57.400 --> 00:25:00.470
better than breathing
where your rib cage moves.

00:25:00.470 --> 00:25:01.780
This is a common misconception.

00:25:01.780 --> 00:25:04.072
People say, oh, if your
shoulders are going up and down

00:25:04.072 --> 00:25:06.587
and your rib cage is moving
while you're breathing,

00:25:06.587 --> 00:25:08.170
well, then you're
not breathing right.

00:25:08.170 --> 00:25:10.855
And if your belly goes out
and the rest of your body

00:25:10.855 --> 00:25:12.730
is still while you
breathe, well, then you're

00:25:12.730 --> 00:25:13.690
breathing correctly.

00:25:13.690 --> 00:25:16.000
I know of zero--

00:25:16.000 --> 00:25:19.330
in fact, zero minus one data
to support that statement.

00:25:19.330 --> 00:25:21.760
You have multiple
parallel mechanisms

00:25:21.760 --> 00:25:24.607
to control the mechanics of
your lungs and for breathing.

00:25:24.607 --> 00:25:26.440
And when you're exerting
yourself very hard,

00:25:26.440 --> 00:25:28.780
you tend to use both
the intercostal muscles

00:25:28.780 --> 00:25:31.870
and your rib cage moving as
well as your diaphragm in order

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:34.240
to bring in a lot of
oxygen and to offload

00:25:34.240 --> 00:25:35.960
a lot of carbon dioxide.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:37.870
And when you're
calmer, frankly, you

00:25:37.870 --> 00:25:39.280
could use
diaphragmatic breathing

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:42.550
or you could use rib cage
type breathing in order

00:25:42.550 --> 00:25:44.590
to bring enough oxygen
into your system.

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:49.000
There's no real data showing
that diaphragmatic breathing is

00:25:49.000 --> 00:25:50.550
somehow better or worse.

00:25:50.550 --> 00:25:53.750
However, being able to
mechanically control those

00:25:53.750 --> 00:25:56.210
independently or to combine
them and use them together

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:58.550
is of tremendous power
toward regulating

00:25:58.550 --> 00:25:59.883
your mental and physical states.

00:25:59.883 --> 00:26:02.175
And we'll talk about how to
do that a little bit later.

00:26:02.175 --> 00:26:03.980
For right now, please
understand that you

00:26:03.980 --> 00:26:06.710
have these different
mechanical components that

00:26:06.710 --> 00:26:11.900
allow you to bring oxygen into
your system and to expel air

00:26:11.900 --> 00:26:15.290
and to thereby offload carbon
dioxide from your system.

00:26:15.290 --> 00:26:18.890
Again, we haven't talked about
the gas exchange of carbon

00:26:18.890 --> 00:26:20.300
dioxide and oxygen
and how that's

00:26:20.300 --> 00:26:21.050
happening in the bloodstream.

00:26:21.050 --> 00:26:22.175
We'll talk about that next.

00:26:22.175 --> 00:26:24.770
But the basic mechanical
components are pretty simple.

00:26:24.770 --> 00:26:28.970
Once again, just to review,
it's nose, mouth, larynx, lungs,

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:31.552
alveoli within the lungs,
and then those two muscles,

00:26:31.552 --> 00:26:33.260
the diaphragm and the
intercostal muscles

00:26:33.260 --> 00:26:34.350
of the ribs.

00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:36.260
And one thing I
failed to mention

00:26:36.260 --> 00:26:40.520
is why it's so important that
that larynx be rigid, that it's

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:42.650
a tube that is very rigid.

00:26:42.650 --> 00:26:45.590
And the reason for that is
that unlike the lungs, which

00:26:45.590 --> 00:26:48.320
you want to act as
sort of a bellow pump

00:26:48.320 --> 00:26:52.280
where you can deflate it and
inflate it in order to move air

00:26:52.280 --> 00:26:56.060
in and out, the larynx
needs to be rigid

00:26:56.060 --> 00:26:58.310
so that it doesn't collapse
while you're bringing air

00:26:58.310 --> 00:26:58.850
in and out.

00:26:58.850 --> 00:27:02.180
You can imagine that if it was
a very flimsy tube or the walls

00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:04.310
of the larynx were
very flimsy and thin,

00:27:04.310 --> 00:27:07.100
well, then you can imagine
breathing in very vigorously,

00:27:07.100 --> 00:27:09.800
and it would shut
like a tube that

00:27:09.800 --> 00:27:12.600
suddenly flattens on itself,
which would not be good.

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:14.720
So the fact that
the larynx is rigid

00:27:14.720 --> 00:27:17.670
is actually a very crucial
part of this whole system.

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:20.060
The other important
aspect of this system

00:27:20.060 --> 00:27:22.250
as it relates to the
mechanics of breathing

00:27:22.250 --> 00:27:24.620
is the fact that your
nose and your mouth

00:27:24.620 --> 00:27:26.990
have different
resistances to air.

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:29.048
You can probably
notice this right now

00:27:29.048 --> 00:27:30.590
if you were to, for
instance, breathe

00:27:30.590 --> 00:27:32.300
in through your mouth
[INHALES] and only

00:27:32.300 --> 00:27:33.590
through your mouth
versus breathing

00:27:33.590 --> 00:27:34.715
through your nose [SNIFFS].

00:27:34.715 --> 00:27:37.010
Some of you perhaps have
a harder time breathing in

00:27:37.010 --> 00:27:37.580
through your nose.

00:27:37.580 --> 00:27:38.960
By the way, it's
perfectly normal

00:27:38.960 --> 00:27:41.355
that one or the
other nostril would

00:27:41.355 --> 00:27:43.730
be harder to breathe through
or easier to breathe through

00:27:43.730 --> 00:27:45.200
and that switches
across the day.

00:27:45.200 --> 00:27:47.857
It has to do with the flow
of mucus and cerebrospinal

00:27:47.857 --> 00:27:49.190
fluid and intracranial pressure.

00:27:49.190 --> 00:27:50.678
Totally normal.

00:27:50.678 --> 00:27:52.220
Many people out
there think they have

00:27:52.220 --> 00:27:53.870
a deviated septum
who don't actually

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:55.100
have a deviated septum.

00:27:55.100 --> 00:27:56.210
A little bit later,
we'll talk about how

00:27:56.210 --> 00:27:58.820
to repair a deviated septum
without surgery because that

00:27:58.820 --> 00:28:01.340
actually is possible
in many, not all, cases

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:04.760
and is immensely
beneficial to do.

00:28:04.760 --> 00:28:07.400
But what we know is that
breathing in through the nose

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:09.227
is a little bit harder,
and it's supposed

00:28:09.227 --> 00:28:10.310
to be a little bit harder.

00:28:10.310 --> 00:28:13.760
However, because it's a little
bit harder because there's

00:28:13.760 --> 00:28:16.370
more resistance, as we
say, you are actually

00:28:16.370 --> 00:28:19.790
able to draw more force into
these mechanical aspects

00:28:19.790 --> 00:28:21.680
of the breathing
apparatus and actually

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:24.870
bring more air into your lungs.

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:26.150
You can try this right now.

00:28:26.150 --> 00:28:28.970
Try breathing in through your
mouth to maximally inflate

00:28:28.970 --> 00:28:32.390
your lungs and try and do it
through mostly diaphragmatic

00:28:32.390 --> 00:28:34.080
breathing, just for
sake of example.

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:36.330
In other words, try and
breathe in through your mouth.

00:28:36.330 --> 00:28:38.678
And as you do that, have your
belly expand and maximally

00:28:38.678 --> 00:28:39.470
inflate your lungs.

00:28:39.470 --> 00:28:42.170
I'll do it right now with you
so that we can do it together

00:28:42.170 --> 00:28:44.990
and I can prove to
everyone that I'm just as

00:28:44.990 --> 00:28:47.210
deficient in this as you are.

00:28:47.210 --> 00:28:49.010
[INHALES]

00:28:49.010 --> 00:28:51.770
OK, so I can inflate
my stomach doing that.

00:28:51.770 --> 00:28:53.440
But now try doing
it with your nose,

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:55.940
and please do exhale before you
try doing it with your nose.

00:28:55.940 --> 00:28:57.680
With your nose, you're going
to feel more resistance,

00:28:57.680 --> 00:29:00.275
but you'll notice that you can
inflate it quite a bit further.

00:29:00.275 --> 00:29:05.630
[SNIFFS] And you'll feel your
entire cavity, your belly

00:29:05.630 --> 00:29:08.690
and maybe even in your lower
back, fill with some pressure.

00:29:08.690 --> 00:29:10.850
So the increased
resistance actually

00:29:10.850 --> 00:29:14.240
allows you to draw more
air into the system.

00:29:14.240 --> 00:29:15.800
This turns out to
be very important.

00:29:15.800 --> 00:29:17.990
And it also wipes away
a common misconception,

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:20.810
which is if you're somebody
who has challenges breathing in

00:29:20.810 --> 00:29:23.510
through your nose, that somehow
you should avoid breathing in

00:29:23.510 --> 00:29:25.520
through your nose, actually,
quite the opposite is true.

00:29:25.520 --> 00:29:27.050
And we can go a
step further and say

00:29:27.050 --> 00:29:29.570
that if you have challenges
breathing in through your nose,

00:29:29.570 --> 00:29:31.445
chances are that's
because the increased

00:29:31.445 --> 00:29:33.320
resistance of breathing
in through your nose,

00:29:33.320 --> 00:29:35.870
provided it's not
completely occluded,

00:29:35.870 --> 00:29:39.320
is going to allow you to bring
more oxygen into your system.

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:41.540
This will turn out to
be useful later when

00:29:41.540 --> 00:29:43.550
we explore different
techniques, for instance,

00:29:43.550 --> 00:29:45.470
not just to calm down
quickly but to elevate

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:49.708
your energy quickly, to remove
a cramp during exercise,

00:29:49.708 --> 00:29:51.500
and a number of other
things that breathing

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:53.720
can be used for that
can be immensely

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:56.570
useful for mental and
physical challenges.

