00:31:10.803 --> 00:31:13.220
So now let's talk about the
chemical aspects of breathing.

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:15.170
And the two major players
in this discussion

00:31:15.170 --> 00:31:19.310
are oxygen, which all the cells
and tissues of your body need,

00:31:19.310 --> 00:31:22.730
and carbon dioxide,
which all the cells

00:31:22.730 --> 00:31:24.080
and tissues of your body need.

00:31:24.080 --> 00:31:26.780
In fact, carbon dioxide
plays critical roles

00:31:26.780 --> 00:31:28.730
in delivering oxygen
to your cells.

00:31:28.730 --> 00:31:30.470
And without carbon
dioxide, you're

00:31:30.470 --> 00:31:32.330
not going to get enough
oxygen to the cells

00:31:32.330 --> 00:31:34.820
and tissues of your body.

00:31:34.820 --> 00:31:38.030
That said, if carbon
dioxide levels are too high,

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:39.300
that is very problematic.

00:31:39.300 --> 00:31:44.240
In fact, one of the ways that
one can reliably induce panic

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:47.750
in anybody is to
have them breathe

00:31:47.750 --> 00:31:50.820
air that contains too
much carbon dioxide,

00:31:50.820 --> 00:31:53.810
so much so that for
people that lack

00:31:53.810 --> 00:31:55.227
a so-called
amygdala-- many of you

00:31:55.227 --> 00:31:56.727
have probably heard
of the amygdala.

00:31:56.727 --> 00:31:59.360
This is a brain area that's
associated with fear and threat

00:31:59.360 --> 00:31:59.990
detection.

00:31:59.990 --> 00:32:03.770
Even in people who
completely lack amygdalas

00:32:03.770 --> 00:32:06.080
on both sides of the brain
because they were removed

00:32:06.080 --> 00:32:07.880
because they had
epileptic seizures there

00:32:07.880 --> 00:32:10.850
and, therefore, those people are
completely unafraid of things

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:12.890
that they ought to be
afraid of like heights,

00:32:12.890 --> 00:32:16.250
poisonous snakes, any
number of different things

00:32:16.250 --> 00:32:18.950
dangerous to humans,
well, if those people

00:32:18.950 --> 00:32:21.080
breathe an excess amount
of carbon dioxide,

00:32:21.080 --> 00:32:23.160
they immediately
have a panic attack.

00:32:23.160 --> 00:32:25.370
What that tells us
is that, again, there

00:32:25.370 --> 00:32:27.980
are parallel mechanisms,
there's redundancy in the system

00:32:27.980 --> 00:32:30.890
to protect ourselves from
having too much carbon

00:32:30.890 --> 00:32:32.310
dioxide in our system.

00:32:32.310 --> 00:32:35.840
So we need enough carbon dioxide
and enough oxygen in our system

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:38.420
but not too much.

00:32:38.420 --> 00:32:42.790
The way that's accomplished is,
of course, we breathe in air.

00:32:42.790 --> 00:32:44.110
Our lungs inflate.

00:32:44.110 --> 00:32:46.930
And if you recall those
little alveoli of the lungs,

00:32:46.930 --> 00:32:50.950
those little sacs, oxygen can
actually move from the air

00:32:50.950 --> 00:32:54.550
into those little sacs and
then from those little sacs

00:32:54.550 --> 00:32:57.220
into the vasculature--
the vasculature are

00:32:57.220 --> 00:33:00.010
the capillaries, the veins,
and the arteries of the body--

00:33:00.010 --> 00:33:02.620
because the walls of
those little alveoli

00:33:02.620 --> 00:33:06.400
are exceedingly thin, and they
have tons of little capillaries

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:09.727
that go into them and
are all around them.

00:33:09.727 --> 00:33:10.810
So this is amazing, right?

00:33:10.810 --> 00:33:14.230
There's oxygen literally
passing from inside

00:33:14.230 --> 00:33:16.780
of these little
sacs in our lungs

00:33:16.780 --> 00:33:20.280
because we inhaled the
oxygen from the air

00:33:20.280 --> 00:33:22.890
into the bloodstream,
and then that oxygen

00:33:22.890 --> 00:33:26.580
gets bound up by
proteins in the blood,

00:33:26.580 --> 00:33:28.110
in particular hemoglobin.

00:33:28.110 --> 00:33:31.620
And hemoglobin then
delivers oxygen

00:33:31.620 --> 00:33:34.330
to the various cells
and tissues of the body.

00:33:34.330 --> 00:33:39.300
However, oxygen can't just
hop on hemoglobin and cruise

00:33:39.300 --> 00:33:41.880
along with hemoglobin until
it gets to, say, your brain

00:33:41.880 --> 00:33:42.870
and then hop off.

00:33:42.870 --> 00:33:44.920
It doesn't work that way.

00:33:44.920 --> 00:33:47.560
You require carbon
dioxide in order

00:33:47.560 --> 00:33:51.030
to liberate oxygen
from hemoglobin.

00:33:51.030 --> 00:33:53.370
Carbon dioxide has this
incredible property

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:56.470
of actually being able to
change the shape of hemoglobin.

00:33:56.470 --> 00:33:59.940
Hemoglobin is shaped as a
sort of a cage around oxygen

00:33:59.940 --> 00:34:00.850
molecules.

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:02.610
And when it's in
that cage shape,

00:34:02.610 --> 00:34:04.440
the oxygen can't be liberated.

00:34:04.440 --> 00:34:07.890
So you've got oxygen and
hemoglobin bound to one another

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:09.239
moving through your bloodstream.

00:34:09.239 --> 00:34:12.989
But if a tissue
needs oxygen, there

00:34:12.989 --> 00:34:17.219
needs to be carbon dioxide
present to open up that cage.

00:34:17.219 --> 00:34:18.929
And that's what
carbon dioxide does.

00:34:18.929 --> 00:34:21.389
It allows that cage
to change shape,

00:34:21.389 --> 00:34:23.520
and then the oxygen
can be liberated

00:34:23.520 --> 00:34:25.290
and then can be
delivered to the tissues,

00:34:25.290 --> 00:34:27.820
whether or not that's brain
tissue or muscle tissue,

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:29.560
so on and so forth.

00:34:29.560 --> 00:34:31.650
And so those are the
major chemical components

00:34:31.650 --> 00:34:32.310
of breathing.

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:34.433
There are a few
other aspects related

00:34:34.433 --> 00:34:36.100
to the chemical
components of breathing,

00:34:36.100 --> 00:34:40.230
such as the fact that carbon
dioxide is strongly related

00:34:40.230 --> 00:34:44.860
to how acidic or how basic
your body is in general.

00:34:44.860 --> 00:34:48.360
So for instance, if carbon
dioxide levels go way down,

00:34:48.360 --> 00:34:51.270
your blood pH goes way up.

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:53.190
That is, you become
more alkaline.

00:34:53.190 --> 00:34:56.250
Now, for many people, the word
pH and the whole concept of pH

00:34:56.250 --> 00:34:59.790
immediately starts to evoke
anxiety in and of itself.

00:34:59.790 --> 00:35:01.330
pH is actually very simple.

00:35:01.330 --> 00:35:05.790
You want the body basically
to be at a pH of about 7.4.

00:35:05.790 --> 00:35:07.290
There are some
regions of your body,

00:35:07.290 --> 00:35:10.110
in particular along the gut,
for which that number is

00:35:10.110 --> 00:35:13.260
importantly different in order
for digestion to work properly.

00:35:13.260 --> 00:35:16.230
You've all heard of the gut
microbiome, the little microbes

00:35:16.230 --> 00:35:17.940
that, provided you
have enough of them

00:35:17.940 --> 00:35:20.900
and they're diverse enough,
allow your brain and body

00:35:20.900 --> 00:35:23.400
to function optimally at the
level of immune system, hormone

00:35:23.400 --> 00:35:25.320
system, brain, et cetera.

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:28.740
Well, in the gut, you
want the pH sometimes

00:35:28.740 --> 00:35:30.570
be slightly more acidic.

00:35:30.570 --> 00:35:33.780
Because when it's more
acidic, the little microbiota

00:35:33.780 --> 00:35:37.167
flourish far more than
if it were more basic.

00:35:37.167 --> 00:35:39.000
But basically, you want
the rest of the body

00:35:39.000 --> 00:35:41.440
to be at about pH 7.4.

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:46.260
If carbon dioxide levels go to
low, the pH increases in a way

00:35:46.260 --> 00:35:48.150
that you might say,
oh, well, that's bad,

00:35:48.150 --> 00:35:50.682
but that actually
allows more oxygen

00:35:50.682 --> 00:35:52.890
to be available to the
tissues of your body, at least

00:35:52.890 --> 00:35:53.430
temporarily.

00:35:53.430 --> 00:35:55.497
We'll talk about this
a bit more later.

00:35:55.497 --> 00:35:57.330
If I'm losing any of
you, just hang in there

00:35:57.330 --> 00:35:59.010
because we're almost done
with this whole business

00:35:59.010 --> 00:36:01.052
of the mechanics and the
chemistry of breathing,

00:36:01.052 --> 00:36:03.510
and then we can get into the
tools and revisit some of this

00:36:03.510 --> 00:36:06.450
later to clean up any
misunderstandings that

00:36:06.450 --> 00:36:07.740
may have arisen.

00:36:07.740 --> 00:36:10.530
But as we're talking about
carbon dioxide over and over

00:36:10.530 --> 00:36:13.410
again and how key it is to have
carbon dioxide and the problems

00:36:13.410 --> 00:36:15.648
with it going too
high to low, you

00:36:15.648 --> 00:36:17.940
should probably be asking
yourself, what actually makes

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.040
carbon dioxide go too low?

00:36:20.040 --> 00:36:22.110
We know that we
breathe in oxygen,

00:36:22.110 --> 00:36:24.603
and then it can pass from
the lungs and the alveoli

00:36:24.603 --> 00:36:27.270
into the bloodstream and that we
need carbon dioxide to liberate

00:36:27.270 --> 00:36:29.370
oxygen from the
hemoglobin into the cells

00:36:29.370 --> 00:36:30.660
and tissues of the body.

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:33.357
And we know that
when we exhale--

00:36:33.357 --> 00:36:35.190
well, actually, I haven't
told you this yet.

00:36:35.190 --> 00:36:37.140
But you should know
that when you exhale,

00:36:37.140 --> 00:36:39.900
carbon dioxide is actually
taken from the bloodstream

00:36:39.900 --> 00:36:42.510
back into the
alveoli of the lungs.

00:36:42.510 --> 00:36:45.570
And then when you exhale, it's
expelled through your mouth

00:36:45.570 --> 00:36:47.290
or through your nose
out into the world.

00:36:47.290 --> 00:36:50.490
So the way I just described all
that-- inhale, bring in oxygen,

00:36:50.490 --> 00:36:54.180
exhale, expel carbon dioxide--

00:36:54.180 --> 00:36:56.270
pretty straightforward, right?

00:36:56.270 --> 00:36:57.320
Indeed, it is.

00:36:57.320 --> 00:36:59.570
And it also tells
you that were you

00:36:59.570 --> 00:37:03.470
to exhale a lot more or
a lot more vigorously,

00:37:03.470 --> 00:37:06.440
you would expel
more carbon dioxide.

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:08.510
And in fact, that's
exactly the way it works.

00:37:08.510 --> 00:37:11.330
When you hyperventilate,
of course, you

00:37:11.330 --> 00:37:14.300
are inhaling more than
usual, but you are also

00:37:14.300 --> 00:37:15.630
exhaling more than usual.

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:17.930
So you're, of course, bringing
in more air and oxygen

00:37:17.930 --> 00:37:18.650
to your body.

00:37:18.650 --> 00:37:21.950
But you're also removing more
carbon dioxide from your body

00:37:21.950 --> 00:37:23.720
than normal.

00:37:23.720 --> 00:37:26.840
Carbon dioxide, because of the
ways that it regulates brain

00:37:26.840 --> 00:37:29.810
state-- in fact, the way
in which it regulates

00:37:29.810 --> 00:37:32.810
the excitability, literally
the ability of your neurons

00:37:32.810 --> 00:37:37.160
to engage electrically or not--

00:37:37.160 --> 00:37:40.460
it can create states of
panic and anxiety, which

00:37:40.460 --> 00:37:43.010
is why when you
hyperventilate, you

00:37:43.010 --> 00:37:45.800
feel an increase
in anxiety, or when

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:48.230
you feel an increase in
anxiety, you hyperventilate.

00:37:48.230 --> 00:37:49.790
It's a reciprocal relationship.

00:37:49.790 --> 00:37:53.060
In fact, I don't want
anyone who has anxiety

00:37:53.060 --> 00:37:55.250
or who has panic
attacks to try this now.

00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:57.080
But for most people,
it's probably safe

00:37:57.080 --> 00:37:59.870
as long as you're not driving
or doing something mechanical

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:01.640
or operating machinery, that is.

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:05.540
Probably safe to do 25 or
30 deep inhales and exhales.

00:38:05.540 --> 00:38:08.270
And you'll notice that
by about breath 10,

00:38:08.270 --> 00:38:10.752
you'll start to feel
tingly, and you'll probably

00:38:10.752 --> 00:38:11.960
feel a little bit more alert.

00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:16.220
And, again, if you have anxiety
or panic attack tendencies,

00:38:16.220 --> 00:38:17.270
please don't do this.

00:38:17.270 --> 00:38:20.960
But you will feel an increase
in so-called autonomic arousal,

00:38:20.960 --> 00:38:23.420
an increase in the activity
of your overall sympathetic

00:38:23.420 --> 00:38:25.040
nervous system, which has
nothing to do with sympathy,

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:26.540
has everything to
do with alertness.

00:38:26.540 --> 00:38:29.308
You'll actually deploy
adrenaline from your adrenals.

00:38:29.308 --> 00:38:30.350
So I'll just do this now.

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:32.850
You can try this now, again,
provided you're in a safe place

00:38:32.850 --> 00:38:35.210
and you don't have anxiety
or panic attack tendencies.

00:38:35.210 --> 00:38:36.500
You would just breathe
in through your nose

00:38:36.500 --> 00:38:37.350
and out through your mouth.

00:38:37.350 --> 00:38:39.380
Remember, we're breathing
in more and more vigorously,

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:41.960
and we're exhaling more and
more vigorously than we normally

00:38:41.960 --> 00:38:42.210
would.

00:38:42.210 --> 00:38:43.377
It goes something like this.

00:38:43.377 --> 00:38:47.028
[INHALING, EXHALING]

00:38:48.510 --> 00:38:50.840
Now, by breath 8
or 9 or 10, you'll

00:38:50.840 --> 00:38:53.360
notice that your body
starts to heat up.

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:55.423
That's due to a
couple of things,

00:38:55.423 --> 00:38:57.590
mainly the release of
adrenaline from your adrenals.

00:38:57.590 --> 00:38:59.780
I'm already feeling a
little bit lightheaded.

00:38:59.780 --> 00:39:03.383
The lightheadedness is actually
because your vasculature,

00:39:03.383 --> 00:39:05.300
the capillaries and veins
and, to some extent,

00:39:05.300 --> 00:39:08.090
even the arteries of your body
and particularly in your brain,

00:39:08.090 --> 00:39:09.590
are actually starting
to constrict.

00:39:09.590 --> 00:39:11.930
So you're cutting off
blood flow to the brain.

00:39:11.930 --> 00:39:12.890
Why?

00:39:12.890 --> 00:39:17.180
Well, because carbon dioxide
actually is a vasodilator.

00:39:17.180 --> 00:39:21.735
Normally, it exists in your
body to keep capillaries, veins,

00:39:21.735 --> 00:39:24.110
and arteries dilated to allow
blood to pass through them.

00:39:24.110 --> 00:39:25.700
When you hyperventilate,
sure, you're

00:39:25.700 --> 00:39:27.170
bringing in a lot
of oxygen, which

00:39:27.170 --> 00:39:29.587
you think would make you more
alert, and, indeed, it does.

00:39:29.587 --> 00:39:32.690
But you are also expelling
a lot more carbon dioxide

00:39:32.690 --> 00:39:33.840
than you normally would.

00:39:33.840 --> 00:39:35.780
And that's causing
some vasoconstriction,

00:39:35.780 --> 00:39:36.620
and you're going
to start feeling

00:39:36.620 --> 00:39:38.930
tingly in the periphery,
in your fingers and toes

00:39:38.930 --> 00:39:40.613
perhaps or your legs.

00:39:40.613 --> 00:39:43.280
You will also notice that you're
feeling more alert in the brain

00:39:43.280 --> 00:39:45.950
but that you might start
to feel a bit of anxiety.

00:39:45.950 --> 00:39:48.290
So hyperventilation, yes,
brings in more oxygen,

00:39:48.290 --> 00:39:50.630
also removes more
carbon dioxide.

00:39:50.630 --> 00:39:53.000
The removal of
excess carbon dioxide

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:57.290
puts you into a state that's
called hypocapnic, hypoxia.

00:39:57.290 --> 00:40:01.970
Hypoxia is reduced levels of
oxygen relative to normal.

00:40:01.970 --> 00:40:05.300
Hypocapnia is reduced
levels of carbon dioxide

00:40:05.300 --> 00:40:06.590
relative to normal.

00:40:06.590 --> 00:40:09.140
And it is those reduced
levels of carbon dioxide

00:40:09.140 --> 00:40:11.720
that are largely responsible
for that elevation

00:40:11.720 --> 00:40:14.510
in energy and at the same time
a feeling of a bit of anxiety,

00:40:14.510 --> 00:40:17.120
the construction of
the microvasculature

00:40:17.120 --> 00:40:19.010
in the brain and
body, and therefore

00:40:19.010 --> 00:40:21.230
the feelings of
being kind of tingly

00:40:21.230 --> 00:40:23.420
and having kind of
an urgency to move.

00:40:23.420 --> 00:40:25.310
OK, so by now, it
should be clear

00:40:25.310 --> 00:40:28.587
that we need both oxygen
and carbon dioxide.

00:40:28.587 --> 00:40:30.170
And across the course
of this episode,

00:40:30.170 --> 00:40:33.050
I will explain how to adjust
those ratios of oxygen

00:40:33.050 --> 00:40:36.860
to carbon dioxide depending on
what your immediate needs are

00:40:36.860 --> 00:40:39.800
and what you plan to
do next, whether or not

00:40:39.800 --> 00:40:43.550
that's sleep or exercise
or mental work, et cetera.

00:40:43.550 --> 00:40:45.307
Before going any
further, however, there

00:40:45.307 --> 00:40:46.640
is something I want to touch on.

00:40:46.640 --> 00:40:49.520
Because even though not
everyone will experience this,

00:40:49.520 --> 00:40:51.590
I think enough
people experience it

00:40:51.590 --> 00:40:53.720
that it is of
interest, and now's

00:40:53.720 --> 00:40:57.200
the right time to touch into
what happens when you go up

00:40:57.200 --> 00:41:01.970
to a very high altitude, meaning
why it's hard to breathe when

00:41:01.970 --> 00:41:03.450
you get up to high altitudes.

00:41:03.450 --> 00:41:05.960
So if you're close
to sea level, you

00:41:05.960 --> 00:41:09.200
are getting out of the
optimal balance of oxygen

00:41:09.200 --> 00:41:10.670
in the air you breathe.

00:41:10.670 --> 00:41:12.650
As you ascend in
altitude-- so let's

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:15.950
say you go to 6,000
feet or 10,000

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:17.900
or maybe even 11,000
feet above sea level.

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:20.300
Or maybe you're one of
those rare individuals that

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:24.165
climbs Denali, or you
climb Mount Everest,

00:41:24.165 --> 00:41:26.540
and you get up there, and you
notice that most people are

00:41:26.540 --> 00:41:28.610
going to wear an oxygen mask.

00:41:28.610 --> 00:41:33.560
Why is it that you need an
oxygen mask at those very high

00:41:33.560 --> 00:41:36.320
altitudes or when people
do these very high altitude

00:41:36.320 --> 00:41:39.232
skydives that they need
oxygen way up high?

00:41:39.232 --> 00:41:41.690
Well, a lot of people will say,
oh, there's not much oxygen

00:41:41.690 --> 00:41:42.190
up there.

00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:43.640
The air is thinner.

00:41:43.640 --> 00:41:46.017
OK, well, perhaps a better
way to think about it

00:41:46.017 --> 00:41:47.600
is that, remember
when we were talking

00:41:47.600 --> 00:41:51.170
about the mechanical aspects
of breathing and the fact

00:41:51.170 --> 00:41:53.242
that the lungs don't
really move themselves,

00:41:53.242 --> 00:41:54.950
that they have the
muscles, the diaphragm

00:41:54.950 --> 00:41:56.660
and the intercostal
muscles to move them?

00:41:56.660 --> 00:42:00.710
Well, a lot of the reason why
your lungs can fill so readily

00:42:00.710 --> 00:42:05.570
with air is that when you don't
have much air in your lungs,

00:42:05.570 --> 00:42:10.610
there's very low air pressure in
your lungs relative to outside

00:42:10.610 --> 00:42:11.780
you.

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:14.510
So what we mean
then is if you were

00:42:14.510 --> 00:42:16.772
to open up your mouth
[INHALES] or your nose

00:42:16.772 --> 00:42:18.980
and breathe in, that is,
breathe in through your nose

00:42:18.980 --> 00:42:21.680
or mouth, what's
going to happen is

00:42:21.680 --> 00:42:24.308
air is going to move from
high pressure to low pressure.

00:42:24.308 --> 00:42:25.850
So it's very easy
to fill your lungs.

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:27.267
Even though you
need those muscles

00:42:27.267 --> 00:42:29.090
to move the various
things around that

00:42:29.090 --> 00:42:30.800
allow your lungs
to fill, the air

00:42:30.800 --> 00:42:33.060
is going to go from high
pressure to low pressure.

00:42:33.060 --> 00:42:35.450
So [INHALES] for those
of you listening,

00:42:35.450 --> 00:42:38.200
I just took a big
inhale through my nose.

00:42:38.200 --> 00:42:40.908
And then when you exhale, you're
basically taking the lungs

00:42:40.908 --> 00:42:42.450
from a state in
which the pressure is

00:42:42.450 --> 00:42:44.190
really high in the
lungs, high pressure,

00:42:44.190 --> 00:42:45.540
like a balloon that's full--

00:42:45.540 --> 00:42:49.080
and the pressure in your
lungs when your lungs are full

00:42:49.080 --> 00:42:50.700
is higher than the air outside.

00:42:50.700 --> 00:42:53.520
So it's pretty easy
[EXHALES] to expel that air

00:42:53.520 --> 00:42:54.870
through the nose or mouth.

00:42:54.870 --> 00:42:59.230
When you're at high altitudes,
the air pressure is lower.

00:42:59.230 --> 00:43:01.920
And so what happens is when
the air pressure is lower

00:43:01.920 --> 00:43:06.450
outside your body and your
lungs are not full of air,

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:08.490
you don't have that
really steep gradient

00:43:08.490 --> 00:43:09.930
of high pressure
outside the body

00:43:09.930 --> 00:43:11.950
to low pressure
inside your lungs.

00:43:11.950 --> 00:43:14.730
And so you actually have
to put a lot more effort

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:17.230
into breathing air
into your lungs.

00:43:17.230 --> 00:43:19.080
You have to really
exert a lot of force.

00:43:19.080 --> 00:43:21.427
You have to get the diaphragm,
those intercostal muscles

00:43:21.427 --> 00:43:22.260
working really hard.

00:43:22.260 --> 00:43:24.030
You might even find that
your shoulders are lifting

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:25.905
with each breath [INHALES]
because you really

00:43:25.905 --> 00:43:27.960
have to generate a lot of
force to get enough air

00:43:27.960 --> 00:43:30.002
and oxygen into your lungs.

00:43:30.002 --> 00:43:31.710
Now, an important
principle to understand

00:43:31.710 --> 00:43:34.350
is that in humans, and
in some other species,

00:43:34.350 --> 00:43:36.270
but really what we're
talking about now

00:43:36.270 --> 00:43:39.540
is humans, when you inhale,
that's an active process.

00:43:39.540 --> 00:43:41.490
You really need to
use those muscles

00:43:41.490 --> 00:43:43.140
of the intercostals
and the diaphragm

00:43:43.140 --> 00:43:44.850
in order to inflate the lungs.

00:43:44.850 --> 00:43:47.220
But the whole process
is made easier

00:43:47.220 --> 00:43:49.290
when air pressure
outside your body

00:43:49.290 --> 00:43:51.180
is higher than it
is in your lungs

00:43:51.180 --> 00:43:53.820
because then they're going
to fill up really readily.

00:43:53.820 --> 00:43:57.990
Exhaling, at least for
humans, is a passive thing.

00:43:57.990 --> 00:43:59.670
You just have to
relax the diaphragm

00:43:59.670 --> 00:44:02.460
and relax the intercostals and
let the rib cage kind of fall

00:44:02.460 --> 00:44:04.510
back to its original position.

00:44:04.510 --> 00:44:07.540
So inhaling is active,
and exhaling is passive.

00:44:07.540 --> 00:44:10.170
And so what happens is if
you're at a high altitude

00:44:10.170 --> 00:44:11.980
and the air pressure
is very low,

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:14.070
then you have to
put a lot of energy

00:44:14.070 --> 00:44:15.990
into breathing air
into your lungs

00:44:15.990 --> 00:44:19.080
to get an equivalent amount
of oxygen into your lungs

00:44:19.080 --> 00:44:20.470
and then into the bloodstream.

00:44:20.470 --> 00:44:23.160
So that's why when you arrive
at a high altitude location,

00:44:23.160 --> 00:44:25.493
for the first few days, you're
going to feel lightheaded

00:44:25.493 --> 00:44:26.230
maybe a headache.

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:27.570
You're also going to have
more buildup of carbon

00:44:27.570 --> 00:44:29.080
dioxide in your system.

00:44:29.080 --> 00:44:31.980
And so the whole balance of
oxygen and carbon dioxide

00:44:31.980 --> 00:44:33.840
is going to be disrupted.

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:36.480
I mention all that
because, yes, indeed, there

00:44:36.480 --> 00:44:38.250
are some changes in
the atmospheric gases

00:44:38.250 --> 00:44:40.912
at high altitudes,
and that can impact

00:44:40.912 --> 00:44:42.870
how much oxygen you can
bring into your system,

00:44:42.870 --> 00:44:43.740
into your tissues.

00:44:43.740 --> 00:44:46.950
But I've heard many explanations
of why it's hard to breathe

00:44:46.950 --> 00:44:49.180
or why you feel
lousy at altitude.

00:44:49.180 --> 00:44:50.742
Well, you just
discovered one reason,

00:44:50.742 --> 00:44:52.950
which is that you don't have
that steep high pressure

00:44:52.950 --> 00:44:55.158
to low pressure gradient
from the outside of the body

00:44:55.158 --> 00:44:57.160
into the inside of the body.

00:44:57.160 --> 00:44:58.262
The converse is also true.

00:44:58.262 --> 00:44:59.970
If you've been at
altitude for a few days

00:44:59.970 --> 00:45:02.520
and you've had the opportunity
to adjust-- a lot of athletes,

00:45:02.520 --> 00:45:04.680
for instance, will
go train at altitude.

00:45:04.680 --> 00:45:06.600
It's hard for them in
the first days or weeks,

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:08.850
and then they get really
good at training at altitude.

00:45:08.850 --> 00:45:10.770
There are a number of
different adaptations

00:45:10.770 --> 00:45:13.230
that occur in terms of
the amount of oxygen

00:45:13.230 --> 00:45:15.660
that can be carried in
the blood by hemoglobin

00:45:15.660 --> 00:45:18.090
and the interactions between
carbon dioxide and hemoglobin

00:45:18.090 --> 00:45:20.130
and oxygen that
allow more oxygen

00:45:20.130 --> 00:45:24.130
to be delivered to the tissues,
such that, at altitude,

00:45:24.130 --> 00:45:25.810
you can function just normally.

00:45:25.810 --> 00:45:28.920
But if you then move very
quickly from altitude-- say,

00:45:28.920 --> 00:45:31.050
you've been training at
8,000 feet or 10,000 feet.

00:45:31.050 --> 00:45:32.970
You've been hiking up at that
high level, and you've adapted,

00:45:32.970 --> 00:45:34.770
and you come down to sea level.

00:45:34.770 --> 00:45:37.020
Well, for about
two to five days,

00:45:37.020 --> 00:45:39.390
you're going to feel
like an absolute beast.

00:45:39.390 --> 00:45:42.210
You're going to be able
to essentially deliver

00:45:42.210 --> 00:45:45.270
far more oxygen to your
muscles per breath.

00:45:45.270 --> 00:45:47.670
In part, that is
because of the way

00:45:47.670 --> 00:45:50.070
that the hemoglobin and the
oxygen that it's carrying

00:45:50.070 --> 00:45:52.170
has been altered when you
were at high altitude.

00:45:52.170 --> 00:45:55.560
But it's also because when you
were at that high altitude,

00:45:55.560 --> 00:45:57.810
those intercostal muscles
and those diaphragms

00:45:57.810 --> 00:46:02.280
got trained up quite a bit and
allowed you to generate more

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:03.518
air volume for every breath.

00:46:03.518 --> 00:46:05.310
In other words, those
muscles got stronger,

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:07.920
and you got more efficient
at driving the phrenic nerve

00:46:07.920 --> 00:46:10.745
consciously to [INHALES] really
breathe in a lot of oxygen

00:46:10.745 --> 00:46:12.870
so you don't feel lightheaded,
headache, et cetera.

00:46:12.870 --> 00:46:14.495
OK, so that's a little
bit of an aside.

00:46:14.495 --> 00:46:16.620
But it's an important aside,
I believe, because, A,

00:46:16.620 --> 00:46:19.110
it answers a question
a lot of people ask

00:46:19.110 --> 00:46:21.360
and they a lot of people
wonder about and, B,

00:46:21.360 --> 00:46:24.270
because it incorporates both the
mechanical aspects of breathing

00:46:24.270 --> 00:46:26.040
and the chemical
aspects of breathing.

00:46:26.040 --> 00:46:28.812
I realize it's a little bit
of a unusual circumstance.

00:46:28.812 --> 00:46:30.270
But now if anyone
asks you why it's

00:46:30.270 --> 00:46:31.500
hard to breathe at
altitude, you know

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:33.417
it has to do with this
lack of a high pressure

00:46:33.417 --> 00:46:35.820
to low pressure
gradient across the body

00:46:35.820 --> 00:46:37.530
and with the
atmosphere outside you.

00:46:37.530 --> 00:46:39.150
It's also an opportunity
for me to say

00:46:39.150 --> 00:46:40.630
that if you do find
yourself at altitude

00:46:40.630 --> 00:46:42.922
and you have a headache or
you're feeling like you just

00:46:42.922 --> 00:46:45.090
can't catch your breath,
spending some time really

00:46:45.090 --> 00:46:47.940
consciously trying to draw
in larger breaths of air,

00:46:47.940 --> 00:46:49.710
as much as that
might seem fatiguing

00:46:49.710 --> 00:46:51.570
and you'll be short of
breath, it will allow

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:52.590
you to adapt more quickly.

00:46:52.590 --> 00:46:53.730
And a little bit
later in the episode,

00:46:53.730 --> 00:46:55.355
we'll touch on a few
methods, including

00:46:55.355 --> 00:46:57.660
deliberate hyperventilation
combined with some breath

00:46:57.660 --> 00:46:59.368
holds, that can allow
you to deliver more

00:46:59.368 --> 00:47:01.433
oxygen to the cells
immediately upon arriving

00:47:01.433 --> 00:47:02.850
at altitude so you
don't get quite

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:05.250
as much headache,
disorientation, and so on.

00:47:05.250 --> 00:47:08.250
So leaving breathing
at altitude aside let's

00:47:08.250 --> 00:47:12.060
all come back down to the
same conceptual level.

00:47:12.060 --> 00:47:14.110
We can ask ourselves,
for instance,

00:47:14.110 --> 00:47:17.070
what is healthy breathing, and
what is unhealthy breathing?

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:19.110
And the first place
we want to tackle this

00:47:19.110 --> 00:47:21.390
is within the context of sleep.

00:47:21.390 --> 00:47:24.210
So when we go to sleep at
night, we continue to breathe.

00:47:24.210 --> 00:47:25.230
That's no surprise.

00:47:25.230 --> 00:47:27.330
If we didn't, we would
die during sleep.

00:47:27.330 --> 00:47:29.370
However, there is
a large fraction

00:47:29.370 --> 00:47:32.770
of the population that
underbreathes during sleep.

00:47:32.770 --> 00:47:36.410
They're not taking deep enough
or frequent enough breaths.

00:47:36.410 --> 00:47:38.570
And therefore, they
are experiencing

00:47:38.570 --> 00:47:39.920
what's called sleep apnea.

00:47:39.920 --> 00:47:43.190
They are becoming
hypoxic, hypo-oxic.

00:47:43.190 --> 00:47:45.980
There's less oxygen being
brought into their system

00:47:45.980 --> 00:47:46.970
than is necessary.

00:47:46.970 --> 00:47:49.130
People that are
carrying excess weight,

00:47:49.130 --> 00:47:51.920
either fat weight or
muscle weight or both,

00:47:51.920 --> 00:47:55.280
are more prone to
nighttime sleep apnea.

00:47:55.280 --> 00:47:56.990
However, there are
a lot of people

00:47:56.990 --> 00:47:59.990
who are not overweight who
also experience sleep apnea.

00:47:59.990 --> 00:48:02.120
How do you know if you're
experiencing sleep apnea?

00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:04.550
Well, first of all,
excessive daytime sleepiness

00:48:04.550 --> 00:48:06.710
and excessive daytime
anxiety combined

00:48:06.710 --> 00:48:08.720
with daytime
sleepiness is one sign

00:48:08.720 --> 00:48:11.000
that you might be
suffering from sleep apnea.

00:48:11.000 --> 00:48:13.400
The other thing is if
you happen to snore,

00:48:13.400 --> 00:48:16.820
it's very likely that you
are experiencing sleep apnea.

00:48:16.820 --> 00:48:19.820
And I should mention that sleep
apnea is a very serious health

00:48:19.820 --> 00:48:20.390
concern.

00:48:20.390 --> 00:48:22.610
It greatly increases
the probability

00:48:22.610 --> 00:48:26.180
of a cardiovascular event,
heart attack, stroke.

00:48:26.180 --> 00:48:29.300
It is a precursor or
sometimes the direct cause

00:48:29.300 --> 00:48:31.790
of sexual dysfunction
in males and females.

00:48:31.790 --> 00:48:34.100
Cognitive dysfunction
during the daytime.

00:48:34.100 --> 00:48:37.520
It can exacerbate the
effects of dementia,

00:48:37.520 --> 00:48:40.370
whether or not it's age-related
dementia of the normal sort

00:48:40.370 --> 00:48:42.230
or Alzheimer's type
dementia, which

00:48:42.230 --> 00:48:44.580
is an acceleration of
age-related cognitive decline.

00:48:44.580 --> 00:48:46.580
If you're somebody who
has had a traumatic brain

00:48:46.580 --> 00:48:49.320
injury, if you're
experiencing a lot of stress,

00:48:49.320 --> 00:48:51.290
sleep apnea is going
to greatly disrupt

00:48:51.290 --> 00:48:53.540
the amount of oxygen brought
in to your brain and body

00:48:53.540 --> 00:48:55.280
during sleep and
is going to lead

00:48:55.280 --> 00:48:58.163
to a number of nighttime
and daytime issues.

00:48:58.163 --> 00:49:00.330
So it's something that
really needs to be addressed.

00:49:00.330 --> 00:49:02.060
And we'll get into
this a bit more later.

00:49:02.060 --> 00:49:03.860
But since I raised
it as a problem,

00:49:03.860 --> 00:49:05.340
I do want to raise the solution.

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:08.087
One of the major
treatments for sleep apnea

00:49:08.087 --> 00:49:09.920
is that people will get
a CPAP device, which

00:49:09.920 --> 00:49:13.617
is this face mask and a machine
that they'll sleep with.

00:49:13.617 --> 00:49:15.200
And while those can
be very effective,

00:49:15.200 --> 00:49:16.880
not everyone needs a CPAP.

00:49:16.880 --> 00:49:19.070
One of the more common
methods nowadays

00:49:19.070 --> 00:49:20.880
that's being used to
treat sleep apnea,

00:49:20.880 --> 00:49:23.090
which is purely behavioral,
an intervention,

00:49:23.090 --> 00:49:25.610
and is essentially zero
cost, is that people

00:49:25.610 --> 00:49:27.410
are starting to
shift deliberately

00:49:27.410 --> 00:49:30.110
to nasal breathing
during sleep because

00:49:30.110 --> 00:49:32.570
of the additional resistance
of nasal breathing

00:49:32.570 --> 00:49:34.070
and because of the
fact that there's

00:49:34.070 --> 00:49:37.700
far less tendency if
any, excuse me, to snore

00:49:37.700 --> 00:49:39.080
when nasal breathing.

00:49:39.080 --> 00:49:42.800
Taping the mouth shut using
medical tape prior to sleep--

00:49:42.800 --> 00:49:43.310
excuse me.

00:49:43.310 --> 00:49:46.580
Putting medical tape on the
mouth prior to going to sleep

00:49:46.580 --> 00:49:49.430
and then sleeping all night
with medical tape on the mouth

00:49:49.430 --> 00:49:52.220
is one way that people can learn
to nasal breathe during sleep

00:49:52.220 --> 00:49:55.910
and can greatly offset a
lot of sleep apnea, snoring,

00:49:55.910 --> 00:49:57.800
and sleep-related issues.

00:49:57.800 --> 00:49:59.750
A number of people
don't want to or don't

00:49:59.750 --> 00:50:02.600
feel safe putting medical tape
on their mouth prior to sleep.

00:50:02.600 --> 00:50:04.220
For some reason, they think
they're going to suffocate.

00:50:04.220 --> 00:50:05.595
But, of course,
you would wake up

00:50:05.595 --> 00:50:08.370
if you start to run out
of air at any moment.

00:50:08.370 --> 00:50:09.870
So that's not so much a concern.

00:50:09.870 --> 00:50:12.050
But what they'll do
is they will start

00:50:12.050 --> 00:50:15.630
to use pure nasal breathing
during any type of exercise

00:50:15.630 --> 00:50:18.200
or even just for some period
of time walking during the day

00:50:18.200 --> 00:50:19.310
or while working.

00:50:19.310 --> 00:50:22.280
And, again, later, we'll get
into the enormous benefits

00:50:22.280 --> 00:50:26.180
of shifting to pure nasal
breathing when not exercising

00:50:26.180 --> 00:50:29.297
hard, meaning at a rate
that you could normally

00:50:29.297 --> 00:50:31.880
hold a conversation-- although
if you're pure nasal breathing,

00:50:31.880 --> 00:50:33.547
you won't be holding
that conversation--

00:50:33.547 --> 00:50:38.150
or when simply doing work
or any number of things

00:50:38.150 --> 00:50:39.830
that are of low intensity.

00:50:39.830 --> 00:50:42.500
You can train your system to
become a better nasal breather

00:50:42.500 --> 00:50:44.390
during the daytime
through these deliberate

00:50:44.390 --> 00:50:46.310
actions of taping the mouth
shut or just being conscious

00:50:46.310 --> 00:50:47.435
of keeping your mouth shut.

00:50:47.435 --> 00:50:50.120
And that, in addition to having
a number of positive health

00:50:50.120 --> 00:50:52.370
and aesthetic effects
during the daytime,

00:50:52.370 --> 00:50:55.880
is known to also transfer to
nighttime breathing patterns

00:50:55.880 --> 00:50:58.820
and allow people to become
nasal breathers as opposed

00:50:58.820 --> 00:51:01.610
to mouth breathers during
sleep and to snore less

00:51:01.610 --> 00:51:03.050
and to have less sleep apnea.

00:51:03.050 --> 00:51:04.700
Again, if you have
severe sleep apnea,

00:51:04.700 --> 00:51:07.790
you probably do need
to check out a CPAP.

00:51:07.790 --> 00:51:09.270
You should talk
to your physician.

00:51:09.270 --> 00:51:12.080
But for people who have
minor sleep apnea or sleep

00:51:12.080 --> 00:51:15.200
apnea that's starting
to take hold,

00:51:15.200 --> 00:51:18.230
these other methods of shifting
to becoming a nasal breather

00:51:18.230 --> 00:51:20.930
are going to be far more
beneficial and far more cost

00:51:20.930 --> 00:51:24.230
effective than going all the way
to the CPAP, which, by the way,

00:51:24.230 --> 00:51:26.630
doesn't really teach you
how to breathe properly

00:51:26.630 --> 00:51:30.615
as much as it does adjust the
airflow going into your system.

00:51:30.615 --> 00:51:32.990
That's an important point,
that when you shift from mouth

00:51:32.990 --> 00:51:34.615
to nasal breathing
during sleep, you're

00:51:34.615 --> 00:51:36.680
actually learning and
training your system

00:51:36.680 --> 00:51:37.760
to breathe properly.

00:51:37.760 --> 00:51:39.410
And when I say
learning and training

00:51:39.410 --> 00:51:41.820
your system to breathe
properly, what do I mean?

00:51:41.820 --> 00:51:44.732
Let's put some scientific
and mechanistic meat on that.

00:51:44.732 --> 00:51:46.940
We already talked about the
phrenic nerve, this nerve

00:51:46.940 --> 00:51:49.693
that innervates the diaphragm
and that allows for the lungs

00:51:49.693 --> 00:51:51.860
to fill up because of the
movement of the diaphragm.

00:51:51.860 --> 00:51:53.870
What we didn't talk
about, however,

00:51:53.870 --> 00:51:55.370
were the brain
centers that actually

00:51:55.370 --> 00:51:57.943
control the phrenic nerve
and control breathing.

00:51:57.943 --> 00:52:00.110
Knowing about these two
brain areas and what they do

00:52:00.110 --> 00:52:03.440
is extremely important, not just
for understanding the content

00:52:03.440 --> 00:52:06.230
of this episode but for
understanding all of the tools

00:52:06.230 --> 00:52:08.870
that we'll discuss and, indeed,
your general health as it

00:52:08.870 --> 00:52:10.260
relates to respiration.

00:52:10.260 --> 00:52:12.380
So there are basically
two areas of the brain

00:52:12.380 --> 00:52:13.430
that control breathing.

00:52:13.430 --> 00:52:15.710
The first is called the
pre-Botzinger complex.

00:52:15.710 --> 00:52:17.090
You don't have to worry
about the name so much.

00:52:17.090 --> 00:52:19.173
Just know that it was named
after a bottle of wine

00:52:19.173 --> 00:52:22.460
and that it was discovered by
the great Jack Feldman, who's

00:52:22.460 --> 00:52:25.460
a professor of neuroscience at
the University of California,

00:52:25.460 --> 00:52:26.112
Los Angeles.

00:52:26.112 --> 00:52:28.070
This is one of the most
fundamental discoveries

00:52:28.070 --> 00:52:30.260
in all of neuroscience
in the last hundred years

00:52:30.260 --> 00:52:33.350
or more because this brain area
that Jack and his colleagues

00:52:33.350 --> 00:52:37.270
discovered controls all aspects
of breathing that are rhythmic,

00:52:37.270 --> 00:52:40.000
that is, when inhales
follow exhales

00:52:40.000 --> 00:52:42.130
follow inhales follow exhales.

00:52:42.130 --> 00:52:43.870
That's all controlled
by a small set

00:52:43.870 --> 00:52:46.600
of neurons in this
brainstem area,

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:49.150
so around the
region of the neck,

00:52:49.150 --> 00:52:50.950
called the
pre-Botzinger complex.

00:52:50.950 --> 00:52:53.440
And we really owe
a debt of gratitude

00:52:53.440 --> 00:52:55.960
to Jack and his colleagues
for discovering that area

00:52:55.960 --> 00:52:58.703
because it's involved in
everything from breathing when

00:52:58.703 --> 00:53:00.370
we're asleep to
breathing when we're not

00:53:00.370 --> 00:53:01.720
thinking about our breathing.

00:53:01.720 --> 00:53:03.640
It may have a role--

00:53:03.640 --> 00:53:06.410
that is, when its
function is disrupted,

00:53:06.410 --> 00:53:09.190
it may cause things like
sudden infant death syndrome.

00:53:09.190 --> 00:53:11.680
Believe it or not,
it can explain

00:53:11.680 --> 00:53:15.910
in large part many of the deaths
related to the opioid crisis

00:53:15.910 --> 00:53:18.910
because exogenous opioids
like fentanyl and other sorts

00:53:18.910 --> 00:53:21.640
of drugs, which are
opioids obviously,

00:53:21.640 --> 00:53:27.110
bind to opioid receptors on
that structure and shut it down.

00:53:27.110 --> 00:53:29.290
Now, keep in mind these
neurons are designed

00:53:29.290 --> 00:53:32.800
to be incredibly robust and
are designed to fire inhale,

00:53:32.800 --> 00:53:36.010
exhale, inhale, exhale no
matter if we're awake or aware,

00:53:36.010 --> 00:53:40.450
unaware or asleep
to keep us alive.

00:53:40.450 --> 00:53:42.820
Exogenous opioids like
fentanyl and drugs

00:53:42.820 --> 00:53:46.090
that are similar to that
can shut down that structure

00:53:46.090 --> 00:53:48.620
because it's rich with
these opioid receptors.

00:53:48.620 --> 00:53:50.182
So it binds to that,
and it shuts off

00:53:50.182 --> 00:53:51.640
the pre-Botzinger
complex, which is

00:53:51.640 --> 00:53:53.170
the major cause
of death of people

00:53:53.170 --> 00:53:54.618
who die from opioid overdoses.

00:53:54.618 --> 00:53:56.410
I think a lot of people
don't realize that.

00:53:56.410 --> 00:53:58.360
They think, oh, the opioids
must shut off the brain

00:53:58.360 --> 00:53:59.440
or shut down the heart.

00:53:59.440 --> 00:54:01.270
No, it shuts down breathing.

00:54:01.270 --> 00:54:03.430
So Jack's discovery
no doubt will

00:54:03.430 --> 00:54:06.730
lead to some important things
as it relates to addiction,

00:54:06.730 --> 00:54:10.420
and hopefully I think we frankly
can expect that it's also

00:54:10.420 --> 00:54:14.920
going to eventually lead to
ways to prevent death in people

00:54:14.920 --> 00:54:16.630
using opioids or
other types of drugs,

00:54:16.630 --> 00:54:18.670
maybe by blocking
opioid receptors

00:54:18.670 --> 00:54:20.650
in pre-Botzinger
complex using things

00:54:20.650 --> 00:54:22.240
like naltrexone, et cetera.

00:54:22.240 --> 00:54:24.010
In any event,
pre-Botzinger complex

00:54:24.010 --> 00:54:26.800
is controlling inhale, exhale,
inhale, exhale patterns

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:27.430
of breathing.

00:54:27.430 --> 00:54:30.250
The other brain center
controlling breathing, again,

00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:31.375
through the phrenic nerve--

00:54:31.375 --> 00:54:33.667
it all converges and goes
out through the phrenic nerve

00:54:33.667 --> 00:54:35.080
in these intercostal muscles--

00:54:35.080 --> 00:54:37.000
is the so-called
parafacial nucleus.

00:54:37.000 --> 00:54:39.190
And the parafacial nucleus
is involved in patterns

00:54:39.190 --> 00:54:44.720
of breathing where there is not
an inhale followed by exhale,

00:54:44.720 --> 00:54:47.060
inhale followed by exhale--
that is, it's not rhythmic,

00:54:47.060 --> 00:54:48.150
one than the other--

00:54:48.150 --> 00:54:50.660
but, rather, where there
is a doubling up of inhales

00:54:50.660 --> 00:54:54.080
or a doubling up of exhales or
a deliberate pause in breathing,

00:54:54.080 --> 00:54:58.100
so inhale, pause, exhale, pause,
inhale, pause, exhale, pause,

00:54:58.100 --> 00:54:59.003
this sort of thing.

00:54:59.003 --> 00:55:00.920
A little bit later, we'll
talk about a pattern

00:55:00.920 --> 00:55:02.587
of breathing called
box breathing, which

00:55:02.587 --> 00:55:06.390
has very specific and
useful applications,

00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:09.170
in particular for
adjusting anxiety.

00:55:09.170 --> 00:55:12.590
And in that case, it involves
going from rhythmic breathing

00:55:12.590 --> 00:55:15.800
of inhale, inhale,
inhale, exhale, that is,

00:55:15.800 --> 00:55:18.560
relying on the pre-Botzinger
complex neurons,

00:55:18.560 --> 00:55:22.487
to reliance on the parafacial
nucleus neurons and box

00:55:22.487 --> 00:55:24.320
breathing, just to give
away what's probably

00:55:24.320 --> 00:55:28.370
already obvious, as you inhale,
hold, exhale, hold, and repeat.

00:55:28.370 --> 00:55:30.380
And that pattern of
breathing, even though it's

00:55:30.380 --> 00:55:33.380
rhythmic in nature because
inhales precede exhales precede

00:55:33.380 --> 00:55:35.990
inhales and so on, there's
a deliberate breath hold

00:55:35.990 --> 00:55:36.740
inserted there.

00:55:36.740 --> 00:55:38.690
So anytime we're taking
conscious control

00:55:38.690 --> 00:55:42.860
of our breathing, the parafacial
nucleus is getting involved.

00:55:42.860 --> 00:55:46.160
Now, you don't have to assume
that the parafacial nucleus is

00:55:46.160 --> 00:55:48.182
the only way in which we
take conscious control

00:55:48.182 --> 00:55:48.890
of our breathing.

00:55:48.890 --> 00:55:51.440
We can also take control of
the pre-Botzinger complex.

00:55:51.440 --> 00:55:52.560
You can do that right now.

00:55:52.560 --> 00:55:53.690
So for instance,
you are breathing

00:55:53.690 --> 00:55:55.850
in some specific pattern
now that, unless you're

00:55:55.850 --> 00:55:59.042
speaking or eating, no doubt
is going to involve inhales

00:55:59.042 --> 00:55:59.875
followed by exhales.

00:55:59.875 --> 00:56:01.790
But you could, for
instance, decide

00:56:01.790 --> 00:56:05.780
that, yes, inhales are active
and exhales are passive.

00:56:05.780 --> 00:56:08.640
But now you're going to make
the exhales active as well.

00:56:08.640 --> 00:56:11.420
So rather than just inhale and
then let your lungs deflate,

00:56:11.420 --> 00:56:14.090
you could inhale [INHALES]
and then force the air out.

00:56:14.090 --> 00:56:17.030
[EXHALES] That's going to
represent a conscious taking

00:56:17.030 --> 00:56:19.340
over of control of the
pre-Botzinger complex.

00:56:19.340 --> 00:56:23.420
And so the reason I'm giving
this mechanistic detail is, A,

00:56:23.420 --> 00:56:26.270
it's super important if you
want to understand all the tools

00:56:26.270 --> 00:56:27.180
related to breathing.

00:56:27.180 --> 00:56:29.220
B, it's actually a
pretty simple system.

00:56:29.220 --> 00:56:31.310
Even though the areas
have fancy names

00:56:31.310 --> 00:56:33.050
like pre-Botzinger
or parafacial,

00:56:33.050 --> 00:56:34.250
it's pretty straightforward.

00:56:34.250 --> 00:56:35.930
You have one area that
controls rhythmic breathing--

00:56:35.930 --> 00:56:38.555
inhale follows exhales-- and the
other area which gets involved

00:56:38.555 --> 00:56:40.430
in breathing any time
you start doubling up

00:56:40.430 --> 00:56:41.410
on inhales or exhales.

00:56:41.410 --> 00:56:43.160
In fact, the parafacial
nucleus is the one

00:56:43.160 --> 00:56:45.590
that you're relying on
while you speak in order

00:56:45.590 --> 00:56:47.702
to make sure that you
still get enough oxygen.

00:56:47.702 --> 00:56:49.160
It's also the one
that you will use

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:51.680
if you incorporate the
physiological sigh or box

00:56:51.680 --> 00:56:52.430
breathing.

00:56:52.430 --> 00:56:54.650
And, frankly, most
of the time, you're

00:56:54.650 --> 00:56:57.650
using both of these circuits
or these brain systems,

00:56:57.650 --> 00:57:00.390
parafacial and
pre-Botzinger, in parallel.

00:57:00.390 --> 00:57:03.055
Again, biology loves
parallel systems,

00:57:03.055 --> 00:57:04.430
especially for
things that are so

00:57:04.430 --> 00:57:07.340
critical that if we didn't
do them, we would die,

00:57:07.340 --> 00:57:08.120
like breathing.

00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:09.710
And so it makes sense that
we have two different brain

00:57:09.710 --> 00:57:11.220
structures that control this.

00:57:11.220 --> 00:57:13.940
So now you have an understanding
of the mechanical control

00:57:13.940 --> 00:57:17.060
of breathing, that is,
the different parts

00:57:17.060 --> 00:57:19.398
within the parts list that
are involved in breathing,

00:57:19.398 --> 00:57:21.690
everything from nose to mouth
to alveoli, the lungs, et

00:57:21.690 --> 00:57:24.110
cetera, and the muscles
involved in moving the lungs.

00:57:24.110 --> 00:57:28.190
You understand, I like to think,
a bit about bringing oxygen

00:57:28.190 --> 00:57:31.430
in and removing carbon dioxide
but not so much carbon dioxide

00:57:31.430 --> 00:57:34.190
that you can't actually use
the oxygen that you have.

00:57:34.190 --> 00:57:37.130
And you know about
two brain centers,

00:57:37.130 --> 00:57:39.440
one controlling rhythmic
breathing and one that

00:57:39.440 --> 00:57:42.432
controls nonrhythmic breathing.

00:57:42.432 --> 00:57:44.890
I want to repeat something that
I said a little bit earlier

00:57:44.890 --> 00:57:47.440
as well, which is that
breathing is incredible

00:57:47.440 --> 00:57:50.140
because it represents
the interface

00:57:50.140 --> 00:57:53.050
between conscious and
subconscious control

00:57:53.050 --> 00:57:56.810
over your not just body,
not just your lungs,

00:57:56.810 --> 00:58:00.440
but that how you breathe
influences your brain state.

00:58:00.440 --> 00:58:02.410
So by using your
brain consciously

00:58:02.410 --> 00:58:05.350
to control your breathing,
you are using your brain

00:58:05.350 --> 00:58:06.588
to control your brain.

00:58:06.588 --> 00:58:08.380
The best way I've ever
heard this described

00:58:08.380 --> 00:58:10.492
was from a beautiful,
I should say now

00:58:10.492 --> 00:58:12.700
classic paper in The Journal
of Physiology, published

00:58:12.700 --> 00:58:15.340
in 1988 from
Balestrino and Somjen,

00:58:15.340 --> 00:58:20.350
where the final line of
their summary intro states,

00:58:20.350 --> 00:58:22.420
"The brain, by
regulating breathing,

00:58:22.420 --> 00:58:24.520
controls its own excitability."

00:58:24.520 --> 00:58:26.290
And just to remind
those of you that

00:58:26.290 --> 00:58:27.940
don't remember what
excitability is,

00:58:27.940 --> 00:58:32.710
excitability is the threshold
at which a given neuron, nerve

00:58:32.710 --> 00:58:34.310
cell can be active or not.

00:58:34.310 --> 00:58:38.350
So when we breathe a certain
way, the neurons of our brain

00:58:38.350 --> 00:58:40.240
are more likely to get engaged.

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:41.770
They're more likely
to be active.

00:58:41.770 --> 00:58:43.900
And when we breathe
in other ways,

00:58:43.900 --> 00:58:47.080
our brain becomes
harder to activate.

00:58:47.080 --> 00:58:49.150
Its excitability is reduced.

00:58:49.150 --> 00:58:51.310
Now, you might think
excitability is a great thing.

00:58:51.310 --> 00:58:53.102
You always want your
brain to be excitable.

00:58:53.102 --> 00:58:54.560
But that's actually
not the case.

00:58:54.560 --> 00:58:57.070
And, in fact, that
very statement

00:58:57.070 --> 00:58:59.770
that Balestrino
and Somjen made led

00:58:59.770 --> 00:59:01.480
to a number of
other investigations

00:59:01.480 --> 00:59:03.640
that were really
important in defining

00:59:03.640 --> 00:59:06.730
how if people
overbreathe, that is,

00:59:06.730 --> 00:59:12.370
if they hyperventilate, at
rest, they expel, that is, they

00:59:12.370 --> 00:59:15.160
exhale too much
carbon dioxide, what

00:59:15.160 --> 00:59:18.353
that classic paper by
Balestrino and Somjen led to

00:59:18.353 --> 00:59:20.020
was a number of
different investigations

00:59:20.020 --> 00:59:24.220
in humans looking at how
different patterns of breathing

00:59:24.220 --> 00:59:27.370
impact the overall state of
the brain and the ability

00:59:27.370 --> 00:59:29.440
of the brain to
respond to certain what

00:59:29.440 --> 00:59:31.510
are called sensory stimuli.

00:59:31.510 --> 00:59:33.940
Keep in mind that your
brain is always active.

00:59:33.940 --> 00:59:37.130
The neurons are firing at low
level, low level, low level.

00:59:37.130 --> 00:59:40.220
But when you see something
or hear something,

00:59:40.220 --> 00:59:41.840
or you want to
focus on something,

00:59:41.840 --> 00:59:44.170
or you want to exercise or
really listen to something

00:59:44.170 --> 00:59:46.960
or learn, certain
circuits in your brain

00:59:46.960 --> 00:59:49.765
need to be more active
than everything else.

00:59:49.765 --> 00:59:51.640
That is, there needs to
be really high what's

00:59:51.640 --> 00:59:52.600
called signal to noise.

00:59:52.600 --> 00:59:55.100
There's always a lot of noise
and chatter in the background,

00:59:55.100 --> 00:59:57.250
just like the chatter
at a cocktail party

00:59:57.250 --> 00:59:58.870
or at a stadium event.

00:59:58.870 --> 01:00:02.120
In order to really pay
attention, focus, learn,

01:00:02.120 --> 01:00:04.120
all the incredible things
that the brain can do,

01:00:04.120 --> 01:00:07.800
you need that signal
to get above the noise.

01:00:07.800 --> 01:00:11.040
There's a beautiful
paper that asks,

01:00:11.040 --> 01:00:13.860
how does the pattern of
breathing, in particular, how

01:00:13.860 --> 01:00:18.210
does overbreathing, change
the patterns of activity

01:00:18.210 --> 01:00:19.570
in the brain?

01:00:19.570 --> 01:00:21.030
This is a paper
entitled "Effects

01:00:21.030 --> 01:00:24.048
of Voluntary Hyperventilation
on Cortical Sensory Responses."

01:00:24.048 --> 01:00:26.340
And I will provide a link to
the study in the show note

01:00:26.340 --> 01:00:27.060
captions.

01:00:27.060 --> 01:00:28.950
It's a somewhat
complicated paper

01:00:28.950 --> 01:00:31.590
if you look at all
the detailed analyzes.

01:00:31.590 --> 01:00:35.130
However, the takeaway from this
paper is exquisitely simple

01:00:35.130 --> 01:00:37.770
and I also believe
incredibly important.

01:00:37.770 --> 01:00:39.600
Basically, what it
showed is that when

01:00:39.600 --> 01:00:42.540
people hyperventilate,
they expel, that is,

01:00:42.540 --> 01:00:46.290
they exhale more carbon dioxide
than they would normally.

01:00:46.290 --> 01:00:50.010
So they become what's
called hypocapnic, OK?

01:00:50.010 --> 01:00:52.740
Carbon dioxide levels
are low in the blood.

01:00:52.740 --> 01:00:54.840
And over a short
period of time, they

01:00:54.840 --> 01:00:58.050
become low in the
tissues of the body.

01:00:58.050 --> 01:01:01.710
When that carbon
dioxide level drops low,

01:01:01.710 --> 01:01:03.870
you would say, OK,
well, you're still

01:01:03.870 --> 01:01:06.030
bringing in a lot of
oxygen, because these people

01:01:06.030 --> 01:01:07.110
are hyperventilating.

01:01:07.110 --> 01:01:08.675
So they should
feel really alert.

01:01:08.675 --> 01:01:10.050
And, indeed, that's
what happens.

01:01:10.050 --> 01:01:12.000
The people feel very alert.

01:01:12.000 --> 01:01:14.160
However, because
they're not bringing

01:01:14.160 --> 01:01:17.010
enough carbon dioxide in
or, rather, the proper way

01:01:17.010 --> 01:01:19.620
to say it would be because
they're overbreathing,

01:01:19.620 --> 01:01:24.210
exhaling too much, they are
not retaining or keeping

01:01:24.210 --> 01:01:27.000
in enough carbon dioxide.

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:29.670
Well, then that lack
of carbon dioxide

01:01:29.670 --> 01:01:32.700
means that the oxygen
that they are breathing in

01:01:32.700 --> 01:01:35.700
can't be liberated
from the hemoglobin,

01:01:35.700 --> 01:01:36.900
can't get to the brain.

01:01:36.900 --> 01:01:39.960
And what they observe
is about a 30% to 40%

01:01:39.960 --> 01:01:43.260
reduction in the
amount of oxygen that's

01:01:43.260 --> 01:01:45.180
being delivered to the brain.

01:01:45.180 --> 01:01:48.420
And the reduction
in carbon dioxide

01:01:48.420 --> 01:01:52.620
also prevents some of the
normal patterns of vasodilation,

01:01:52.620 --> 01:01:56.190
the dilating, the opening
up of the capillaries,

01:01:56.190 --> 01:01:58.520
so, again, less blood flow.

01:01:58.520 --> 01:02:01.820
But most importantly, as
it's shown in this paper,

01:02:01.820 --> 01:02:05.450
the brain overall
becomes hyperexcitable.

01:02:05.450 --> 01:02:08.780
It's as if it's being starved
of oxygen and blood flow.

01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:12.050
And all the neurons in
a very nonspecific way

01:02:12.050 --> 01:02:13.717
start increasing
their firing levels.

01:02:13.717 --> 01:02:16.050
So the background activity
is getting louder and louder.

01:02:16.050 --> 01:02:19.040
It's like the rumble or the
noise of a crowd at a stadium.

01:02:19.040 --> 01:02:24.620
And as a consequence, the
sensory input from a sound

01:02:24.620 --> 01:02:28.220
or from a touch or from some
other event in the world

01:02:28.220 --> 01:02:29.840
doesn't get above the noise.

01:02:29.840 --> 01:02:32.780
What this means is that
when we hyperventilate,

01:02:32.780 --> 01:02:36.140
because we aren't retaining
enough carbon dioxide,

01:02:36.140 --> 01:02:38.570
we are not getting enough
oxygen to the tissues

01:02:38.570 --> 01:02:42.710
that need oxygen. And as a
consequence of that, the brain

01:02:42.710 --> 01:02:44.030
becomes hyperexcitable.

01:02:44.030 --> 01:02:46.910
We actually know that there's
an increase in anxiety.

01:02:46.910 --> 01:02:51.800
And we become less
good, less efficient

01:02:51.800 --> 01:02:54.570
at detecting things
in our environment.

01:02:54.570 --> 01:02:57.450
So we're not processing
information as well at all.

01:02:57.450 --> 01:02:59.960
The noise goes up, and
the signal goes down.

01:02:59.960 --> 01:03:03.050
Again, incredibly
important set of findings.

01:03:03.050 --> 01:03:06.410
I should also mention
that hyperventilation

01:03:06.410 --> 01:03:09.020
is one way that, in
the laboratory anyway

01:03:09.020 --> 01:03:13.030
or in neurosurgery
units for some time,

01:03:13.030 --> 01:03:17.120
physicians would evoke seizure
in seizure-prone patients.

01:03:17.120 --> 01:03:19.420
The reason that works is
exactly the explanation

01:03:19.420 --> 01:03:20.470
I just gave you.

01:03:20.470 --> 01:03:23.260
Seizure is a excitability
of the brain, not enough

01:03:23.260 --> 01:03:25.650
inhibition or suppression
of the overall circuitry.

01:03:25.650 --> 01:03:28.930
So you get these waves or these
storms of electrical activity.

01:03:28.930 --> 01:03:32.110
Low levels of carbon
dioxide in the brain because

01:03:32.110 --> 01:03:35.500
of low levels of carbon
dioxide in the blood

01:03:35.500 --> 01:03:37.718
are one of the major
triggers for seizures.

01:03:37.718 --> 01:03:39.760
Now, I realize that most
people listening to this

01:03:39.760 --> 01:03:41.440
are not epileptic.

01:03:41.440 --> 01:03:44.032
But nonetheless, this
brings us all back

01:03:44.032 --> 01:03:46.240
to this question of what is
normal healthy breathing.

01:03:46.240 --> 01:03:48.198
As I mentioned before,
normal healthy breathing

01:03:48.198 --> 01:03:50.938
is breathing about six
liters of air per minute.

01:03:50.938 --> 01:03:52.480
But of course, most
of us don't think

01:03:52.480 --> 01:03:53.820
in terms of liters
of air, and we're not

01:03:53.820 --> 01:03:56.112
going to measure our lung
capacity, at least most of us

01:03:56.112 --> 01:03:57.250
aren't going to do that.

01:03:57.250 --> 01:04:00.550
Basically, if you are taking
relatively shallow breaths

01:04:00.550 --> 01:04:03.490
and you're just sitting there
working or maybe even walking

01:04:03.490 --> 01:04:05.650
slowly, again, not
talking or engaging

01:04:05.650 --> 01:04:09.050
in any kind of speech
or eating, chances

01:04:09.050 --> 01:04:15.020
are six liters of air per minute
is about 12 shallowish breaths.

01:04:15.020 --> 01:04:16.520
And when I say
shallow, I don't mean

01:04:16.520 --> 01:04:18.570
breathing [INHALES SHALLOWLY]
like a little bunny

01:04:18.570 --> 01:04:19.820
rabbit or something like that.

01:04:19.820 --> 01:04:24.570
I just mean casually
breathing in out, in out.

01:04:24.570 --> 01:04:26.820
The studies that have explored
the breathing patterns

01:04:26.820 --> 01:04:31.020
in large populations of
individuals who are not

01:04:31.020 --> 01:04:35.350
suffering necessarily from
any one specific ailment

01:04:35.350 --> 01:04:39.310
have shown that most
people breathe far too much

01:04:39.310 --> 01:04:41.260
per minute, that
they're engaging

01:04:41.260 --> 01:04:43.900
in anywhere from
15 to 20 or even 30

01:04:43.900 --> 01:04:45.350
shallow breaths per minute.

01:04:45.350 --> 01:04:49.008
So they are vastly
overbreathing relative to how

01:04:49.008 --> 01:04:50.050
they should be breathing.

01:04:50.050 --> 01:04:52.460
Now, of course, if you
breathe more deeply,

01:04:52.460 --> 01:04:55.870
so you take a vigorous
inhale [INHALES]

01:04:55.870 --> 01:04:58.420
and then you expel
that air, well,

01:04:58.420 --> 01:05:02.050
then to get six liters of air
into your system per minute,

01:05:02.050 --> 01:05:06.610
you're probably only going to
need somewhere between four

01:05:06.610 --> 01:05:11.000
and six breaths in order to
get that six liters per minute.

01:05:11.000 --> 01:05:13.510
Now, the total
time that it takes

01:05:13.510 --> 01:05:16.270
to do that inhale and exhale
isn't that much longer

01:05:16.270 --> 01:05:19.210
than a shallow breath, provided
you're not deliberately

01:05:19.210 --> 01:05:21.280
breathing quickly during
those shallow breaths.

01:05:21.280 --> 01:05:25.060
So then you say, well, how is
it that normal healthy breathing

01:05:25.060 --> 01:05:27.970
that delivers the appropriate
amount of carbon dioxide

01:05:27.970 --> 01:05:30.460
into the system
and doesn't expel,

01:05:30.460 --> 01:05:32.640
doesn't exhale too
much carbon dioxide--

01:05:32.640 --> 01:05:34.640
how are we supposed to
do that normal breathing?

01:05:34.640 --> 01:05:35.800
Are you supposed to
breathe four times

01:05:35.800 --> 01:05:37.390
and then hold your breath
until the minute passes?

01:05:37.390 --> 01:05:37.990
No.

01:05:37.990 --> 01:05:41.230
What you find is that the
correct pattern of breathing

01:05:41.230 --> 01:05:42.677
is going to involve two things.

01:05:42.677 --> 01:05:45.010
First of all, nasal breathing,
because of the resistance

01:05:45.010 --> 01:05:47.060
it provides through the nose
that we talked about earlier,

01:05:47.060 --> 01:05:49.240
is going to deliver more
oxygen into your system.

01:05:49.240 --> 01:05:51.130
You're going to be able
to generate more air

01:05:51.130 --> 01:05:52.930
pressure to fill your lungs.

01:05:52.930 --> 01:05:57.760
That greater air pressure is
going to take longer to exhale.

01:05:57.760 --> 01:05:59.680
So already we're increasing
the amount of time

01:05:59.680 --> 01:06:01.180
that each breath
is going to take.

01:06:01.180 --> 01:06:03.460
And also what you find
is that people that

01:06:03.460 --> 01:06:05.890
are breathing in the proper
healthy manner, that is,

01:06:05.890 --> 01:06:07.510
that are balancing
oxygen and carbon

01:06:07.510 --> 01:06:10.120
dioxide in the
proper ways, are also

01:06:10.120 --> 01:06:12.523
taking pauses between breaths.

01:06:12.523 --> 01:06:13.690
This is extremely important.

01:06:13.690 --> 01:06:15.482
Because even though we
have a brain center,

01:06:15.482 --> 01:06:18.910
the pre-Botzinger complex, that
can control or, I should say,

01:06:18.910 --> 01:06:21.520
does control inhale-exhale
rhythmic breathing,

01:06:21.520 --> 01:06:25.510
those pauses between breaths
are not always present

01:06:25.510 --> 01:06:27.490
and, in fact, often
are not present

01:06:27.490 --> 01:06:29.380
from people's baseline
breathing patterns.

01:06:29.380 --> 01:06:31.600
As a consequence,
they overbreathe.

01:06:31.600 --> 01:06:34.180
And as I told you before,
when people overbreathe,

01:06:34.180 --> 01:06:35.980
their brain becomes
hyperexcitable

01:06:35.980 --> 01:06:37.570
at the level of the
background noise.

01:06:37.570 --> 01:06:40.900
And yet they are less efficient
at detecting and learning

01:06:40.900 --> 01:06:42.142
information.

01:06:42.142 --> 01:06:43.600
We'll get into the
specific studies

01:06:43.600 --> 01:06:46.100
that really illustrate the
learning aspect a bit later.

01:06:46.100 --> 01:06:49.540
But they are less efficient
at detecting and learning

01:06:49.540 --> 01:06:53.657
information, at focusing,
and so on as a consequence

01:06:53.657 --> 01:06:55.615
of this overbreathing
and the hyperexcitability

01:06:55.615 --> 01:06:57.170
that it causes.

01:06:57.170 --> 01:07:00.400
Now, of course, that's
also just emphasizing

01:07:00.400 --> 01:07:02.380
the effects of
overbreathing and lack

01:07:02.380 --> 01:07:04.120
of carbon dioxide on the brain.

01:07:04.120 --> 01:07:06.730
There are hundreds,
if not thousands

01:07:06.730 --> 01:07:09.100
of studies showing that when
we don't have enough carbon

01:07:09.100 --> 01:07:10.930
dioxide in the
tissues of our body,

01:07:10.930 --> 01:07:14.090
that's also problematic for
all the tissues-- the liver,

01:07:14.090 --> 01:07:17.740
the lungs themselves, the
stomach, et cetera-- that

01:07:17.740 --> 01:07:21.640
relate largely to shifts
in pH because of the fact

01:07:21.640 --> 01:07:23.380
that carbon dioxide
strongly regulates

01:07:23.380 --> 01:07:26.500
the acidity, alkalinity of
the blood and the tissues

01:07:26.500 --> 01:07:29.050
that that blood
supplies nutrients to,

01:07:29.050 --> 01:07:30.530
including carbon dioxide.

01:07:30.530 --> 01:07:33.250
So the basic
takeaway here is you

01:07:33.250 --> 01:07:35.650
want to breathe in a
healthy manner at rest.

01:07:35.650 --> 01:07:39.130
And the best way to do that
is to spend some time--

01:07:39.130 --> 01:07:42.130
and it doesn't take much,
maybe a minute or so each day--

01:07:42.130 --> 01:07:46.450
paying attention to how quickly
you are breathing per minute

01:07:46.450 --> 01:07:49.148
when you are simply at rest,
when you're making coffee

01:07:49.148 --> 01:07:51.190
in the morning, when you're
sitting down to read,

01:07:51.190 --> 01:07:53.670
when you're on social media.

01:07:53.670 --> 01:07:55.420
Chronically holding
your breath isn't good

01:07:55.420 --> 01:07:57.160
but neither is overbreathing.

01:07:57.160 --> 01:08:01.900
And, again, every study that has
examined the typical patterns

01:08:01.900 --> 01:08:03.820
of breathing and
patterns of breathing

01:08:03.820 --> 01:08:05.620
that show up as
normal and abnormal

01:08:05.620 --> 01:08:07.330
has found that more
often than not,

01:08:07.330 --> 01:08:10.270
during the nighttime,
people are underbreathing.

01:08:10.270 --> 01:08:12.940
And in the daytime,
they are overbreathing.

01:08:12.940 --> 01:08:14.350
They're hyperventilating.

