01:09:31.904 --> 01:09:33.279
So next, I'd like
to address what

01:09:33.279 --> 01:09:35.404
you can do about your normal
patterns of breathing,

01:09:35.404 --> 01:09:38.069
that is, how you or
anyone can adjust

01:09:38.069 --> 01:09:40.500
their normal patterns of
breathing from an unhealthy

01:09:40.500 --> 01:09:41.670
to an unhealthy state.

01:09:41.670 --> 01:09:43.180
But the first thing we
have to do, of course,

01:09:43.180 --> 01:09:44.930
is determine whether
or not you're already

01:09:44.930 --> 01:09:47.729
breathing in an unhealthy
or in a healthy way.

01:09:47.729 --> 01:09:50.250
And, again, when I say
healthy or unhealthy, I mean,

01:09:50.250 --> 01:09:51.372
are you overbreathing?

01:09:51.372 --> 01:09:52.330
Are you underbreathing?

01:09:52.330 --> 01:09:54.038
Are you delivering
the appropriate ratios

01:09:54.038 --> 01:09:56.190
of oxygen and carbon
dioxide to the tissues

01:09:56.190 --> 01:09:57.810
of your brain and body?

01:09:57.810 --> 01:10:00.570
In order to do this, we're
going to do a simple test.

01:10:00.570 --> 01:10:02.160
Again, please
don't do this while

01:10:02.160 --> 01:10:06.360
driving or operating heavy
machinery or near water

01:10:06.360 --> 01:10:07.170
of any kind.

01:10:07.170 --> 01:10:10.200
But assuming that you're not
doing any of those things,

01:10:10.200 --> 01:10:12.960
I encourage you to
sit down, certainly

01:10:12.960 --> 01:10:14.683
not lie down but just sit down.

01:10:14.683 --> 01:10:16.350
I suppose you also
could do it standing.

01:10:16.350 --> 01:10:19.200
And we are going to do what's
called the carbon dioxide

01:10:19.200 --> 01:10:20.495
tolerance test.

01:10:20.495 --> 01:10:21.870
The carbon dioxide
tolerance test

01:10:21.870 --> 01:10:23.580
is a sort of back
of the envelope

01:10:23.580 --> 01:10:27.270
measure of how well you are
managing carbon dioxide,

01:10:27.270 --> 01:10:30.120
that is, how well you can
control your breathing

01:10:30.120 --> 01:10:33.640
at both the mechanical
and the chemical level.

01:10:33.640 --> 01:10:34.710
It's a very simple test.

01:10:34.710 --> 01:10:37.590
What you're going to do
is for the next 10 seconds

01:10:37.590 --> 01:10:40.530
or so while I'm speaking, you're
just going to breathe normally.

01:10:40.530 --> 01:10:43.180
Now, again and again
throughout this episode,

01:10:43.180 --> 01:10:46.470
I'm going to encourage you to
be a nasal breather whenever

01:10:46.470 --> 01:10:47.160
possible.

01:10:47.160 --> 01:10:50.070
But of course, there are
instances in which you

01:10:50.070 --> 01:10:51.810
want to engage mouth breathing.

01:10:51.810 --> 01:10:53.490
But for the time
being, as I continue

01:10:53.490 --> 01:10:56.430
to blab on for the
next few seconds,

01:10:56.430 --> 01:10:59.537
just inhale through your nose,
exhale through your nose.

01:10:59.537 --> 01:11:01.620
You don't have to deliberately
slow your breathing

01:11:01.620 --> 01:11:03.870
or increase the cadence
of your breathing.

01:11:03.870 --> 01:11:05.850
However, in that
time, you're also

01:11:05.850 --> 01:11:08.580
going to want to find some
sort of time measuring

01:11:08.580 --> 01:11:11.850
device, like could be your phone
or it could be a stopwatch.

01:11:11.850 --> 01:11:14.190
What I'm going to ask you
to do in a few minutes

01:11:14.190 --> 01:11:17.910
is I'm going to ask you to
inhale through your nose

01:11:17.910 --> 01:11:19.698
as deeply as you possibly can.

01:11:19.698 --> 01:11:21.990
That is, you're going to fill
your lungs as much as you

01:11:21.990 --> 01:11:23.760
can through your nose.

01:11:23.760 --> 01:11:27.510
And then start a
timer and measure

01:11:27.510 --> 01:11:31.560
how long it takes for you
to deliberately control

01:11:31.560 --> 01:11:34.270
that exhale until
your lungs are empty.

01:11:34.270 --> 01:11:36.300
So this is going to
be a controlled exhale

01:11:36.300 --> 01:11:38.797
through the nose after
a big deep breath.

01:11:38.797 --> 01:11:40.380
But for the time
being, keep breathing

01:11:40.380 --> 01:11:42.990
at a kind of calm,
regular cadence.

01:11:42.990 --> 01:11:45.210
So you can find that time
measuring device now,

01:11:45.210 --> 01:11:47.100
or you can come back to
it later if you like.

01:11:47.100 --> 01:11:50.130
When I say inhale, you're going
to inhale as deeply as you can

01:11:50.130 --> 01:11:53.340
through your nose, remembering
that the diaphragm can really

01:11:53.340 --> 01:11:55.380
help you here to get a
deep inhale by having

01:11:55.380 --> 01:11:58.830
your belly move out
while you inhale.

01:11:58.830 --> 01:12:01.080
And then when I
say start, you're

01:12:01.080 --> 01:12:03.510
going to measure the
time that it takes to do

01:12:03.510 --> 01:12:05.730
a complete lungs empty exhale.

01:12:05.730 --> 01:12:08.110
In fact, I'll
measure it for you.

01:12:08.110 --> 01:12:10.860
This will be one of the rare
instances in this podcast

01:12:10.860 --> 01:12:14.190
where there's going to be
a long period of silence

01:12:14.190 --> 01:12:15.280
as I measure something.

01:12:15.280 --> 01:12:16.488
So I've got a stopwatch here.

01:12:16.488 --> 01:12:23.210
So please prepare to do the big
inhale and start inhaling now.

01:12:23.210 --> 01:12:25.670
So inhale as deeply as
you can through your nose.

01:12:25.670 --> 01:12:29.430
Fill your lungs as
much as you can.

01:12:29.430 --> 01:12:30.090
OK?

01:12:30.090 --> 01:12:34.410
Now start, meaning
slowly control the exhale

01:12:34.410 --> 01:12:35.160
through your nose.

01:12:35.160 --> 01:12:37.690
You're trying to let that air
out as slowly as possible.

01:12:37.690 --> 01:12:41.880
And I'm just going to call out
every 10 or 15 seconds or so.

01:12:41.880 --> 01:12:44.970
And you want to note when
your lungs are empty.

01:12:44.970 --> 01:12:47.230
I know you can hold your
breath with your lungs empty.

01:12:47.230 --> 01:12:48.780
That is not an accurate measure.

01:12:48.780 --> 01:12:51.820
15 seconds.

01:12:51.820 --> 01:12:55.240
It is important that when
note your lungs are empty

01:12:55.240 --> 01:12:57.640
and that you're trying to
control the exhale as much

01:12:57.640 --> 01:13:01.510
as possible so that you don't
arrive at that lungs empty time

01:13:01.510 --> 01:13:02.920
too quickly.

01:13:02.920 --> 01:13:05.380
I'll explain what
too quickly means.

01:13:05.380 --> 01:13:07.680
30 seconds.

01:13:07.680 --> 01:13:11.070
OK, for those of you that have
already reached lungs empty,

01:13:11.070 --> 01:13:14.350
please go back to
breathing normally.

01:13:14.350 --> 01:13:16.510
For those of you
that haven't, you

01:13:16.510 --> 01:13:19.180
can hang in here a little
longer if you're still

01:13:19.180 --> 01:13:20.320
discarding that air.

01:13:20.320 --> 01:13:22.710
45 seconds.

01:13:22.710 --> 01:13:25.840
And we're rounding toward
a minute, not quite there.

01:13:25.840 --> 01:13:28.530
Some of you are probably
still letting out that air.

01:13:28.530 --> 01:13:30.030
I want to point out
none of this has

01:13:30.030 --> 01:13:32.610
to do with cardiovascular
fitness level, at least

01:13:32.610 --> 01:13:35.080
not in any kind of direct way.

01:13:35.080 --> 01:13:36.398
And 60 seconds.

01:13:36.398 --> 01:13:38.440
And I realize there will
be a small subset of you

01:13:38.440 --> 01:13:40.630
out there that are
still expelling

01:13:40.630 --> 01:13:43.390
your air in a slow lungs--

01:13:43.390 --> 01:13:45.760
slow exhale manner
through the nose.

01:13:45.760 --> 01:13:48.610
OK, so what we just did is a
back of the envelope carbon

01:13:48.610 --> 01:13:53.020
dioxide discard rate if
you need to pause this

01:13:53.020 --> 01:13:54.640
and go back and try
it again you just

01:13:54.640 --> 01:13:56.057
want to time how
long it takes you

01:13:56.057 --> 01:13:58.368
to go from lungs full
to lungs empty, again,

01:13:58.368 --> 01:14:00.910
with the full understanding I
know that you can all sit there

01:14:00.910 --> 01:14:03.190
like beasts and hold your
breath with your lungs empty.

01:14:03.190 --> 01:14:05.232
But please don't do that
because that's not going

01:14:05.232 --> 01:14:07.420
has been informative for
what I'm telling you now.

01:14:07.420 --> 01:14:08.795
What I'm going to
tell you now is

01:14:08.795 --> 01:14:14.530
that if it took you 20 seconds
or less to expel all your air,

01:14:14.530 --> 01:14:16.780
that is, you couldn't
extend that exhale longer

01:14:16.780 --> 01:14:20.740
than 20 seconds, in a kind
of back of the envelope way,

01:14:20.740 --> 01:14:24.370
we can say that have a
relatively brief or low carbon

01:14:24.370 --> 01:14:26.950
dioxide tolerance.

01:14:26.950 --> 01:14:32.530
If it took you somewhere between
25 and 40, maybe 45 seconds

01:14:32.530 --> 01:14:34.540
to expel all your
air, that is, you

01:14:34.540 --> 01:14:39.010
could control that exhale for
about 45 seconds or 30 seconds,

01:14:39.010 --> 01:14:43.480
then you have a moderate level
of carbon dioxide tolerance.

01:14:43.480 --> 01:14:46.720
And if, for instance, you
were able to go 50 seconds

01:14:46.720 --> 01:14:49.990
or longer for that discard
until you hit lungs empty,

01:14:49.990 --> 01:14:52.870
you have a fairly high degree
of carbon dioxide tolerance.

01:14:52.870 --> 01:14:55.190
Now, here's the deal.

01:14:55.190 --> 01:14:58.030
If you had low carbon
dioxide tolerance, that

01:14:58.030 --> 01:14:59.770
is, you're 20 seconds
or less, you're

01:14:59.770 --> 01:15:02.920
going to write down
the number three.

01:15:02.920 --> 01:15:05.860
If you had moderate levels
of carbon dioxide tolerance,

01:15:05.860 --> 01:15:08.800
you're going to write
down the number five.

01:15:08.800 --> 01:15:11.230
or you could even
put five to six.

01:15:11.230 --> 01:15:14.500
And then if you are in
that bracket of people

01:15:14.500 --> 01:15:18.220
that was able to discard your
air over a period of 50 seconds

01:15:18.220 --> 01:15:22.830
or more, you're going to
write down the number 8 to 10.

01:15:22.830 --> 01:15:23.380
OK?

01:15:23.380 --> 01:15:24.640
Now, what are these numbers?

01:15:24.640 --> 01:15:25.723
What are we talking about?

01:15:25.723 --> 01:15:28.390
And before we get into what
to do with these numbers,

01:15:28.390 --> 01:15:30.850
I want to emphasize
again, this does not

01:15:30.850 --> 01:15:32.920
have to do with
fitness level per se.

01:15:32.920 --> 01:15:35.350
I know some world
class triathletes

01:15:35.350 --> 01:15:38.440
that have very fast carbon
dioxide blow-off times.

01:15:38.440 --> 01:15:40.642
That is, their discard rates
are 20 seconds or less.

01:15:40.642 --> 01:15:42.850
I should also point out that
if you're very stressed,

01:15:42.850 --> 01:15:44.725
that number is going
to be very small.

01:15:44.725 --> 01:15:46.600
If you're very relaxed,
like you just woke up

01:15:46.600 --> 01:15:49.210
after a long night of
sleep and you feel great,

01:15:49.210 --> 01:15:50.832
that number is going
to be extended.

01:15:50.832 --> 01:15:52.540
So this is a back of
the envelope measure

01:15:52.540 --> 01:15:54.742
that you're going
to use each time you

01:15:54.742 --> 01:15:56.200
decide to do the
exercise I'm going

01:15:56.200 --> 01:15:57.410
to tell you about in a moment.

01:15:57.410 --> 01:15:59.743
And the exercise I'm going
to tell you about in a moment

01:15:59.743 --> 01:16:01.540
can be done every
day if you like.

01:16:01.540 --> 01:16:05.110
But what the most interesting
studies, at least to me,

01:16:05.110 --> 01:16:07.280
indicate is that you
could do the exercise

01:16:07.280 --> 01:16:09.670
I'll tell you about even
just once or twice a week

01:16:09.670 --> 01:16:13.060
and greatly improve your
efficiency of breathing

01:16:13.060 --> 01:16:15.868
and shift yourself away from
overbreathing when at rest,

01:16:15.868 --> 01:16:17.410
even if you're not
thinking about how

01:16:17.410 --> 01:16:18.940
you're breathing at rest.

01:16:18.940 --> 01:16:20.170
So what is this exercise?

01:16:20.170 --> 01:16:23.710
Well, you just got your number,
either low, medium, or high

01:16:23.710 --> 01:16:26.950
bracket number for carbon
dioxide discard rate.

01:16:26.950 --> 01:16:29.590
Remember, if you're in the low
category, your number is three.

01:16:29.590 --> 01:16:31.150
If you're medium,
it's five to six.

01:16:31.150 --> 01:16:33.850
And if you are in the
long carbon dioxide

01:16:33.850 --> 01:16:35.530
discard rate, long
duration carbon

01:16:35.530 --> 01:16:38.570
dioxide discard rate, that
is, 8 to 10 is your number.

01:16:38.570 --> 01:16:42.010
Now you're going to do two
minutes of what most people

01:16:42.010 --> 01:16:43.450
would call box breathing.

01:16:43.450 --> 01:16:44.500
What is box breathing?

01:16:44.500 --> 01:16:49.030
Box breathing are equal
duration inhale, hold,

01:16:49.030 --> 01:16:51.220
exhale, hold, repeat.

01:16:51.220 --> 01:16:53.170
So inhale, hold, exhale, hold.

01:16:53.170 --> 01:16:54.460
Sounds very easy, right?

01:16:54.460 --> 01:16:59.800
How long do you inhale and then
hold, exhale and then hold?

01:16:59.800 --> 01:17:00.940
Well, you now know.

01:17:00.940 --> 01:17:04.067
If you are in the low group of
carbon dioxide discard rate,

01:17:04.067 --> 01:17:06.400
your inhale is going to be
three seconds, your hold will

01:17:06.400 --> 01:17:09.100
be three seconds, your
exhale will be three seconds,

01:17:09.100 --> 01:17:11.180
and then you repeat,
three seconds.

01:17:11.180 --> 01:17:12.943
So each side of the
box, if you will,

01:17:12.943 --> 01:17:14.360
is going to be
three seconds long.

01:17:14.360 --> 01:17:18.190
If you were in the moderate
carbon dioxide discard rate

01:17:18.190 --> 01:17:21.290
category, then you're going to
inhale for five to six seconds,

01:17:21.290 --> 01:17:23.800
hold for five to six,
exhale for five to six,

01:17:23.800 --> 01:17:26.195
hold for five to six, repeat
for about two minutes.

01:17:26.195 --> 01:17:27.820
You could do three
minutes if you want.

01:17:27.820 --> 01:17:30.430
But I think it's important
to have protocols that

01:17:30.430 --> 01:17:31.990
are feasible for most people.

01:17:31.990 --> 01:17:34.930
And that's going to mean
doing things for about two

01:17:34.930 --> 01:17:37.690
to five minutes when it comes
to these breath rehabilitation

01:17:37.690 --> 01:17:40.300
exercises for restoring
normal breathing.

01:17:40.300 --> 01:17:44.380
And then, of course, if you are
in the long category of carbon

01:17:44.380 --> 01:17:47.530
dioxide discard rate, you
should be able to do an 8 to 10

01:17:47.530 --> 01:17:52.380
second inhale, 8 to 10 second
hold, 8 to 10 second exhale, 8

01:17:52.380 --> 01:17:54.980
to 10 second hold, and repeat.

01:17:54.980 --> 01:17:57.310
So you could do
that exercise now

01:17:57.310 --> 01:18:00.340
if you like, or you could
do it at some point offline.

01:18:00.340 --> 01:18:02.740
You can pause this podcast
if you want and go try it.

01:18:02.740 --> 01:18:05.590
That's an exercise
that you can do

01:18:05.590 --> 01:18:09.460
for about two to three minutes
once or twice per week.

01:18:09.460 --> 01:18:11.530
What's happening when
you do that exercise?

01:18:11.530 --> 01:18:13.930
Well, first of all,
you are greatly

01:18:13.930 --> 01:18:18.230
increasing your neuromechanical
control over the diaphragm.

01:18:18.230 --> 01:18:19.352
This is very important.

01:18:19.352 --> 01:18:21.310
Most people are not aware
of this phrenic nerve

01:18:21.310 --> 01:18:22.660
pathway in the diaphragm.

01:18:22.660 --> 01:18:26.068
And you are greatly increasing
your mechanical control

01:18:26.068 --> 01:18:27.610
over this pathway
through the process

01:18:27.610 --> 01:18:28.990
we call neuroplasticity.

01:18:28.990 --> 01:18:33.280
When you deliberately focus on
a aspect of your nervous system

01:18:33.280 --> 01:18:35.958
control and particular nervous
system control over musculature

01:18:35.958 --> 01:18:38.500
that normally is subconscious
and you're not paying attention

01:18:38.500 --> 01:18:40.840
to and when you actively
take control of that,

01:18:40.840 --> 01:18:45.008
it requires that your
brain adjust and rewire

01:18:45.008 --> 01:18:47.050
the relationship between
the different components

01:18:47.050 --> 01:18:47.800
of that circuit.

01:18:47.800 --> 01:18:50.020
And the wonderful
thing is that has

01:18:50.020 --> 01:18:52.780
been shown to lead to changes
in your resting pattern

01:18:52.780 --> 01:18:53.650
of breathing.

01:18:53.650 --> 01:18:56.020
Now, why did we go
through the whole business

01:18:56.020 --> 01:18:58.030
of doing the carbon
dioxide tolerance test?

01:18:58.030 --> 01:19:00.490
Well, for people who don't
tolerate carbon dioxide very

01:19:00.490 --> 01:19:03.130
well, they don't have
very good phrenic,

01:19:03.130 --> 01:19:05.860
that is, neuromechanical
control of the diaphragm,

01:19:05.860 --> 01:19:08.410
for whatever reason-- again,
it doesn't mean you're not fit.

01:19:08.410 --> 01:19:11.620
It just means you don't have
or you have not yet developed

01:19:11.620 --> 01:19:13.670
neuromechanical control
of the diaphragm.

01:19:13.670 --> 01:19:15.670
It would be near
impossible for you

01:19:15.670 --> 01:19:18.530
to do box breathing for two
or three minutes with eight

01:19:18.530 --> 01:19:20.830
seconds in, eight seconds
hold, eight seconds exhale,

01:19:20.830 --> 01:19:21.670
eight second hold.

01:19:21.670 --> 01:19:24.910
So that's why we
do a test to see

01:19:24.910 --> 01:19:26.125
what you're capable of doing.

01:19:26.125 --> 01:19:29.200
You don't want the box breathing
to be too strained where you're

01:19:29.200 --> 01:19:31.780
[GRUNTS],, where you're
really challenged

01:19:31.780 --> 01:19:32.947
to get around the whole box.

01:19:32.947 --> 01:19:35.113
You want it to be relatively
easy because, remember,

01:19:35.113 --> 01:19:37.750
you're trying to translate this
pattern to your normal pattern

01:19:37.750 --> 01:19:40.420
of breathing, that is,
your pattern of breathing

01:19:40.420 --> 01:19:42.700
when you're not consciously
thinking about breathing.

01:19:42.700 --> 01:19:44.590
And what are we
really translating

01:19:44.590 --> 01:19:46.750
when we do this box
breathing type exercise?

01:19:46.750 --> 01:19:49.270
What you're translating
is the ability

01:19:49.270 --> 01:19:52.630
to pause between
breaths and yet take

01:19:52.630 --> 01:19:57.520
full mechanically-driven breaths
that involve the phrenic nerve

01:19:57.520 --> 01:19:58.190
and diaphragm.

01:19:58.190 --> 01:20:00.263
So, again, you're
encouraging, especially

01:20:00.263 --> 01:20:02.680
if you use nasal breathing
when you do the box breathing--

01:20:02.680 --> 01:20:05.890
you're encouraging phrenic
control over the diaphragm.

01:20:05.890 --> 01:20:09.220
And you're getting that six
liters of air per minute

01:20:09.220 --> 01:20:12.680
or so using fewer and
fewer breaths over time.

01:20:12.680 --> 01:20:14.950
So this is a, again,
zero cost-- although it

01:20:14.950 --> 01:20:17.500
does cost a little bit of
time-- zero cost approach

01:20:17.500 --> 01:20:20.740
to adjusting your normal pattern
of breathing at rest, which

01:20:20.740 --> 01:20:24.070
has a huge number of
positive outcomes in terms

01:20:24.070 --> 01:20:27.550
of your ability to stay
relatively calm, to not

01:20:27.550 --> 01:20:29.690
get the hyperexcitability
of the brain.

01:20:29.690 --> 01:20:32.143
It has actually been
shown in various studies--

01:20:32.143 --> 01:20:34.060
and we'll talk about one
in particular later--

01:20:34.060 --> 01:20:38.290
to greatly improve not
just levels of calm

01:20:38.290 --> 01:20:41.680
and reduce bouts of stress but
also improve nighttime sleep.

01:20:41.680 --> 01:20:43.060
There are huge
number of benefits

01:20:43.060 --> 01:20:45.160
that can come from doing
this box breathing exercise.

01:20:45.160 --> 01:20:47.702
But you got to get the duration
of the size of the box right,

01:20:47.702 --> 01:20:50.170
and that's why you do the
carbon dioxide tolerance test.

01:20:50.170 --> 01:20:51.700
One thing that
many people notice

01:20:51.700 --> 01:20:54.760
after doing the carbon dioxide
tolerance test even just once

01:20:54.760 --> 01:20:58.300
and then doing this box
breathing exercise once

01:20:58.300 --> 01:21:02.650
or twice a week is that after
two or three weeks, the box

01:21:02.650 --> 01:21:04.580
breathing itself
becomes very easy.

01:21:04.580 --> 01:21:07.030
And in that case, I recommend
taking the carbon dioxide

01:21:07.030 --> 01:21:08.650
tolerance test over again.

01:21:08.650 --> 01:21:10.870
And almost always
what you'll find

01:21:10.870 --> 01:21:14.020
is that you have been
able to extend your carbon

01:21:14.020 --> 01:21:17.650
dioxide discard rate,
and therefore, you now

01:21:17.650 --> 01:21:21.370
fall into a different category,
not just the lower medium

01:21:21.370 --> 01:21:25.000
but the long carbon dioxide
discard rate category,

01:21:25.000 --> 01:21:28.390
and you are able to extend
the duration of those inhale,

01:21:28.390 --> 01:21:30.940
hold, exhale, holds
during the box breathing.

01:21:30.940 --> 01:21:34.090
And, of course, the
ultimate benefit of all this

01:21:34.090 --> 01:21:38.710
is that it translates to
deeper and yet less frequent

01:21:38.710 --> 01:21:42.082
breathing when at rest and
when not consciously paying

01:21:42.082 --> 01:21:44.290
attention to how you're
breathing during the daytime.

01:21:44.290 --> 01:21:47.110
Again, if at all possible,
do all of this breathing

01:21:47.110 --> 01:21:48.160
through the nose.

01:21:48.160 --> 01:21:51.040
For those of you that have
a severely occluded nose,

01:21:51.040 --> 01:21:54.417
the recommendation always is to
breathe through your nose more.

01:21:54.417 --> 01:21:56.000
But I do realize
that for some people,

01:21:56.000 --> 01:21:57.760
it's really uncomfortable
to breathe through the nose

01:21:57.760 --> 01:22:00.110
because they have such an
occluded nasal pathway.

01:22:00.110 --> 01:22:02.710
And for you folks, doing
some of this breathing

01:22:02.710 --> 01:22:05.600
through the mouth
can probably suffice.

01:22:05.600 --> 01:22:08.440
But if at all possible, do the
breathing through the nose.

01:22:08.440 --> 01:22:12.850
And please also let me know how
your progress evolves over time

01:22:12.850 --> 01:22:15.430
with the carbon dioxide discard
rate and the box breathing.

01:22:15.430 --> 01:22:16.870
And of course, the
positive shifts

01:22:16.870 --> 01:22:20.260
that occur in normal
unconscious daytime breathing

01:22:20.260 --> 01:22:23.140
translate to all
the opposite things

01:22:23.140 --> 01:22:25.480
that we talked about when
you are overbreathing

01:22:25.480 --> 01:22:26.830
during the daytime.

01:22:26.830 --> 01:22:29.200
So what I just described in
terms of the carbon dioxide

01:22:29.200 --> 01:22:33.430
tolerance test and the
exercise using box breathing

01:22:33.430 --> 01:22:35.380
to restore normal
patterns of breathing

01:22:35.380 --> 01:22:38.530
and not overbreathe and
therefore not eliminate

01:22:38.530 --> 01:22:41.410
too much carbon
dioxide is exactly

01:22:41.410 --> 01:22:44.020
the two tests that were
incorporated into a study

01:22:44.020 --> 01:22:45.880
that my laboratory
did in collaboration

01:22:45.880 --> 01:22:48.017
with our associate
chair of psychiatry

01:22:48.017 --> 01:22:50.350
at Stanford School of Medicine,
Dr. David Spiegel, who's

01:22:50.350 --> 01:22:52.870
also been a guest on
this podcast previously.

01:22:52.870 --> 01:22:56.290
And that study
explored box breathing.

01:22:56.290 --> 01:22:58.750
But it also explored
other forms of breathing

01:22:58.750 --> 01:23:01.180
and actually compared those
forms of deliberate breathing

01:23:01.180 --> 01:23:04.810
to meditation as
a means to explore

01:23:04.810 --> 01:23:09.310
what are going to be the
minimal effective doses and most

01:23:09.310 --> 01:23:13.750
effective ways to chronically
reduce stress around the clock

01:23:13.750 --> 01:23:16.660
and improve mood
and improve sleep.

01:23:16.660 --> 01:23:20.020
So the study I'm referring to
was just published recently.

01:23:20.020 --> 01:23:23.680
It's entitled "Brief Structured
Respiration Practices Enhance

01:23:23.680 --> 01:23:25.733
Mood and Reduce
Physiological Arousal."

01:23:25.733 --> 01:23:28.150
We will also provide a link
to this paper in the show note

01:23:28.150 --> 01:23:29.050
captions.

01:23:29.050 --> 01:23:31.030
What this study
really focused on

01:23:31.030 --> 01:23:35.560
was a simple question, which is,
what is the shortest and most

01:23:35.560 --> 01:23:38.410
effective practice that
people can use in order

01:23:38.410 --> 01:23:42.790
to reduce their levels of stress
not just during that breathwork

01:23:42.790 --> 01:23:46.040
practice or meditation practice
but around the clock, 24

01:23:46.040 --> 01:23:48.760
hours a day, including
improvements in sleep?

01:23:48.760 --> 01:23:51.610
And we were excited to do this
study because many studies had

01:23:51.610 --> 01:23:55.120
explored how meditation
or, in some cases,

01:23:55.120 --> 01:23:58.060
fewer studies have
explored how breathwork

01:23:58.060 --> 01:24:00.430
can impact different brain
states or bodily states.

01:24:00.430 --> 01:24:04.510
But very few studies had
explored how those breathwork

01:24:04.510 --> 01:24:07.990
or meditation practices
influenced body-brain states

01:24:07.990 --> 01:24:09.940
around the clock
when people were not

01:24:09.940 --> 01:24:12.490
performing the particular
meditation or breathwork

01:24:12.490 --> 01:24:13.270
practice.

01:24:13.270 --> 01:24:15.250
The reason we were
able to do this study

01:24:15.250 --> 01:24:16.717
was really fortunate.

01:24:16.717 --> 01:24:18.550
The folks over at WHOOP
were generous enough

01:24:18.550 --> 01:24:20.560
to donate a bunch of
WHOOP straps, which

01:24:20.560 --> 01:24:23.740
allowed us to measure
heart rate variability,

01:24:23.740 --> 01:24:26.080
a number of other different
physiological parameters.

01:24:26.080 --> 01:24:28.735
We also got subjective
reports about people's mood

01:24:28.735 --> 01:24:29.860
and feelings of well-being.

01:24:29.860 --> 01:24:33.070
We got data about their
sleep pinged to us

01:24:33.070 --> 01:24:34.680
from remote locations.

01:24:34.680 --> 01:24:37.180
So these people, rather than
being brought to the laboratory

01:24:37.180 --> 01:24:40.950
and being in a very artificial
circumstance, the laboratory,

01:24:40.950 --> 01:24:42.700
as much as we like to
think our laboratory

01:24:42.700 --> 01:24:44.283
is realistic-- we
have virtual reality

01:24:44.283 --> 01:24:45.867
and things like
that-- there's nothing

01:24:45.867 --> 01:24:47.255
as realistic as the real world.

01:24:47.255 --> 01:24:48.880
And so we were able
to have more than a

01:24:48.880 --> 01:24:51.880
hundred subjects out in the real
world living their real lives

01:24:51.880 --> 01:24:55.270
pinging back to us data all
the time, 24 hours a day

01:24:55.270 --> 01:24:58.960
so that we could measure how
their different interventions

01:24:58.960 --> 01:25:01.000
that we asked them to
do, breathwork practices

01:25:01.000 --> 01:25:02.860
or meditation practices,
were impacting

01:25:02.860 --> 01:25:04.180
physiological parameters.

01:25:04.180 --> 01:25:06.190
And they were also
informing us regularly

01:25:06.190 --> 01:25:09.010
about their subjective
mood, et cetera.

01:25:09.010 --> 01:25:11.320
We got a lot of data,
as you can imagine.

01:25:11.320 --> 01:25:14.030
And the basic takeaway
from the study was twofold.

01:25:14.030 --> 01:25:19.060
First of all, we discovered that
deliberate breathwork practices

01:25:19.060 --> 01:25:22.010
done for about five
minutes per day

01:25:22.010 --> 01:25:25.170
across the course
of about a month led

01:25:25.170 --> 01:25:29.910
to greater reductions in stress
than did a five minute a day

01:25:29.910 --> 01:25:31.200
meditation practice.

01:25:31.200 --> 01:25:33.930
Now, that is not to say that
meditation is not useful.

01:25:33.930 --> 01:25:37.480
In fact, there are dozens,
if not hundreds, of papers,

01:25:37.480 --> 01:25:39.900
including one
particular, I should

01:25:39.900 --> 01:25:42.720
say, particularly beautiful
study from Wendy Suzuki's lab

01:25:42.720 --> 01:25:46.980
at New York University showing
that a daily 10 to 13 minute

01:25:46.980 --> 01:25:51.180
mindfulness meditation practice
can greatly improve focus,

01:25:51.180 --> 01:25:53.820
memory, and a number of other
things related to cognition

01:25:53.820 --> 01:25:54.760
and learning.

01:25:54.760 --> 01:25:56.850
However, the research
on meditation

01:25:56.850 --> 01:26:00.740
has shown us that meditation,
at least short meditations,

01:26:00.740 --> 01:26:03.650
mainly lead to improvements
in focus and memory,

01:26:03.650 --> 01:26:06.260
not so much reductions in
stress, although they do

01:26:06.260 --> 01:26:07.880
lead to reductions in stress.

01:26:07.880 --> 01:26:11.540
What we found was that any
number of different breathwork

01:26:11.540 --> 01:26:13.190
practices-- and we
explored three--

01:26:13.190 --> 01:26:16.940
done for five minutes a
day outperformed meditation

01:26:16.940 --> 01:26:19.340
in terms of the
ability of breathwork

01:26:19.340 --> 01:26:23.690
to reduce stress around the
clock compared to meditation.

01:26:23.690 --> 01:26:26.510
The three types of breathwork
that we explored also

01:26:26.510 --> 01:26:28.163
showed different effects.

01:26:28.163 --> 01:26:30.080
I should mention the
three types of breathwork

01:26:30.080 --> 01:26:33.320
that we compared were
box breathing of the sort

01:26:33.320 --> 01:26:34.730
that you just learned about.

01:26:34.730 --> 01:26:38.990
We compare that to something
called cyclic sighing, which

01:26:38.990 --> 01:26:42.140
involves two inhales
through the nose

01:26:42.140 --> 01:26:45.710
to get maximally inflated lungs
followed by a long exhale.

01:26:45.710 --> 01:26:47.430
I'll return to that in a moment.

01:26:47.430 --> 01:26:48.920
That was repeated
for five minutes

01:26:48.920 --> 01:26:50.550
at a time for each session.

01:26:50.550 --> 01:26:52.430
And a third breathwork
practice, which

01:26:52.430 --> 01:26:55.130
was cyclic hyperventilation,
which, as the name suggests,

01:26:55.130 --> 01:26:58.370
involves people inhaling
deeply through the nose,

01:26:58.370 --> 01:27:00.398
then exhaling passively
through the mouth,

01:27:00.398 --> 01:27:02.190
and then repeating
inhale through the nose,

01:27:02.190 --> 01:27:03.648
exhale through the
mouth, repeating

01:27:03.648 --> 01:27:07.280
that for 25 cycles, one cycle
being an inhale and an exhale.

01:27:07.280 --> 01:27:08.330
So that equals one cycle.

01:27:08.330 --> 01:27:11.222
Repeating that for 25 cycles,
then exhaling all their air

01:27:11.222 --> 01:27:12.680
and holding their
breath with lungs

01:27:12.680 --> 01:27:14.390
empty for about
15 to 30 seconds,

01:27:14.390 --> 01:27:17.990
and then repeating inhale,
exhale, cyclic hyperventilation

01:27:17.990 --> 01:27:19.915
for the duration
of five minutes.

01:27:19.915 --> 01:27:22.040
So people were divided into
these different groups,

01:27:22.040 --> 01:27:23.915
either mindfulness
meditation where they sat,

01:27:23.915 --> 01:27:25.791
they were not told to
control their breathing

01:27:25.791 --> 01:27:26.660
in any specific way.

01:27:26.660 --> 01:27:27.618
They closed their eyes.

01:27:27.618 --> 01:27:30.275
They focused their
attention on a region

01:27:30.275 --> 01:27:31.400
just behind their forehead.

01:27:31.400 --> 01:27:32.330
One group did that.

01:27:32.330 --> 01:27:35.600
The other group
did cyclic sighing.

01:27:35.600 --> 01:27:37.280
Another group did box breathing.

01:27:37.280 --> 01:27:40.700
Another group did
cyclic hyperventilation.

01:27:40.700 --> 01:27:43.640
As any sort of clinical
trial like this ought to,

01:27:43.640 --> 01:27:45.990
we then swapped people
into different groups.

01:27:45.990 --> 01:27:47.640
So they served as
their own control.

01:27:47.640 --> 01:27:51.260
So we could evaluate
any between and within

01:27:51.260 --> 01:27:53.465
individual variability.

01:27:53.465 --> 01:27:55.340
Again, there are a lot
of data in this paper.

01:27:55.340 --> 01:27:59.780
But the takeaway was that for
the sake of stress reduction

01:27:59.780 --> 01:28:03.260
around the clock and for
the sake of improving sleep

01:28:03.260 --> 01:28:05.810
and mood, the most
effective practice

01:28:05.810 --> 01:28:07.400
of the four practices
that we examined

01:28:07.400 --> 01:28:08.750
was the cyclic sighing.

01:28:08.750 --> 01:28:11.180
Again, cyclic sighing is
performed the following way.

01:28:11.180 --> 01:28:13.250
You inhale through the
nose as deeply as you can.

01:28:13.250 --> 01:28:16.790
Then you do a second inhale
immediately afterwards to try

01:28:16.790 --> 01:28:18.500
and maximally inflate the lungs.

01:28:18.500 --> 01:28:19.730
In fact, that's what happens.

01:28:19.730 --> 01:28:22.250
We know that during
that second inhale,

01:28:22.250 --> 01:28:25.700
even if it's just a very
sharp, short inhale,

01:28:25.700 --> 01:28:28.700
the extra physical
vigor that's required

01:28:28.700 --> 01:28:32.290
to generate that
second inhale causes

01:28:32.290 --> 01:28:35.320
those alveoli of the lungs,
which may have collapsed--

01:28:35.320 --> 01:28:38.587
and, indeed, in between
breaths and often even just

01:28:38.587 --> 01:28:40.420
through the course of
the day and especially

01:28:40.420 --> 01:28:43.240
if we get stressed, those
alveoli of the lungs

01:28:43.240 --> 01:28:44.470
start to collapse.

01:28:44.470 --> 01:28:46.737
And because they're
damp on the inside--

01:28:46.737 --> 01:28:48.070
they have a little bit of fluid.

01:28:48.070 --> 01:28:49.300
They're like a balloon
with a little bit

01:28:49.300 --> 01:28:50.680
of fluid in the middle.

01:28:50.680 --> 01:28:53.110
It takes a little
bit of physical force

01:28:53.110 --> 01:28:54.025
to pop those open.

01:28:54.025 --> 01:28:55.900
Now, you're not literally
exploding them pop.

01:28:55.900 --> 01:28:58.540
But you're reinflating
them with air.

01:28:58.540 --> 01:29:03.040
And then you perform the
long exhale through the mouth

01:29:03.040 --> 01:29:04.100
until lungs are empty.

01:29:04.100 --> 01:29:05.890
So it looks exactly like this.

01:29:05.890 --> 01:29:07.858
[INHALES DEEPLY]

01:29:07.858 --> 01:29:08.842
[INHALES SHARPLY]

01:29:08.842 --> 01:29:11.794
[EXHALES]

01:29:12.780 --> 01:29:17.910
Now, we know that one
single physiological sigh

01:29:17.910 --> 01:29:20.010
of the sort that
I just described

01:29:20.010 --> 01:29:23.342
performed at any time of
day under any conditions,

01:29:23.342 --> 01:29:25.800
whether or not you're about to
walk on stage to give a talk

01:29:25.800 --> 01:29:28.110
or you're in a meeting and
you're feeling stressed,

01:29:28.110 --> 01:29:30.385
or you're in a conversation
that's very stressful,

01:29:30.385 --> 01:29:32.760
or you can feel stress mounting
because you're in traffic

01:29:32.760 --> 01:29:35.820
or any number of psychological
or physical stressors

01:29:35.820 --> 01:29:38.190
that may be approaching
you or you feel

01:29:38.190 --> 01:29:42.270
are oppressing you, doing one
physiological sigh of the sort

01:29:42.270 --> 01:29:46.950
that I just described is
the fastest physiologically

01:29:46.950 --> 01:29:50.760
verified way that we
are aware of to reduce

01:29:50.760 --> 01:29:54.630
your levels of stress and to
reintroduce calm, that is,

01:29:54.630 --> 01:29:56.610
to shift your autonomic
nervous system

01:29:56.610 --> 01:30:00.240
from a state of heightened
levels of autonomic arousal.

01:30:00.240 --> 01:30:02.560
That is, sympathetic nervous
system as, it's called,

01:30:02.560 --> 01:30:04.890
is at a higher activation
level than the so-called

01:30:04.890 --> 01:30:06.220
parasympathetic nervous system.

01:30:06.220 --> 01:30:08.220
Again, sympathetic nervous
system having nothing

01:30:08.220 --> 01:30:09.678
to do with sympathy,
has everything

01:30:09.678 --> 01:30:11.220
to do with so-called
fight or flight,

01:30:11.220 --> 01:30:12.636
although it controls
other things,

01:30:12.636 --> 01:30:14.190
too, including positive arousal.

01:30:14.190 --> 01:30:15.880
And the parasympathetic
nervous system,

01:30:15.880 --> 01:30:17.880
often referred to as the
rest and digest system,

01:30:17.880 --> 01:30:20.385
although it does
other things, too,

01:30:20.385 --> 01:30:21.510
is associated with calming.

01:30:21.510 --> 01:30:22.968
Those two things
are always in kind

01:30:22.968 --> 01:30:25.530
of push-pull with one another,
like a seesaw or push-pull,

01:30:25.530 --> 01:30:26.988
however you want
to think about it.

01:30:26.988 --> 01:30:29.970
One physiological sigh,
meaning that big, deep inhale,

01:30:29.970 --> 01:30:31.950
short second inhale
also through the nose,

01:30:31.950 --> 01:30:35.070
and then long exhale to
completely lungs empty,

01:30:35.070 --> 01:30:37.590
is known to restore
the level of balance

01:30:37.590 --> 01:30:40.380
in the
sympathetic-parasympathetic

01:30:40.380 --> 01:30:43.620
neural circuitry and
is the fastest way

01:30:43.620 --> 01:30:46.050
to reintroduce calm.

01:30:46.050 --> 01:30:47.670
That's one physiological sigh.

01:30:47.670 --> 01:30:49.710
In this study, what we
asked was that people

01:30:49.710 --> 01:30:52.620
perform that repeatedly,
so-called cyclic sighing,

01:30:52.620 --> 01:30:54.180
for the duration
of five minutes.

01:30:54.180 --> 01:30:56.610
And the people who did
that cyclic sighing

01:30:56.610 --> 01:30:59.520
for five minutes a day,
regardless of the time of day

01:30:59.520 --> 01:31:01.980
that they did it, experienced
the greatest reductions

01:31:01.980 --> 01:31:04.290
in stress not just
during the practice

01:31:04.290 --> 01:31:06.780
but around the 24-hour cycle.

01:31:06.780 --> 01:31:08.520
And it translated,
again, to all sorts

01:31:08.520 --> 01:31:12.270
of positive subjective changes--
improvements in sleep, lower

01:31:12.270 --> 01:31:14.645
resting heart rate
at all times of day.

01:31:14.645 --> 01:31:15.520
So this is important.

01:31:15.520 --> 01:31:18.060
Again, this study was
not just exploring

01:31:18.060 --> 01:31:20.250
what happens during
meditation or breathwork,

01:31:20.250 --> 01:31:21.660
cyclic sighing, et cetera.

01:31:21.660 --> 01:31:25.650
It was exploring how the changes
that occur during that practice

01:31:25.650 --> 01:31:27.750
translate to
changes in breathing

01:31:27.750 --> 01:31:30.640
and heart rate, mood, et cetera,
throughout the 24-hour cycle.

01:31:30.640 --> 01:31:32.135
So the takeaway here is twofold.

01:31:32.135 --> 01:31:33.510
First of all, if
you are somebody

01:31:33.510 --> 01:31:35.940
who wants to improve
your mood and reduce

01:31:35.940 --> 01:31:38.970
your overall levels
of stress and you only

01:31:38.970 --> 01:31:41.408
have five minutes a
day to invest in that,

01:31:41.408 --> 01:31:43.200
hopefully you're doing
all the other things

01:31:43.200 --> 01:31:45.120
like trying to get proper
sleep and exercise,

01:31:45.120 --> 01:31:47.190
social connection, nutrition,
et cetera, sunlight

01:31:47.190 --> 01:31:48.890
in the morning, of course.

01:31:48.890 --> 01:31:50.080
Can't leave that out.

01:31:50.080 --> 01:31:52.650
But if you were going to
devote five minutes a day

01:31:52.650 --> 01:31:55.890
to a stress reduction
practice that is now supported

01:31:55.890 --> 01:31:58.830
by data to translate
to reductions in stress

01:31:58.830 --> 01:32:02.110
around the clock,
the data say that you

01:32:02.110 --> 01:32:05.110
would want to invest that
in cyclic sighing, that is,

01:32:05.110 --> 01:32:07.085
double inhale through
the nose, extended exhale

01:32:07.085 --> 01:32:08.960
through the mouth until
your lungs are empty,

01:32:08.960 --> 01:32:10.570
then repeat for
five minutes a day.

01:32:10.570 --> 01:32:13.367
You, of course, if you
like, could do meditation.

01:32:13.367 --> 01:32:14.950
It still had positive
effects, meaning

01:32:14.950 --> 01:32:17.410
it reduced stress, although
not as much as cyclic sighing.

01:32:17.410 --> 01:32:18.785
You could do box
breathing if you

01:32:18.785 --> 01:32:21.520
want for the purpose
of reducing stress.

01:32:21.520 --> 01:32:24.460
All the practices we
explored did reduce stress.

01:32:24.460 --> 01:32:26.890
But cyclic sighing performed
for five minutes a day

01:32:26.890 --> 01:32:29.350
had the most robust
and pervasive effect

01:32:29.350 --> 01:32:32.200
in reducing stress, improving
mood, and improving sleep.

01:32:32.200 --> 01:32:33.830
That's the first
message of the study.

01:32:33.830 --> 01:32:37.750
The second takeaway is that
one physiological sigh--

01:32:37.750 --> 01:32:40.453
that's right just one
physiological sigh, where

01:32:40.453 --> 01:32:41.870
you inhale deeply
through the nose

01:32:41.870 --> 01:32:43.750
another inhale through the
nose to maximally inflate

01:32:43.750 --> 01:32:45.430
the alveoli of the
lungs, and then you

01:32:45.430 --> 01:32:49.600
exhale to completely lungs
empty and then go back

01:32:49.600 --> 01:32:52.120
to normal breathing,
is the fastest

01:32:52.120 --> 01:32:55.330
way to introduce a level
of calm and to reduce

01:32:55.330 --> 01:32:57.875
your overall levels of
stress in real time.

01:32:57.875 --> 01:32:59.000
And this is very important.

01:32:59.000 --> 01:33:00.590
I think that out
there these days,

01:33:00.590 --> 01:33:02.590
we hear a lot about stress
reduction techniques.

01:33:02.590 --> 01:33:05.272
And most all of the stress
reduction techniques

01:33:05.272 --> 01:33:06.730
that have been
explored, everything

01:33:06.730 --> 01:33:10.720
from massage to meditation
to breathwork to a hot shower

01:33:10.720 --> 01:33:13.120
to a foot rub,
will calm you down.

01:33:13.120 --> 01:33:14.950
The question is, do
they calm you down just

01:33:14.950 --> 01:33:16.450
during that practice?

01:33:16.450 --> 01:33:18.280
Great if it does.

01:33:18.280 --> 01:33:21.010
But does it also translate
to reduced levels

01:33:21.010 --> 01:33:23.590
of stress at other times
in the 24-hour cycle

01:33:23.590 --> 01:33:25.220
and other positive
effects as well?

01:33:25.220 --> 01:33:28.180
So one physiological sigh
is a very efficient way

01:33:28.180 --> 01:33:32.680
to adjust that ratio of
sympathetic to parasympathetic

01:33:32.680 --> 01:33:34.690
activation and immediately
bring about calm.

01:33:34.690 --> 01:33:37.212
So it's excellent for
real-time control of stress.

01:33:37.212 --> 01:33:38.920
The other thing about
physiological sighs

01:33:38.920 --> 01:33:39.940
is that it's not a hack.

01:33:39.940 --> 01:33:42.730
It's not the application of a
breathing practice to something

01:33:42.730 --> 01:33:44.110
that it wasn't intended for.

01:33:44.110 --> 01:33:46.210
In fact, physiological
sighs were not

01:33:46.210 --> 01:33:48.070
discovered by me at all.

01:33:48.070 --> 01:33:49.840
They were discovered
by physiologists

01:33:49.840 --> 01:33:54.590
in the 1930s, who found that
when people underbreathe,

01:33:54.590 --> 01:33:57.270
they have a buildup of carbon
dioxide in their system.

01:33:57.270 --> 01:33:59.780
And even though carbon
dioxide is essential for life,

01:33:59.780 --> 01:34:01.910
you don't want too much
of it in your system.

01:34:01.910 --> 01:34:05.150
And that people, whether or
not they were asleep or awake,

01:34:05.150 --> 01:34:08.540
would engage a physiological
sigh spontaneously,

01:34:08.540 --> 01:34:09.408
subconsciously.

01:34:09.408 --> 01:34:11.450
They would do this double
inhale through the nose

01:34:11.450 --> 01:34:13.430
and extended exhale
through the mouth.

01:34:13.430 --> 01:34:17.240
And that did not just eliminate
excessive carbon dioxide

01:34:17.240 --> 01:34:18.180
from the system.

01:34:18.180 --> 01:34:21.440
It also rebalanced the
oxygen-carbon dioxide ratio

01:34:21.440 --> 01:34:22.280
in the proper ways.

01:34:22.280 --> 01:34:23.960
In fact, it's
observed in animals.

01:34:23.960 --> 01:34:25.970
You might see this in
animals that are tired.

01:34:25.970 --> 01:34:27.800
When animals or
humans get tired,

01:34:27.800 --> 01:34:29.960
they tend to start
underbreathing a little bit,

01:34:29.960 --> 01:34:32.780
and that can often disrupt
the balance of carbon dioxide

01:34:32.780 --> 01:34:34.760
and oxygen. And right
before a dog will go down

01:34:34.760 --> 01:34:36.802
for a nap, for instance,
you'll notice that it'll

01:34:36.802 --> 01:34:38.150
do this double inhale, exhale.

01:34:38.150 --> 01:34:40.490
people when they are sleeping,
if they hold their breath

01:34:40.490 --> 01:34:43.010
for a period of time,
which, frankly, all of us

01:34:43.010 --> 01:34:44.720
do periodically
throughout sleep,

01:34:44.720 --> 01:34:49.040
they will engage a spontaneous
physiological sigh.

01:34:49.040 --> 01:34:51.830
During the daytime, we are often
holding our breath, especially

01:34:51.830 --> 01:34:53.430
nowadays-- and there's
a study on this

01:34:53.430 --> 01:34:55.430
that we'll talk about a
little bit later-- where

01:34:55.430 --> 01:34:57.350
when people text message
or they're emailing,

01:34:57.350 --> 01:34:59.660
although nowadays people are
mainly on social media and text

01:34:59.660 --> 01:35:01.618
messaging, they often
are holding their breath.

01:35:01.618 --> 01:35:04.383
They will follow a breath
hold by a physiological sigh

01:35:04.383 --> 01:35:06.050
because during that
breath hold, they're

01:35:06.050 --> 01:35:08.383
building up the level of
carbon dioxide in their system.

01:35:08.383 --> 01:35:10.490
Now, mind you, I spent
close to a half an hour

01:35:10.490 --> 01:35:13.100
telling you that most people
are overbreathing at rest,

01:35:13.100 --> 01:35:14.600
and that's also true.

01:35:14.600 --> 01:35:17.570
But people often will
shift from overbreathing

01:35:17.570 --> 01:35:20.750
to underbreathing, which
is a terrible pattern.

01:35:20.750 --> 01:35:24.920
So physiological sighs done
either as a one-off, one

01:35:24.920 --> 01:35:27.650
physiological sigh to clamp
stress or reduce stress

01:35:27.650 --> 01:35:30.920
in real time, or repeatedly
over five minutes as a practice

01:35:30.920 --> 01:35:34.220
that you do each day is
going to be not just the most

01:35:34.220 --> 01:35:36.380
effective way to
approach reducing stress

01:35:36.380 --> 01:35:40.280
around the clock and in real
time but also the one that's

01:35:40.280 --> 01:35:42.680
highly compatible with the
way that the neural circuits

01:35:42.680 --> 01:35:44.420
that control breathing
were designed.

01:35:44.420 --> 01:35:48.020
The physiological sigh has some
other very useful applications.

01:35:48.020 --> 01:35:51.560
One of the more, I would say,
useful ones, at least to those

01:35:51.560 --> 01:35:53.840
of you that exercise,
is going to be

01:35:53.840 --> 01:35:55.940
the use of physiological
sigh in order

01:35:55.940 --> 01:35:57.950
to remove the
so-called side stitch.

01:35:57.950 --> 01:36:00.935
So if you've ever been running
or swimming or exercising

01:36:00.935 --> 01:36:03.620
and you felt a cramp
on your right side,

01:36:03.620 --> 01:36:06.890
chances are, despite what your
high school PE coach told you,

01:36:06.890 --> 01:36:08.960
that raising your
arms above your head

01:36:08.960 --> 01:36:11.300
or drinking less water
before you exercise

01:36:11.300 --> 01:36:13.940
is not going to get
rid of that cramp.

01:36:13.940 --> 01:36:15.590
And here's why.

01:36:15.590 --> 01:36:17.180
It's not a cramp at all.

01:36:17.180 --> 01:36:22.280
If you recall the cervical
3, 4, and 5 nerves that

01:36:22.280 --> 01:36:25.160
give rise to the phrenic nerve
and go down and innervate

01:36:25.160 --> 01:36:28.190
your diaphragm, well,
as I mentioned before,

01:36:28.190 --> 01:36:29.870
a certain number of
those nerve fibers

01:36:29.870 --> 01:36:33.090
actually course into the
diaphragm and go up underneath.

01:36:33.090 --> 01:36:34.970
And if you recall
earlier, I also

01:36:34.970 --> 01:36:37.910
said that the diaphragm sits
right on top of the liver.

01:36:37.910 --> 01:36:41.060
In other words, you actually
have a sensory innervation

01:36:41.060 --> 01:36:44.720
of the diaphragm, the deep
diaphragm, and the liver.

01:36:44.720 --> 01:36:48.410
And there's something called
referenced pain, which

01:36:48.410 --> 01:36:50.000
is what people
generally experience

01:36:50.000 --> 01:36:52.460
when they have that side stitch
on their right-hand side.

01:36:52.460 --> 01:36:53.960
So if you're ever
exercising and you

01:36:53.960 --> 01:36:56.960
feel a cramp on your
right-hand side,

01:36:56.960 --> 01:36:59.300
it's possible that
it's a genuine cramp.

01:36:59.300 --> 01:37:03.050
But more likely is the fact
that that phrenic nerve

01:37:03.050 --> 01:37:07.190
sensory innervation is now
being carried up to your brain

01:37:07.190 --> 01:37:12.680
and you are detecting some local
or referenced pain in the liver

01:37:12.680 --> 01:37:14.203
and in the diaphragm.

01:37:14.203 --> 01:37:15.620
Now, that doesn't
necessarily mean

01:37:15.620 --> 01:37:17.745
you're doing anything wrong,
although you might not

01:37:17.745 --> 01:37:20.377
be breathing properly for
running at that moment,

01:37:20.377 --> 01:37:21.710
and that's what gave rise to it.

01:37:21.710 --> 01:37:24.410
It could be some spasming
of the phrenic nerve

01:37:24.410 --> 01:37:26.460
or some inefficient
breathing during running.

01:37:26.460 --> 01:37:29.480
We had an entire series on
fitness with Dr. Andy Galpin.

01:37:29.480 --> 01:37:31.850
One of those episodes
included a lot of information

01:37:31.850 --> 01:37:32.550
on breathing.

01:37:32.550 --> 01:37:35.660
It was the episode on
endurance, although breathing

01:37:35.660 --> 01:37:38.270
was a topic that was thread
through multiple episodes

01:37:38.270 --> 01:37:38.960
in that series.

01:37:38.960 --> 01:37:40.830
You can find that series
at HubermanLab.com.

01:37:40.830 --> 01:37:42.830
Talks a lot about how to
breathe during running,

01:37:42.830 --> 01:37:44.788
how to breathe during
weightlifting, et cetera.

01:37:44.788 --> 01:37:47.450
But the point for now
is that if ever you're

01:37:47.450 --> 01:37:51.050
experiencing that
right-side side stitch,

01:37:51.050 --> 01:37:53.990
I encourage you to perform
the physiological sigh.

01:37:53.990 --> 01:37:56.750
And the good news is you can
perform it while still running

01:37:56.750 --> 01:37:58.340
or while still
swimming, although I

01:37:58.340 --> 01:37:59.882
suppose with swimming,
you might have

01:37:59.882 --> 01:38:02.150
to make some adjustments
because, of course,

01:38:02.150 --> 01:38:04.880
you don't want to inhale
water, or while cycling

01:38:04.880 --> 01:38:08.450
or any type of activity.

01:38:08.450 --> 01:38:11.600
If you perform that
physiological sigh generally

01:38:11.600 --> 01:38:13.760
two or three times,
what will occur

01:38:13.760 --> 01:38:15.530
is that because of
changes in the firing

01:38:15.530 --> 01:38:17.990
of the phrenic nerve, and in
particular because of changes

01:38:17.990 --> 01:38:20.930
in the sensory feedback
from the sensory component

01:38:20.930 --> 01:38:22.970
of the phrenic nerve
back to the brain,

01:38:22.970 --> 01:38:26.180
you will experience an
alleviation of the pain

01:38:26.180 --> 01:38:28.313
from that right-side
side stitch.

01:38:28.313 --> 01:38:30.230
In other words, you can
get rid of side cramps

01:38:30.230 --> 01:38:32.480
doing physiological
sighs during activities,

01:38:32.480 --> 01:38:34.920
in particular during
running activities.

01:38:34.920 --> 01:38:37.700
Now, I should also mention that
if you're experiencing a side

01:38:37.700 --> 01:38:40.700
stitch on the
left-side, chances are

01:38:40.700 --> 01:38:46.020
that has to do with excessive
air or fluid in your stomach.

01:38:46.020 --> 01:38:47.480
And there are
reasons for that that

01:38:47.480 --> 01:38:49.813
also have to do with the way
that the phrenic nerve is--

01:38:49.813 --> 01:38:51.980
it's bilateral and
branches to both sides

01:38:51.980 --> 01:38:55.230
and is catching sensory input
on the left side from some

01:38:55.230 --> 01:38:58.680
of the local organs and sensory
innervation of those organs.

01:38:58.680 --> 01:39:00.360
But if you have
right-side side stitch,

01:39:00.360 --> 01:39:02.220
the physiological
sigh done two or three

01:39:02.220 --> 01:39:04.950
times while still running ought
to relieve that side stitch.

01:39:04.950 --> 01:39:06.870
Now, as long as we're
talking about breathing

01:39:06.870 --> 01:39:08.620
and the phrenic nerve
and the relationship

01:39:08.620 --> 01:39:12.107
between the phrenic nerve and
your liver and your stomach

01:39:12.107 --> 01:39:14.190
and some of the other
organs in that neighborhood,

01:39:14.190 --> 01:39:16.500
we should talk about the
relationship between breathing

01:39:16.500 --> 01:39:17.980
and heart rate.

01:39:17.980 --> 01:39:19.715
This is an incredibly
important topic,

01:39:19.715 --> 01:39:21.840
so much so that I perhaps
should have brought it up

01:39:21.840 --> 01:39:23.350
at the beginning of the episode.

01:39:23.350 --> 01:39:28.560
But nonetheless, you now know
what your diaphragm does.

01:39:28.560 --> 01:39:30.748
When you inhale, your
diaphragm moves down.

01:39:30.748 --> 01:39:31.290
That's right.

01:39:31.290 --> 01:39:33.490
When you contract your
diaphragm, it moves down.

01:39:33.490 --> 01:39:35.280
It creates space for
your lungs to inhale.

01:39:35.280 --> 01:39:37.620
And when you exhale,
your diaphragm moves up.

01:39:37.620 --> 01:39:42.580
Well, when you inhale and
your diaphragm moves down,

01:39:42.580 --> 01:39:45.060
what happens is there's
more space created

01:39:45.060 --> 01:39:47.582
in the thoracic cavity and
particularly if you're also

01:39:47.582 --> 01:39:50.040
breathing deeply and you're
using those intercostal muscles

01:39:50.040 --> 01:39:51.690
to expand your ribs.

01:39:51.690 --> 01:39:53.678
As a consequence,
the heart actually

01:39:53.678 --> 01:39:54.720
gets a little bit bigger.

01:39:54.720 --> 01:39:57.100
It's a temporary
enlargement in the heart.

01:39:57.100 --> 01:39:58.710
But it's a real enlargement.

01:39:58.710 --> 01:40:01.950
And as a consequence,
whatever blood is in the heart

01:40:01.950 --> 01:40:04.960
is now in a larger volume
because the heart got bigger.

01:40:04.960 --> 01:40:07.590
And as a consequence, that
blood is moving more slowly

01:40:07.590 --> 01:40:11.290
through that larger volume
for a short period of time.

01:40:11.290 --> 01:40:13.920
But nonetheless, it's
moving more slowly.

01:40:13.920 --> 01:40:17.520
Your nervous system detects
that and sends a neural signal

01:40:17.520 --> 01:40:19.470
to the heart to speed
the heart rate up.

01:40:19.470 --> 01:40:23.860
In other words, inhales
increase heart rate.

01:40:23.860 --> 01:40:26.290
The opposite is true
when you exhale.

01:40:26.290 --> 01:40:29.470
When you exhale, your
diaphragm moves up.

01:40:29.470 --> 01:40:32.050
Your rib cage tends
to move inward a bit.

01:40:32.050 --> 01:40:33.430
And you compact the heart.

01:40:33.430 --> 01:40:36.620
You reduce the volume
of the heart overall.

01:40:36.620 --> 01:40:38.660
When you reduce the volume
of the heart overall,

01:40:38.660 --> 01:40:41.330
blood flow through
the heart accelerates

01:40:41.330 --> 01:40:42.640
because it's a smaller volume.

01:40:42.640 --> 01:40:44.015
So a given unit
of blood is going

01:40:44.015 --> 01:40:45.973
to move more quickly
through that small volume.

01:40:45.973 --> 01:40:49.310
Your nervous system detects
that and sends a signal

01:40:49.310 --> 01:40:51.440
to slow the heart down.

01:40:51.440 --> 01:40:54.260
So just as inhales
speed the heart up,

01:40:54.260 --> 01:40:57.330
exhales slow your
heart rate down.

01:40:57.330 --> 01:41:00.200
Now, of course, even though
you can double up on inhales

01:41:00.200 --> 01:41:03.950
or even triple up on inhales,
sooner or later, if you inhale,

01:41:03.950 --> 01:41:05.960
you're going to have to exhale.

01:41:05.960 --> 01:41:08.130
And the converse is
also true, of course.

01:41:08.130 --> 01:41:10.830
So what does this mean in terms
of controlling your heart rate?

01:41:10.830 --> 01:41:14.540
Well, let's say you are
going in for a blood draw,

01:41:14.540 --> 01:41:16.648
or you're going out on
stage and you're stressed.

01:41:16.648 --> 01:41:19.190
Well, I would encourage you to
do a physiological sigh, maybe

01:41:19.190 --> 01:41:22.040
two physiological sighs to
bring your level of calm up

01:41:22.040 --> 01:41:23.720
and your level of stress down.

01:41:23.720 --> 01:41:25.700
Nonetheless, if
you have any reason

01:41:25.700 --> 01:41:27.740
why you want to quickly
reduce your heart rate

01:41:27.740 --> 01:41:32.250
or accelerate your heart rate
for sake of physical work

01:41:32.250 --> 01:41:36.150
output or to calm yourself
down additionally, not just use

01:41:36.150 --> 01:41:38.280
the physiological
sigh, well, then you

01:41:38.280 --> 01:41:40.650
can take advantage of this
relationship between inhales

01:41:40.650 --> 01:41:42.330
and exhales
controlling heart rate.

01:41:42.330 --> 01:41:44.550
If you want to increase
your heart rate,

01:41:44.550 --> 01:41:48.120
you can simply inhale
longer and more vigorously

01:41:48.120 --> 01:41:49.290
relative to your exhales.

01:41:49.290 --> 01:41:52.230
And if you want to
decrease your heart rate,

01:41:52.230 --> 01:41:54.360
well, then you're going to
make your exhales longer

01:41:54.360 --> 01:41:56.580
and/or more vigorous
than your inhales.

01:41:56.580 --> 01:42:00.240
In fact, this process, which
is called respiratory sinus

01:42:00.240 --> 01:42:03.460
arrhythmia, is the basis of what
we call heart rate variability.

01:42:03.460 --> 01:42:05.460
Heart rate variability
involves the vagus nerve,

01:42:05.460 --> 01:42:08.880
the 10th cranial nerve, which
is a parasympathetic nerve that

01:42:08.880 --> 01:42:10.890
is associated with
a calming aspect

01:42:10.890 --> 01:42:14.280
of the autonomic nervous system,
slowing your heart rate down

01:42:14.280 --> 01:42:15.900
by extending your exhales.

01:42:15.900 --> 01:42:19.380
And it really forms the basis
of most all breathing practices.

01:42:19.380 --> 01:42:21.018
If you look at any
breathing practices,

01:42:21.018 --> 01:42:23.310
whether or not it's Wim Hof
breathing, Tummo breathing,

01:42:23.310 --> 01:42:25.560
Kundalini breathing,
Pranayama breathing,

01:42:25.560 --> 01:42:29.520
physiological sighing, cyclic
sighing, and on and on and on,

01:42:29.520 --> 01:42:32.520
if you were to measure the
ratio of inhales to exhales

01:42:32.520 --> 01:42:35.040
and the vigor of inhales to
exhales, what you would find

01:42:35.040 --> 01:42:40.350
is that each one would create a
net increase or a net decrease

01:42:40.350 --> 01:42:44.010
in heart rate that could be very
accurately predicted by whether

01:42:44.010 --> 01:42:48.540
or not that breathing practice
emphasized inhales, emphasized

01:42:48.540 --> 01:42:53.370
exhales, or had those two
features, inhale and exhale,

01:42:53.370 --> 01:42:55.560
be of equal duration
and intensity.

01:42:55.560 --> 01:42:58.060
In fact, if you wanted to
equilibrate your heart rate,

01:42:58.060 --> 01:43:00.030
what you would do is you
would do box breathing

01:43:00.030 --> 01:43:01.620
because inhale,
hold, exhale, hold

01:43:01.620 --> 01:43:05.880
is, by definition, creating
equal duration inhales

01:43:05.880 --> 01:43:08.520
and exhales of essentially
equivalent vigor.

01:43:08.520 --> 01:43:10.092
When you do a
physiological sigh,

01:43:10.092 --> 01:43:11.550
you're doing two
big inhales, which

01:43:11.550 --> 01:43:12.570
is going the speed
your heart rate up

01:43:12.570 --> 01:43:15.030
just a little bit, but then
a long extended exhale.

01:43:15.030 --> 01:43:18.690
The exhale in the end is much
longer than the two inhales

01:43:18.690 --> 01:43:19.960
even when combined.

01:43:19.960 --> 01:43:22.980
And so you get a net decrease in
heart rate, the calming effect.

01:43:22.980 --> 01:43:26.460
And then practices such as Tummo
breathing or Wim Hof breathing

01:43:26.460 --> 01:43:29.670
or cyclic hyperventilation,
[HYPERVENTILATES] deep inhales

01:43:29.670 --> 01:43:33.090
and exhales, the inhales
are more vigorous compared

01:43:33.090 --> 01:43:35.340
to the more passive
exhales-- are

01:43:35.340 --> 01:43:37.193
going to lead to
increases in heart rate.

01:43:37.193 --> 01:43:39.360
So the relationship between
breathing and heart rate

01:43:39.360 --> 01:43:44.040
is an absolutely lockstep
one where your heart

01:43:44.040 --> 01:43:45.593
rate follows your breathing.

01:43:45.593 --> 01:43:47.010
Your heart rate
and your breathing

01:43:47.010 --> 01:43:48.968
are in an intimate
discussion with one another,

01:43:48.968 --> 01:43:52.530
but where always and
forever your inhales

01:43:52.530 --> 01:43:55.560
increase your heart rate,
your exhales decrease it.

01:43:55.560 --> 01:43:57.840
Now, this feature,
which physicians

01:43:57.840 --> 01:43:59.910
call respiratory
sinus arrhythmia,

01:43:59.910 --> 01:44:02.790
or we sometimes hear about more
often nowadays as heart rate

01:44:02.790 --> 01:44:06.150
variability, is something
that people in sport

01:44:06.150 --> 01:44:08.050
have known about for
a very long time.

01:44:08.050 --> 01:44:12.210
It's why, for instance, that
marksmen will exhale just prior

01:44:12.210 --> 01:44:13.050
to taking a shot.

01:44:13.050 --> 01:44:14.340
That's particularly
true for people

01:44:14.340 --> 01:44:16.740
that compete in the biathlon,
where they cross country ski.

01:44:16.740 --> 01:44:18.180
So their heart rate
is up, up, up, up, up.

01:44:18.180 --> 01:44:20.130
Then they'll get to the
point where they actually

01:44:20.130 --> 01:44:21.960
have to shoot a target,
and they'll exhale,

01:44:21.960 --> 01:44:24.135
and then they'll
shoot the target.

01:44:24.135 --> 01:44:26.760
This is also why, for instance,
if you want to bring your heart

01:44:26.760 --> 01:44:29.420
rate down very quickly between
rounds of martial arts,

01:44:29.420 --> 01:44:31.420
there are a number of
different ways to do that.

01:44:31.420 --> 01:44:34.920
But an extended exhale of any
kind or, frankly, any breathing

01:44:34.920 --> 01:44:36.870
practice that emphasizes
exhales is going

01:44:36.870 --> 01:44:38.748
to bring your heart rate down.

01:44:38.748 --> 01:44:41.290
This has been incorporated in
a number of different contexts,

01:44:41.290 --> 01:44:43.150
including sport, military.

01:44:43.150 --> 01:44:45.870
It's also now being incorporated
in a clinical context

01:44:45.870 --> 01:44:48.270
for people who feel a
panic attack coming on.

01:44:48.270 --> 01:44:51.540
I'm very gratified to learn that
the physiological sigh is now

01:44:51.540 --> 01:44:55.110
being explored as a tool
to prevent panic attacks

01:44:55.110 --> 01:44:56.280
and anxiety attacks.

01:44:56.280 --> 01:44:57.988
This is prior to the
panic attack, people

01:44:57.988 --> 01:44:59.572
bringing their heart
rate down, again,

01:44:59.572 --> 01:45:00.990
through those extended exhales.

01:45:00.990 --> 01:45:03.360
So learning to
extend your exhale

01:45:03.360 --> 01:45:05.730
is really a terrific
skill to master,

01:45:05.730 --> 01:45:07.980
and it's a very easy
skill to master, frankly.

01:45:07.980 --> 01:45:09.330
Why do I say a skill?

01:45:09.330 --> 01:45:11.050
Well, remember what
I said earlier,

01:45:11.050 --> 01:45:14.160
which is that humans
inhale actively

01:45:14.160 --> 01:45:17.400
and most typically
will passively exhale,

01:45:17.400 --> 01:45:20.130
just let the air [EXHALES] drop
out of them at whatever rate,

01:45:20.130 --> 01:45:23.400
depending on how much
air they inhaled.

01:45:23.400 --> 01:45:28.200
Actively exhaling, that is,
actively relaxing the diaphragm

01:45:28.200 --> 01:45:31.050
and actively relaxing
those intercostal muscles

01:45:31.050 --> 01:45:33.300
of the chest, those ones
that are, I should say,

01:45:33.300 --> 01:45:35.670
between the ribs,
is a skill that you

01:45:35.670 --> 01:45:37.350
can very quickly
acquire and will

01:45:37.350 --> 01:45:39.510
allow you to use
that relationship

01:45:39.510 --> 01:45:41.880
between the phrenic nerve,
the diaphragm, and the size

01:45:41.880 --> 01:45:43.963
of the heart, the heart
volume, and all that stuff

01:45:43.963 --> 01:45:45.840
to really take control
of heart rate quickly.

01:45:45.840 --> 01:45:48.293
So that if you feel like your
heart is racing too much--

01:45:48.293 --> 01:45:49.710
and, frankly, a
lot of people have

01:45:49.710 --> 01:45:52.077
a lot of what's called
interoceptive awareness,

01:45:52.077 --> 01:45:53.160
especially anxious people.

01:45:53.160 --> 01:45:54.420
They can really
sense what's going on

01:45:54.420 --> 01:45:56.040
in their body, other
people less so.

01:45:56.040 --> 01:45:57.230
Like, oh my god,
my heart's beating.

01:45:57.230 --> 01:45:59.430
It's ready to jump out of my
chest, and I don't like that.

01:45:59.430 --> 01:46:00.240
I don't like that.

01:46:00.240 --> 01:46:03.138
[EXHALES] Big, long exhale.

01:46:03.138 --> 01:46:05.680
It doesn't matter if you do it
through the nose or the mouth.

01:46:05.680 --> 01:46:07.263
Big, long exhale is
going to allow you

01:46:07.263 --> 01:46:10.020
to slow your heart rate down.

01:46:10.020 --> 01:46:11.610
Let's talk about hiccups.

01:46:11.610 --> 01:46:13.980
Everybody experiences
hiccups from time to time.

01:46:13.980 --> 01:46:16.140
I think most people would
agree that one hiccup is

01:46:16.140 --> 01:46:17.340
sort of funny.

01:46:17.340 --> 01:46:19.770
Two hiccups in a
row is really funny.

01:46:19.770 --> 01:46:21.840
And three hiccups
in a row is where

01:46:21.840 --> 01:46:24.060
it starts to be concerning,
in part because hiccups

01:46:24.060 --> 01:46:25.350
can be kind of painful.

01:46:25.350 --> 01:46:28.740
You can experience pain in
your gut or your lower abdomen

01:46:28.740 --> 01:46:30.428
and sometimes in
your chest as well.

01:46:30.428 --> 01:46:31.720
And it feels kind of intrusive.

01:46:31.720 --> 01:46:33.762
It gets in the way of
having conversation or just

01:46:33.762 --> 01:46:35.860
sitting there and relaxing.

01:46:35.860 --> 01:46:38.560
Fortunately, there's a simple
way to get rid of hiccups.

01:46:38.560 --> 01:46:40.900
And you can arrive at
that simple technique

01:46:40.900 --> 01:46:42.940
if you understand a
little bit about what

01:46:42.940 --> 01:46:44.140
gives rise to hiccups.

01:46:44.140 --> 01:46:45.730
The reason we get
hiccups at all is

01:46:45.730 --> 01:46:48.820
because we experience a
spasm of the phrenic nerve.

01:46:48.820 --> 01:46:50.420
The phrenic nerve,
as you recall,

01:46:50.420 --> 01:46:54.040
is a nerve that emanates
from the cervical region,

01:46:54.040 --> 01:46:56.260
to be specific C3, 4, and 5.

01:46:56.260 --> 01:46:58.630
Those spinal nerves go down,
of course, behind the heart

01:46:58.630 --> 01:47:00.047
and innervate the
diaphragm, which

01:47:00.047 --> 01:47:02.320
is the muscle that when it
contracts, it moves down

01:47:02.320 --> 01:47:03.820
and allows the lungs to fill.

01:47:03.820 --> 01:47:06.250
And then when you
relax the diaphragm,

01:47:06.250 --> 01:47:10.390
then the diaphragm moves
up, and the lungs shrink

01:47:10.390 --> 01:47:13.113
or they expel air,
so-called exhalation.

01:47:13.113 --> 01:47:15.280
Now, the phrenic nerve also
has that sensory branch.

01:47:15.280 --> 01:47:17.530
So it's not just involved
in controlling the diaphragm

01:47:17.530 --> 01:47:18.430
at the motor level.

01:47:18.430 --> 01:47:20.962
It's also sensing things
deep within the diaphragm

01:47:20.962 --> 01:47:23.170
and in the liver as well
because the liver sits right

01:47:23.170 --> 01:47:24.770
below the diaphragm.

01:47:24.770 --> 01:47:28.210
So a hiccup has that
painful sensation from time

01:47:28.210 --> 01:47:32.500
to time because there's
a rapid sensory feedback

01:47:32.500 --> 01:47:36.610
or a signal, rather, of a
sharp sensation of contraction

01:47:36.610 --> 01:47:37.738
within the diaphragm.

01:47:37.738 --> 01:47:39.280
And that's relayed
back to the brain.

01:47:39.280 --> 01:47:42.100
And you consciously perceive
that as a little bit of pain.

01:47:42.100 --> 01:47:44.550
And then, of course, the
hiccup is [HICCUPS] the hiccup,

01:47:44.550 --> 01:47:46.300
which is the spasming
of the phrenic nerve

01:47:46.300 --> 01:47:47.740
that you experience more
or less in your throat.

01:47:47.740 --> 01:47:50.073
But all this really is happening
along the phrenic nerve

01:47:50.073 --> 01:47:51.123
and toward the diaphragm.

01:47:51.123 --> 01:47:52.540
What this all means
is that if you

01:47:52.540 --> 01:47:56.532
can stop the phrenic nerve from
spasming, you can stop hiccups.

01:47:56.532 --> 01:47:58.240
There are a lot of
approaches that people

01:47:58.240 --> 01:48:01.120
have tried to take to eliminate
spasming of the phrenic nerve.

01:48:01.120 --> 01:48:03.760
You'll hear that breathing
into a bag, which

01:48:03.760 --> 01:48:08.440
is one way to reingest or
reinhale carbon dioxide that

01:48:08.440 --> 01:48:11.260
otherwise would be expelled out
into the environment, can help.

01:48:11.260 --> 01:48:13.180
That's a very indirect method.

01:48:13.180 --> 01:48:15.547
It rarely works, frankly,
because it really

01:48:15.547 --> 01:48:17.380
has to do more with
adjusting your breathing

01:48:17.380 --> 01:48:19.547
to try and adjust the
activity of the phrenic nerve.

01:48:19.547 --> 01:48:22.630
It's a really roundabout way
of trying to alleviate hiccups.

01:48:22.630 --> 01:48:24.280
Some people will
experience relief

01:48:24.280 --> 01:48:26.455
from drinking from
a glass of water

01:48:26.455 --> 01:48:27.955
from the opposite
side of the glass.

01:48:27.955 --> 01:48:29.690
So you have to tilt
over at the waist.

01:48:29.690 --> 01:48:31.150
It's a kind of messy approach.

01:48:31.150 --> 01:48:33.638
Again, it doesn't tend to
work a lot of the time.

01:48:33.638 --> 01:48:35.180
For some people, it
works every time.

01:48:35.180 --> 01:48:37.550
But for most people,
it doesn't work at all.

01:48:37.550 --> 01:48:39.400
However, there is
a technique that

01:48:39.400 --> 01:48:41.890
can reliably eliminate hiccups.

01:48:41.890 --> 01:48:45.490
And it's a technique that takes
advantage of hypercontracting

01:48:45.490 --> 01:48:48.860
the phrenic nerve over
a short period of time

01:48:48.860 --> 01:48:53.320
so that it then subsequently
relaxes or alleviates

01:48:53.320 --> 01:48:55.000
the spasming of
the phrenic nerve.

01:48:55.000 --> 01:48:59.257
And that simple method is to
inhale three times in a row.

01:48:59.257 --> 01:49:01.090
This is a very unusual
pattern of breathing.

01:49:01.090 --> 01:49:03.580
But what it involves is
taking a big, deep inhale

01:49:03.580 --> 01:49:04.630
through your nose.

01:49:04.630 --> 01:49:07.732
Then before you exhale any
air, take a second inhale

01:49:07.732 --> 01:49:09.940
through the nose, however
brief that inhale might be,

01:49:09.940 --> 01:49:14.650
and then a third even micro
or millisecond long inhale

01:49:14.650 --> 01:49:16.630
through your nose to
get that third inhale.

01:49:16.630 --> 01:49:19.510
And then hold your breath
for about 15 to 20 seconds,

01:49:19.510 --> 01:49:20.920
and then slowly exhale.

01:49:20.920 --> 01:49:22.960
So even though I'm not
experiencing any hiccups

01:49:22.960 --> 01:49:23.620
right now.

01:49:23.620 --> 01:49:25.540
I will demonstrate the method
for eliminating hiccups

01:49:25.540 --> 01:49:27.248
so that you're all
clear on how to do it.

01:49:27.248 --> 01:49:28.581
OK, here I go.

01:49:28.581 --> 01:49:30.545
[INHALES DEEPLY]

01:49:31.527 --> 01:49:33.491
[INHALES]

01:49:33.491 --> 01:49:34.473
[INHALES]

01:49:44.784 --> 01:49:47.730
[EXHALES]

01:49:50.720 --> 01:49:53.420
OK, so it's three inhales
all through the nose.

01:49:53.420 --> 01:49:56.780
And it is true that that
second and third inhale

01:49:56.780 --> 01:49:59.210
takes some physical
effort to really get

01:49:59.210 --> 01:50:03.572
additional air into the
lungs without exhaling first.

01:50:03.572 --> 01:50:05.780
It feels like-- the only
way I can describe it really

01:50:05.780 --> 01:50:08.600
is as a sharp second
and third inhale

01:50:08.600 --> 01:50:10.247
because you really
have to engage

01:50:10.247 --> 01:50:12.080
the musculature of those
intercostal muscles

01:50:12.080 --> 01:50:13.710
and the diaphragm
in order to do it.

01:50:13.710 --> 01:50:16.520
And then that long exhale can be
through the nose or the mouth.

01:50:16.520 --> 01:50:19.700
But I find it particularly
relaxing or even pleasant to do

01:50:19.700 --> 01:50:21.290
it through the nose.

01:50:21.290 --> 01:50:23.930
This method of three inhales
through the nose followed

01:50:23.930 --> 01:50:25.880
by a long exhale through
the nose or mouth

01:50:25.880 --> 01:50:28.880
will eliminate hiccups right
away because what it does

01:50:28.880 --> 01:50:31.217
is it hyperexcites the
phrenic nerve three

01:50:31.217 --> 01:50:33.800
times in a row, a very unnatural
pattern for the phrenic nerve

01:50:33.800 --> 01:50:34.610
to fire.

01:50:34.610 --> 01:50:38.210
And then it undergoes
a hyperpolarization,

01:50:38.210 --> 01:50:41.120
as we call it, in which
the phrenic nerve actually

01:50:41.120 --> 01:50:45.680
stands a much lower probability
of getting activated again

01:50:45.680 --> 01:50:47.138
for some period of
time afterwards.

01:50:47.138 --> 01:50:48.888
So it is important
that you try and return

01:50:48.888 --> 01:50:51.620
to normal cadence of breathing
after doing this three inhales

01:50:51.620 --> 01:50:52.820
followed by a long exhale.

01:50:52.820 --> 01:50:54.860
If you need to perform
it a second time in order

01:50:54.860 --> 01:50:57.570
to eliminate hiccups because
they're simply not going away,

01:50:57.570 --> 01:50:58.320
that's fine.

01:50:58.320 --> 01:50:59.130
You can do that.

01:50:59.130 --> 01:51:02.030
But as far as we
know, this is the most

01:51:02.030 --> 01:51:05.672
efficient and science-supported
way to eliminate hiccups.

01:51:05.672 --> 01:51:07.130
Now, up until now
I've been talking

01:51:07.130 --> 01:51:09.380
about breathing techniques,
and I've mainly focused

01:51:09.380 --> 01:51:11.490
on breathing techniques
that emphasize the exhale,

01:51:11.490 --> 01:51:13.490
whether or not it's the
carbon dioxide tolerance

01:51:13.490 --> 01:51:16.855
test, whether or not
it's cyclic sighing

01:51:16.855 --> 01:51:18.980
or the physiological sigh
that you use in real time

01:51:18.980 --> 01:51:20.693
to reduce stress.

01:51:20.693 --> 01:51:22.610
One thing that we haven't
talked about so much

01:51:22.610 --> 01:51:24.590
is cyclic hyperventilation.

01:51:24.590 --> 01:51:26.570
Cyclic hyperventilation,
as you recall,

01:51:26.570 --> 01:51:30.173
is a bout of 25 or
so breaths inhaling

01:51:30.173 --> 01:51:32.840
deeply through the nose and then
passively exhaling or sometimes

01:51:32.840 --> 01:51:34.860
actively exhaling,
typically through the mouth.

01:51:34.860 --> 01:51:35.985
So it might look like this.

01:51:35.985 --> 01:51:40.430
[HYPERVENTILATES] That's a very
active inhale through the nose

01:51:40.430 --> 01:51:41.760
and exhale through the mouth.

01:51:41.760 --> 01:51:43.820
It can also be
done active inhale

01:51:43.820 --> 01:51:46.520
through the nose, passive exhale
through the mouth, like so.

01:51:46.520 --> 01:51:49.610
[HYPERVENTILATING]

01:51:49.610 --> 01:51:52.250
In any event, that pattern
of breathing repeated

01:51:52.250 --> 01:51:54.650
for 10 to 25 breaths
greatly increases

01:51:54.650 --> 01:51:55.940
levels of autonomic arousal.

01:51:55.940 --> 01:51:59.720
In fact, it's known to deploy
adrenaline from the adrenals.

01:51:59.720 --> 01:52:04.730
And in our study, we had people
then expel all their air,

01:52:04.730 --> 01:52:07.625
so breathe out, hold their
breath for 15 to 30 seconds,

01:52:07.625 --> 01:52:09.500
and then repeat for a
period of five minutes.

01:52:09.500 --> 01:52:11.990
That did lead to some very
interesting and positive

01:52:11.990 --> 01:52:14.840
physiological changes in
terms of stress mitigation,

01:52:14.840 --> 01:52:16.520
although not as
significant as was

01:52:16.520 --> 01:52:19.770
observed with cyclic sighing,
as I talked about earlier.

01:52:19.770 --> 01:52:23.090
Now, there is a lot of interest
in cyclic hyperventilation

01:52:23.090 --> 01:52:26.080
for sake of, for instance,
extending breath holds.

01:52:26.080 --> 01:52:28.580
This has become popular in part
because of the so-called Wim

01:52:28.580 --> 01:52:30.110
Hof method, which
is a method that

01:52:30.110 --> 01:52:33.500
combines breathing,
cyclic hyperventilation,

01:52:33.500 --> 01:52:36.770
followed by lungs full or
lungs empty breath holds,

01:52:36.770 --> 01:52:39.440
depending on which variant
of the Wim Hof method one

01:52:39.440 --> 01:52:40.040
is using.

01:52:40.040 --> 01:52:42.800
Separately-- and I really
want to emphasize separately--

01:52:42.800 --> 01:52:44.763
the Wim Hof method also
involves deliberate

01:52:44.763 --> 01:52:47.180
cold exposure, which, as all
of you know, I'm a big fan of

01:52:47.180 --> 01:52:48.990
and we've done episodes
of this podcast on.

01:52:48.990 --> 01:52:50.990
And we have toolkits on
deliberate cold exposure

01:52:50.990 --> 01:52:53.540
for increasing dopamine
levels, epinephrine levels,

01:52:53.540 --> 01:52:55.400
immune system
function, et cetera.

01:52:55.400 --> 01:52:57.050
Wim Hof method also
incorporates that.

01:52:57.050 --> 01:52:58.700
And it has a
mindfulness component.

01:52:58.700 --> 01:53:01.190
I do want to caution
people that any time you're

01:53:01.190 --> 01:53:03.110
doing cyclic
hyperventilation, you

01:53:03.110 --> 01:53:06.620
want to be very cautious about
not doing it in or near water

01:53:06.620 --> 01:53:09.620
because it does greatly increase
the risk of shallow water

01:53:09.620 --> 01:53:10.370
blackout.

01:53:10.370 --> 01:53:13.070
And that's because when you
do cyclic hyperventilation,

01:53:13.070 --> 01:53:15.920
you are expelling, you're
exhaling more carbon dioxide

01:53:15.920 --> 01:53:17.010
than usual.

01:53:17.010 --> 01:53:20.840
And what I haven't told you yet
is that the trigger to breathe

01:53:20.840 --> 01:53:24.470
is actually an increase
in carbon dioxide.

01:53:24.470 --> 01:53:26.360
What I mean by that is
you have a small set

01:53:26.360 --> 01:53:28.400
of neurons in your
brainstem that

01:53:28.400 --> 01:53:30.890
can detect when carbon dioxide
levels in your bloodstream

01:53:30.890 --> 01:53:31.950
reach a certain level.

01:53:31.950 --> 01:53:35.090
And when they reach that level,
they trigger the gasp reflex

01:53:35.090 --> 01:53:37.760
and/or the hunger for breathing.

01:53:37.760 --> 01:53:40.790
In other words, we don't
breathe because we crave oxygen,

01:53:40.790 --> 01:53:43.945
although we do need oxygen,
of course, in order to survive

01:53:43.945 --> 01:53:46.070
and for our brain to function
and our bodily organs

01:53:46.070 --> 01:53:46.790
to function.

01:53:46.790 --> 01:53:51.803
But our brain is wired such that
it has a threat sensor, which

01:53:51.803 --> 01:53:53.720
is carbon dioxide levels
are getting too high,

01:53:53.720 --> 01:53:57.560
and that's what triggers
the motor reflex to breathe

01:53:57.560 --> 01:53:59.570
and to, in some
cases, gasp for air,

01:53:59.570 --> 01:54:02.060
depending on how
starved for air we are.

01:54:02.060 --> 01:54:04.190
So if you do cyclic
hyperventilation,

01:54:04.190 --> 01:54:05.750
whether or not
it's Wim Hof method

01:54:05.750 --> 01:54:07.717
or whether or not
it's Tummo method--

01:54:07.717 --> 01:54:09.050
again, these things are similar.

01:54:09.050 --> 01:54:10.230
They're not exactly the same.

01:54:10.230 --> 01:54:11.605
There are other
breathing methods

01:54:11.605 --> 01:54:13.313
that incorporate cyclic
hyperventilation.

01:54:13.313 --> 01:54:14.772
What you're doing
is you're getting

01:54:14.772 --> 01:54:17.270
rid of a lot of carbon
dioxide, and therefore, you're

01:54:17.270 --> 01:54:19.407
removing the impulse
or lowering the impulse

01:54:19.407 --> 01:54:21.740
to breathe so that when you
enter that breath hold phase

01:54:21.740 --> 01:54:24.950
after the hyperventilation, it's
a much longer period of time

01:54:24.950 --> 01:54:27.440
before you feel the
anxiety and the hunger

01:54:27.440 --> 01:54:28.790
and the impulse to breathe.

01:54:28.790 --> 01:54:31.490
That's one of the real
benefits of any technique that

01:54:31.490 --> 01:54:33.500
incorporates cyclic
hyperventilation,

01:54:33.500 --> 01:54:37.340
is that rather than reduce
your stress level in real time,

01:54:37.340 --> 01:54:38.678
it actually does the opposite.

01:54:38.678 --> 01:54:39.970
It increases your stress level.

01:54:39.970 --> 01:54:42.470
It increases your levels
of autonomic arousal.

01:54:42.470 --> 01:54:44.210
But you're doing
it deliberately.

01:54:44.210 --> 01:54:46.520
And then during those breath
holds, what's happening

01:54:46.520 --> 01:54:48.920
is you have a lot of
adrenaline circulating

01:54:48.920 --> 01:54:50.840
in your system
because of the way

01:54:50.840 --> 01:54:53.720
that hyperventilation triggers
the release of adrenaline

01:54:53.720 --> 01:54:54.830
from your adrenal glands.

01:54:54.830 --> 01:54:56.760
It also triggers the
release of epinephrine,

01:54:56.760 --> 01:54:58.933
which is the same as
adrenaline, from a little brain

01:54:58.933 --> 01:55:00.350
area called locus
coeruleus, which

01:55:00.350 --> 01:55:01.790
makes you feel more alert.

01:55:01.790 --> 01:55:03.800
And then during
those breath holds

01:55:03.800 --> 01:55:07.010
and in the subsequent rounds
of cyclic hyperventilation,

01:55:07.010 --> 01:55:10.130
people experience what it is
to have a lot of adrenaline

01:55:10.130 --> 01:55:10.850
in their system.

01:55:10.850 --> 01:55:12.800
But they are
controlling the release

01:55:12.800 --> 01:55:16.130
of that adrenaline, which is
far and away different than when

01:55:16.130 --> 01:55:18.380
life events are triggering
that adrenaline.

01:55:18.380 --> 01:55:21.320
So what it really is is a
form of self-induced stress

01:55:21.320 --> 01:55:22.100
inoculation.

01:55:22.100 --> 01:55:23.510
And I do think
there are benefits

01:55:23.510 --> 01:55:26.060
to practicing cyclic
hyperventilation because it

01:55:26.060 --> 01:55:29.060
does allow you to learn how
to self-deploy adrenaline

01:55:29.060 --> 01:55:30.873
and epinephrine
from locus coeruleus

01:55:30.873 --> 01:55:31.790
and from the adrenals.

01:55:31.790 --> 01:55:33.650
Or I got that
backwards-- adrenaline

01:55:33.650 --> 01:55:35.270
from your adrenals
and epinephrine

01:55:35.270 --> 01:55:36.710
from locus coeruleus.

01:55:36.710 --> 01:55:38.870
And it allows you
to explore what

01:55:38.870 --> 01:55:42.350
it is to maintain calm
state of mind and body

01:55:42.350 --> 01:55:43.850
when you have a
lot of adrenaline

01:55:43.850 --> 01:55:46.640
in your system, which
certain studies are starting

01:55:46.640 --> 01:55:49.880
to show can allow people
to be able to lean

01:55:49.880 --> 01:55:51.710
into the stressful
aspects of life.

01:55:51.710 --> 01:55:54.440
And let's be honest, life
is stressful in any event.

01:55:54.440 --> 01:55:56.150
And we're all going
to experience stress

01:55:56.150 --> 01:55:57.420
at some point or another.

01:55:57.420 --> 01:55:59.570
And when we do, we
want to make sure

01:55:59.570 --> 01:56:02.330
that we're not overtaken by
the release of adrenaline

01:56:02.330 --> 01:56:05.030
from the adrenals, that
sudden surge of epinephrine

01:56:05.030 --> 01:56:06.080
from locus coeruleus.

01:56:06.080 --> 01:56:08.780
So doing cyclic hyperventilation
maybe one or two

01:56:08.780 --> 01:56:12.380
times per week-- again,
25 breaths, active inhale,

01:56:12.380 --> 01:56:14.300
passive or active exhale.

01:56:14.300 --> 01:56:17.210
Do expect to feel tingly because
of that reduction in carbon

01:56:17.210 --> 01:56:18.980
dioxide from exhaling so much.

01:56:18.980 --> 01:56:21.380
Do expect to feel a
little bit agitated.

01:56:21.380 --> 01:56:22.880
Be very careful
doing this if you're

01:56:22.880 --> 01:56:24.950
somebody who has anxiety
attacks or somebody who

01:56:24.950 --> 01:56:27.113
has panic attacks or
disorders of any kind.

01:56:27.113 --> 01:56:29.030
But if you don't and you
want to explore this,

01:56:29.030 --> 01:56:31.760
you'll notice you start
to feel really ramped up.

01:56:31.760 --> 01:56:33.290
And then during
the breath holds,

01:56:33.290 --> 01:56:36.860
which, again, can be done
by exhaling and stopping

01:56:36.860 --> 01:56:40.010
for some period of time,
15, maybe even 60 seconds,

01:56:40.010 --> 01:56:41.570
is a time in which
you can explore

01:56:41.570 --> 01:56:43.610
how to remain mentally calm.

01:56:43.610 --> 01:56:45.620
Some people even choose
to do math problems

01:56:45.620 --> 01:56:47.630
or think of things in a
kind of structured way

01:56:47.630 --> 01:56:51.560
while they have a lot of these
hormone neurotransmitters

01:56:51.560 --> 01:56:55.080
circulating at high levels in
their system, in other words,

01:56:55.080 --> 01:56:59.150
as a way to learn to manage your
mind and body under conditions

01:56:59.150 --> 01:56:59.897
of stress.

01:56:59.897 --> 01:57:02.480
Now, if you are somebody who's
using deliberate cold exposure,

01:57:02.480 --> 01:57:06.660
either cold showers or ice
baths or cold immersion,

01:57:06.660 --> 01:57:09.690
I often get asked
how best to breathe

01:57:09.690 --> 01:57:12.210
during those different
types of activities.

01:57:12.210 --> 01:57:14.010
Really, there's no
best way to breathe.

01:57:14.010 --> 01:57:16.740
Although if you wanted
to turn those activities

01:57:16.740 --> 01:57:19.170
into their own form
of stress inoculation,

01:57:19.170 --> 01:57:21.990
again, please don't use
cyclic hyperventilation.

01:57:21.990 --> 01:57:22.710
That's dangerous.

01:57:22.710 --> 01:57:24.640
I don't recommend it whatsoever.

01:57:24.640 --> 01:57:29.070
But you can try to actively
slow your breathing, that is,

01:57:29.070 --> 01:57:31.920
to make sure that you're
engaging in rhythmic breathing.

01:57:31.920 --> 01:57:34.530
Now, up until now I've said
that rhythmic breathing is

01:57:34.530 --> 01:57:36.450
the default.
Pre-Botzinger nucleus

01:57:36.450 --> 01:57:38.610
controlling rhythmic
breathing is the default

01:57:38.610 --> 01:57:40.800
and that doubling up
on inhales and exhales

01:57:40.800 --> 01:57:44.460
is something that happens when
you deliberately take over

01:57:44.460 --> 01:57:46.620
the action of
pre-Botzinger complex.

01:57:46.620 --> 01:57:49.750
Now, that's true
99% of the time.

01:57:49.750 --> 01:57:51.340
However, there are
certain conditions,

01:57:51.340 --> 01:57:54.960
such as conditions of heightened
state of emotional arousal--

01:57:54.960 --> 01:57:56.923
if you think about
somebody who's been crying,

01:57:56.923 --> 01:57:58.590
oftentimes they'll
do the double inhale,

01:57:58.590 --> 01:58:01.500
exhale [INHALES SHAKILY]
or triple inhales.

01:58:01.500 --> 01:58:04.620
Or if somebody is very, very
afraid, it's all inhales.

01:58:04.620 --> 01:58:06.840
So it does sometimes
happen spontaneously.

01:58:06.840 --> 01:58:09.300
Actually, when we get
into very cold water,

01:58:09.300 --> 01:58:12.743
there's a very robust
decrease in the activation

01:58:12.743 --> 01:58:14.160
of the prefrontal
cortex, which is

01:58:14.160 --> 01:58:17.040
the area of brain real estate
right behind the forehead

01:58:17.040 --> 01:58:19.380
that controls structured
thinking, your ability

01:58:19.380 --> 01:58:22.073
to reason and make sense
of what's going on.

01:58:22.073 --> 01:58:23.490
If you get into
really cold water,

01:58:23.490 --> 01:58:25.980
you should not expect that
brain region to work or at least

01:58:25.980 --> 01:58:28.920
not work very well at all for
the first 20 or 30 seconds

01:58:28.920 --> 01:58:30.600
that you're in the cold water.

01:58:30.600 --> 01:58:33.030
From the time you
get into cold water,

01:58:33.030 --> 01:58:35.550
because here we're talking
about deliberate cold exposure,

01:58:35.550 --> 01:58:38.310
I encourage you to try
and control your breathing

01:58:38.310 --> 01:58:41.550
and make it rhythmic, that is,
inhales follow exhales follow

01:58:41.550 --> 01:58:43.080
inhales follow
exhales, even if they

01:58:43.080 --> 01:58:45.300
have to be fast inhale
exhale, inhale, exhale.

01:58:45.300 --> 01:58:45.900
Why?

01:58:45.900 --> 01:58:49.948
Because the default when we get
into a stressful circumstance,

01:58:49.948 --> 01:58:51.990
emotionally or physically
stressful circumstance,

01:58:51.990 --> 01:58:56.280
is that rhythmic breathing stops
and that parafacial nucleus

01:58:56.280 --> 01:58:58.080
takes over and it's
[INHALES RAPIDLY],,

01:58:58.080 --> 01:59:00.240
and it's that kind
of panicky mode.

01:59:00.240 --> 01:59:03.270
And by simply controlling
our breath, again,

01:59:03.270 --> 01:59:06.763
even if it's fast
from inhale to exhale

01:59:06.763 --> 01:59:09.180
and making sure that we're
alternating inhales and exhales

01:59:09.180 --> 01:59:10.410
rhythmically-- and
what you'll find

01:59:10.410 --> 01:59:11.827
is that you'll be
able to navigate

01:59:11.827 --> 01:59:16.350
that what would otherwise be
a very stressful circumstance

01:59:16.350 --> 01:59:18.960
and make it less stressful
or maybe even pleasant.

01:59:18.960 --> 01:59:21.600
And that skill
definitely translates

01:59:21.600 --> 01:59:23.208
to other aspects
of life in which

01:59:23.208 --> 01:59:25.500
you're hit square in the face
with something stressful.

01:59:25.500 --> 01:59:27.930
You'll notice your breathing
and your pattern of breathing

01:59:27.930 --> 01:59:32.520
switching to multiple
inhales or breath holding,

01:59:32.520 --> 01:59:34.500
essentially departing
from rhythmic breathing.

01:59:34.500 --> 01:59:36.458
And by quickly returning
to rhythmic breathing

01:59:36.458 --> 01:59:38.250
and maybe even trying
to slow the breathing

01:59:38.250 --> 01:59:40.350
and extend those exhales,
you'll find that you

01:59:40.350 --> 01:59:42.510
can very quickly calm down.

01:59:42.510 --> 01:59:45.060
Next, I'd like to discuss what
I find to be an absolutely

01:59:45.060 --> 01:59:46.170
fascinating topic.

01:59:46.170 --> 01:59:49.290
It's also one that's highly
useful in the world, which

01:59:49.290 --> 01:59:51.810
is how your specific
patterns of breathing

01:59:51.810 --> 01:59:55.110
relate to your ability to learn
and to remember information,

01:59:55.110 --> 01:59:58.230
how it can modulate fear,
and a number of other aspects

01:59:58.230 --> 02:00:00.340
of how your brain functions.

02:00:00.340 --> 02:00:02.820
This is a literature that's
been reviewed recently

02:00:02.820 --> 02:00:06.240
in a lot of exquisite
detail in a beautiful review

02:00:06.240 --> 02:00:08.170
by Jack Feldman, who
I mentioned earlier,

02:00:08.170 --> 02:00:11.520
one of the pioneers of the
neuroscience of breathing.

02:00:11.520 --> 02:00:14.460
The title of the review is
"Breathing Rhythm and Pattern

02:00:14.460 --> 02:00:16.140
and Their Influence on Emotion."

02:00:16.140 --> 02:00:19.020
Again we'll, provide a link
to this review in the show

02:00:19.020 --> 02:00:21.300
note captions.

02:00:21.300 --> 02:00:24.570
This review includes discussion
of several studies, one

02:00:24.570 --> 02:00:28.380
in particular that I'll get
into in a bit of detail,

02:00:28.380 --> 02:00:31.020
that describes the following.

02:00:31.020 --> 02:00:33.990
Right now, I just want
you to breathe regularly,

02:00:33.990 --> 02:00:35.190
meaning rhythmically.

02:00:35.190 --> 02:00:37.140
You can inhale and
exhale through your mouth

02:00:37.140 --> 02:00:37.710
or through your nose.

02:00:37.710 --> 02:00:39.502
I'd prefer that you do
it through your nose

02:00:39.502 --> 02:00:41.520
because nasal
breathing, unless you

02:00:41.520 --> 02:00:44.040
need to breathe through your
mouth because of hard exercise

02:00:44.040 --> 02:00:46.260
or eating or talking,
is always going

02:00:46.260 --> 02:00:47.670
to be the better way to go.

02:00:47.670 --> 02:00:50.280
Nasal breathing improves
the aesthetic of your face.

02:00:50.280 --> 02:00:51.030
That's been shown.

02:00:51.030 --> 02:00:53.197
We'll talk about that just
briefly in a few minutes.

02:00:53.197 --> 02:00:54.840
Nasal breathing
improves the amount

02:00:54.840 --> 02:00:56.250
of oxygen you can
bring into your system,

02:00:56.250 --> 02:00:57.125
et cetera, et cetera.

02:00:57.125 --> 02:00:58.080
OK, so just breathe.

02:00:58.080 --> 02:00:59.790
Inhale, exhale, inhale, exhale.

02:00:59.790 --> 02:01:03.960
And know that
during your exhales,

02:01:03.960 --> 02:01:09.210
your pupil, that is, the pupil
of your eye, is getting bigger.

02:01:09.210 --> 02:01:13.030
And as you exhale,
it's getting smaller.

02:01:13.030 --> 02:01:17.040
In addition, when you inhale,
your reaction time to anything

02:01:17.040 --> 02:01:19.605
that happens around you-- a
car swerving in front of you,

02:01:19.605 --> 02:01:22.230
something that you might detect
in the periphery of your vision

02:01:22.230 --> 02:01:23.940
or hear off in the distance--

02:01:23.940 --> 02:01:27.150
increases significantly compared
to when you're exhaling.

02:01:27.150 --> 02:01:30.420
In addition, when
you are inhaling,

02:01:30.420 --> 02:01:33.120
your ability to remember
things, especially

02:01:33.120 --> 02:01:35.700
things that take a bit
of effort to remember,

02:01:35.700 --> 02:01:38.460
and your ability to
learn new information

02:01:38.460 --> 02:01:42.167
is significantly greater than
it is when you're exhaling.

02:01:42.167 --> 02:01:44.500
Now, as you hear all that,
you're probably thinking, OK,

02:01:44.500 --> 02:01:45.510
how do I just inhale?

02:01:45.510 --> 02:01:47.927
Well, of course, that's not
going to be the best approach.

02:01:47.927 --> 02:01:50.670
You need to exhale as well
for all the reasons you now

02:01:50.670 --> 02:01:52.110
are well aware of.

02:01:52.110 --> 02:01:54.090
But what these findings
really illustrate--

02:01:54.090 --> 02:01:55.548
and I should mention
these findings

02:01:55.548 --> 02:01:57.120
are all carried out in humans.

02:01:57.120 --> 02:01:59.400
So these relate to some
stuff in animal studies.

02:01:59.400 --> 02:02:03.060
But what I just described has
been shown in human studies

02:02:03.060 --> 02:02:04.980
consistently.

02:02:04.980 --> 02:02:06.660
When we inhale
and, in particular,

02:02:06.660 --> 02:02:09.450
when we inhale through
our nose, our brain

02:02:09.450 --> 02:02:12.918
is not functioning in the
same way as when we exhale.

02:02:12.918 --> 02:02:14.460
Now, that doesn't
mean that our brain

02:02:14.460 --> 02:02:17.760
is functioning in a
deficient way when we exhale.

02:02:17.760 --> 02:02:20.640
It just doesn't function
as well as it relates

02:02:20.640 --> 02:02:23.280
to memory retrieval,
memory formation,

02:02:23.280 --> 02:02:25.690
and some other
aspects of cognition.

02:02:25.690 --> 02:02:28.620
Now, you might be asking, why
in the world would this be?

02:02:28.620 --> 02:02:32.180
Well, I wasn't consulted
at the design phase,

02:02:32.180 --> 02:02:34.190
and anyone that tells
you that they were you

02:02:34.190 --> 02:02:35.940
should back away from quickly.

02:02:35.940 --> 02:02:38.980
But one reasonable explanation
for why our brain functions

02:02:38.980 --> 02:02:41.480
better, at least in the context
of what I just talked about,

02:02:41.480 --> 02:02:47.420
when we inhale is because the
olfactory system is actually

02:02:47.420 --> 02:02:51.190
the most ancient sensory system
of all the sensory systems we

02:02:51.190 --> 02:02:51.690
have.

02:02:51.690 --> 02:02:54.860
So before vision, before
audition, before touch,

02:02:54.860 --> 02:02:56.930
before all of that,
the olfactory system

02:02:56.930 --> 02:02:58.790
is the most ancient system.

02:02:58.790 --> 02:03:00.750
And the olfactory
system, of course,

02:03:00.750 --> 02:03:03.270
is designed to detect
chemicals in the environment.

02:03:03.270 --> 02:03:06.290
And so if you imagine
an early organism

02:03:06.290 --> 02:03:08.690
that perhaps we evolved
from or perhaps we

02:03:08.690 --> 02:03:10.370
didn't but nonetheless
that we share

02:03:10.370 --> 02:03:14.570
some features of, at least in
terms of olfactory function,

02:03:14.570 --> 02:03:18.110
in order to get that chemical
information into the brain,

02:03:18.110 --> 02:03:19.057
you need to inhale.

02:03:19.057 --> 02:03:20.640
You need to bring
that information in.

02:03:20.640 --> 02:03:23.223
Now, for aquatic animals, they
could take it in through water.

02:03:23.223 --> 02:03:27.900
But for animals that are
terrestrial that live on land,

02:03:27.900 --> 02:03:29.650
they would have to get
it through the air.

02:03:29.650 --> 02:03:33.532
So inhalation, we know,
activates certain regions

02:03:33.532 --> 02:03:34.990
of the so-called
piriformis cortex.

02:03:34.990 --> 02:03:37.570
These are areas of the
neocortex that are more ancient,

02:03:37.570 --> 02:03:40.510
as well as increasing
the activity of brain

02:03:40.510 --> 02:03:43.180
areas such as the hippocampus,
which is a brain area involved

02:03:43.180 --> 02:03:44.300
in learning and memory.

02:03:44.300 --> 02:03:46.550
In fact, one of the studies
that illustrates this most

02:03:46.550 --> 02:03:49.030
beautifully is a study
that was published

02:03:49.030 --> 02:03:51.052
in The Journal of
Neuroscience in 2016.

02:03:51.052 --> 02:03:52.510
By the way, Journal
of Neuroscience

02:03:52.510 --> 02:03:54.100
is a very fine journal.

02:03:54.100 --> 02:03:56.980
And the title of this paper
is "Nasal Respiration Entrains

02:03:56.980 --> 02:04:00.610
Human Limbic Oscillations and
Modulates Cognitive Function."

02:04:00.610 --> 02:04:03.730
This is a paper that followed
up on an earlier paper that

02:04:03.730 --> 02:04:07.420
showed that when people
breathe in through their nose,

02:04:07.420 --> 02:04:10.180
their recognition and
their discrimination

02:04:10.180 --> 02:04:13.840
of different odors was far
greater than when they breathe

02:04:13.840 --> 02:04:14.985
in through their mouth.

02:04:14.985 --> 02:04:16.360
Now, that result
was interesting,

02:04:16.360 --> 02:04:18.788
but it was also sort of a
duh because you smell things

02:04:18.788 --> 02:04:20.080
with your nose, not your mouth.

02:04:20.080 --> 02:04:21.160
You taste things
with your mouth,

02:04:21.160 --> 02:04:22.240
and you speak with
your mouth, and there

02:04:22.240 --> 02:04:24.448
are bunch of other things
you can do with your mouth.

02:04:24.448 --> 02:04:28.510
But nonetheless, that
study pointed to the idea

02:04:28.510 --> 02:04:32.710
that the brain is different
during nasal inhalations

02:04:32.710 --> 02:04:37.210
versus nasal
exhalations versus mouth

02:04:37.210 --> 02:04:39.010
inhalations versus exhalations.

02:04:39.010 --> 02:04:41.350
What it basically showed
is that the brain ramps up

02:04:41.350 --> 02:04:44.050
its levels of activity,
and that signal to noise

02:04:44.050 --> 02:04:45.910
that we talked about
earlier, if you recall,

02:04:45.910 --> 02:04:48.190
that ability for the
brain to detect things

02:04:48.190 --> 02:04:52.750
in the environment, is
increased during inhalations.

02:04:52.750 --> 02:04:56.500
But because that earlier study
focused on smell, on olfaction,

02:04:56.500 --> 02:04:58.000
there was a bit of
a confound there.

02:04:58.000 --> 02:04:59.630
It was hard to separate
out the variables.

02:04:59.630 --> 02:05:01.990
So this paper, the one I just
mentioned, "Nasal Respiration

02:05:01.990 --> 02:05:04.157
Entrains Human and Limbic
Oscillations and Modulates

02:05:04.157 --> 02:05:08.410
Cognitive Function," did not
look at detection of odors.

02:05:08.410 --> 02:05:12.100
Rather, it looked at things
like reaction time or fear.

02:05:12.100 --> 02:05:16.780
And basically, what it found is
that reaction time is greatly

02:05:16.780 --> 02:05:19.720
reduced when people
are inhaling.

02:05:19.720 --> 02:05:21.550
So they had people look
at fearful stimuli.

02:05:21.550 --> 02:05:23.500
They looked at their reaction
time to fearful stimuli,

02:05:23.500 --> 02:05:25.125
in other words, their
ability to detect

02:05:25.125 --> 02:05:26.167
certain kinds of stimuli.

02:05:26.167 --> 02:05:28.500
And they were given a lot of
different kinds of stimuli.

02:05:28.500 --> 02:05:30.640
So they had to be able to
discriminate between one

02:05:30.640 --> 02:05:31.895
sort of-- oops, excuse me.

02:05:31.895 --> 02:05:33.520
By the way, folks,
for those listening,

02:05:33.520 --> 02:05:36.580
I just bumped the microphone,
getting rather animated here.

02:05:36.580 --> 02:05:39.130
What the subjects
had to do was detect

02:05:39.130 --> 02:05:41.710
one type of stimulus
versus another stimulus

02:05:41.710 --> 02:05:43.690
that they were being exposed to.

02:05:43.690 --> 02:05:46.360
And what they found is
if people were inhaling

02:05:46.360 --> 02:05:49.750
as that fear-inducing stimulus
was presented, their reaction

02:05:49.750 --> 02:05:51.550
time to notice it was
much, much faster.

02:05:51.550 --> 02:05:54.580
And they related that to
patterns of brain activity,

02:05:54.580 --> 02:05:56.920
and they were able to do that
because they were actually

02:05:56.920 --> 02:05:59.080
recording from
the brain directly

02:05:59.080 --> 02:06:00.070
from beneath the skull.

02:06:00.070 --> 02:06:01.150
And they were able to
do that because they

02:06:01.150 --> 02:06:03.850
had some patients that had
intracranial electrodes

02:06:03.850 --> 02:06:05.830
embedded in their brain
for sake of trying

02:06:05.830 --> 02:06:07.520
to detect epileptic seizures.

02:06:07.520 --> 02:06:10.510
So there's a lot to
this study and a lot

02:06:10.510 --> 02:06:11.530
that we could discuss.

02:06:11.530 --> 02:06:16.330
But the basic takeaway is
that when people are inhaling,

02:06:16.330 --> 02:06:18.610
that is, when
they're drawing air

02:06:18.610 --> 02:06:20.980
in through their
nose in particular,

02:06:20.980 --> 02:06:23.380
their ability to
detect what's going

02:06:23.380 --> 02:06:25.150
on in the world
around them is greatly

02:06:25.150 --> 02:06:28.420
enhanced and not just
for fear but also

02:06:28.420 --> 02:06:30.440
for surprise of all sorts.

02:06:30.440 --> 02:06:32.830
So when people are
inhaling, their ability

02:06:32.830 --> 02:06:37.180
to detect novel stimuli,
things that are unexpected

02:06:37.180 --> 02:06:39.100
or that are unusual
in their environment,

02:06:39.100 --> 02:06:40.823
is significantly increased.

02:06:40.823 --> 02:06:42.740
Again, we'll put a link
to this study as well.

02:06:42.740 --> 02:06:44.865
I find it to be one of the
more interesting studies

02:06:44.865 --> 02:06:47.620
in this realm, although there
are now many additional studies

02:06:47.620 --> 02:06:49.720
that support this
statement that I

02:06:49.720 --> 02:06:51.940
made earlier, which is
that during inhalation,

02:06:51.940 --> 02:06:54.190
also called
inspiration, there are

02:06:54.190 --> 02:06:56.410
a number of very fast
physiological changes,

02:06:56.410 --> 02:06:58.240
such as changes
in pupil diameter,

02:06:58.240 --> 02:07:00.460
changes in the activity
of the hippocampus,

02:07:00.460 --> 02:07:03.220
this memory encoding and
retrieval area of the brain,

02:07:03.220 --> 02:07:04.730
and other areas of the brain.

02:07:04.730 --> 02:07:06.970
So what's the tool
takeaway from this?

02:07:06.970 --> 02:07:12.005
If you are sitting down to read
or research or study or you

02:07:12.005 --> 02:07:13.630
really want to learn
some information--

02:07:13.630 --> 02:07:16.630
maybe you're listening to a
podcast or some other sorts

02:07:16.630 --> 02:07:19.180
of information that
you want to retain--

02:07:19.180 --> 02:07:21.730
it actually makes
sense to increase

02:07:21.730 --> 02:07:26.540
the duration or the intensity
of your inhales as you do that.

02:07:26.540 --> 02:07:29.620
The more that you're inhaling
relative to exhaling in terms

02:07:29.620 --> 02:07:32.800
of duration, the more that
your brain is in this focused

02:07:32.800 --> 02:07:35.830
mode and this mode of being
able to access and retrieve

02:07:35.830 --> 02:07:37.330
information better.

02:07:37.330 --> 02:07:38.973
Now, there's one
caveat to this that I

02:07:38.973 --> 02:07:41.140
think is important because
I know a number of people

02:07:41.140 --> 02:07:43.473
listen to this podcast
for sake of gleaning tools

02:07:43.473 --> 02:07:44.890
not just for
cognitive enhancement

02:07:44.890 --> 02:07:46.420
but for physical enhancement.

02:07:46.420 --> 02:07:50.470
It turns out that when
you are inhaling air,

02:07:50.470 --> 02:07:54.010
you're actually less
able or, I should say,

02:07:54.010 --> 02:07:57.550
less efficient at generating
voluntary movements.

02:07:57.550 --> 02:07:59.862
Now, that might
come as a surprise.

02:07:59.862 --> 02:08:02.320
Up until now, we've basically
been talking about inhalation

02:08:02.320 --> 02:08:04.180
is great, almost to the
point where you wonder like,

02:08:04.180 --> 02:08:05.860
is the exhalation
good for anything?

02:08:05.860 --> 02:08:07.690
You don't want to overbreathe
and kick out too much carbon

02:08:07.690 --> 02:08:08.050
dioxide.

02:08:08.050 --> 02:08:10.008
Well, of course exhalation
is great for things.

02:08:10.008 --> 02:08:12.130
In fact, if you're somebody
that's played baseball

02:08:12.130 --> 02:08:14.320
or softball, what are you told?

02:08:14.320 --> 02:08:15.970
That you should
exhale on the swing

02:08:15.970 --> 02:08:17.920
to generate the maximum
amount of power.

02:08:17.920 --> 02:08:21.460
If you're somebody who has
done martial arts of any kind,

02:08:21.460 --> 02:08:24.010
was traditional Western
boxing, as you strike,

02:08:24.010 --> 02:08:26.950
that's where people
typically do the hiya,

02:08:26.950 --> 02:08:28.930
laying the sort of
classic karate type thing.

02:08:28.930 --> 02:08:29.980
That's more of a movie thing.

02:08:29.980 --> 02:08:32.380
I don't know whether or not
people actually use the hiya.

02:08:32.380 --> 02:08:35.350
But in boxing, oftentimes people
will do [EXHALES SHARPLY]..

02:08:35.350 --> 02:08:39.310
They'll do a rapid exhalation,
a forceful exhalation,

02:08:39.310 --> 02:08:43.420
keeping in mind, again, that
inhales typically are active.

02:08:43.420 --> 02:08:45.730
They engage the
diaphragmatic muscle.

02:08:45.730 --> 02:08:47.950
They engage those
intercostal muscles.

02:08:47.950 --> 02:08:49.720
Whereas exhales
tend to be passive

02:08:49.720 --> 02:08:51.970
unless we take active
control of the exhale.

02:08:51.970 --> 02:08:54.070
And, indeed, our
ability to generate

02:08:54.070 --> 02:08:58.540
fast, directed, so-called
volitional, voluntary movements

02:08:58.540 --> 02:09:02.830
is greatly enhanced if we do
them during the exhale, not

02:09:02.830 --> 02:09:03.670
the inhale.

02:09:03.670 --> 02:09:07.060
Now, with all of that
said, I haven't yet

02:09:07.060 --> 02:09:09.460
really talked about mouth
versus nasal breathing.

02:09:09.460 --> 02:09:11.830
And it really can be a
fairly short discussion

02:09:11.830 --> 02:09:16.840
because what abundant data now
show and has been beautifully

02:09:16.840 --> 02:09:19.600
described in the book called
Jaws, A Hidden Epidemic--

02:09:19.600 --> 02:09:22.090
this is a book that was written
by Paul Ehrlich and Sandra

02:09:22.090 --> 02:09:24.215
Kahn, my colleagues at
Stanford School of Medicine.

02:09:24.215 --> 02:09:27.550
It has an introduction and a
foreword from Jared Diamond

02:09:27.550 --> 02:09:29.020
and from the great
Robert Sapolsky.

02:09:29.020 --> 02:09:30.850
So some real heavy
hitters on this book.

02:09:30.850 --> 02:09:32.650
What that book
really describes is

02:09:32.650 --> 02:09:35.530
that whenever possible,
meaning unless you're

02:09:35.530 --> 02:09:38.890
speaking or eating or you're
exercising or other activities

02:09:38.890 --> 02:09:41.440
require some change in
your pattern of breathing,

02:09:41.440 --> 02:09:44.800
we should really all be striving
to breathe through our nose,

02:09:44.800 --> 02:09:46.300
not through our mouth.

02:09:46.300 --> 02:09:47.800
And that relates
to the increased

02:09:47.800 --> 02:09:49.150
resistance to breathing
through the nose

02:09:49.150 --> 02:09:50.150
we talked about earlier.

02:09:50.150 --> 02:09:53.400
Again, I'll say it a third
time, that increased resistance

02:09:53.400 --> 02:09:55.980
through the nose allows you to
inflate your lungs more, not

02:09:55.980 --> 02:09:56.310
less.

02:09:56.310 --> 02:09:58.310
The other thing that
breathing through your nose

02:09:58.310 --> 02:10:01.128
allows you to do is it both
warms and moisturizes the air

02:10:01.128 --> 02:10:02.670
that you bring into
your lungs, which

02:10:02.670 --> 02:10:04.710
is more favorable
for lung health

02:10:04.710 --> 02:10:06.570
than breathing
through the mouth.

02:10:06.570 --> 02:10:08.550
Hard breathing through
the mouth or simply

02:10:08.550 --> 02:10:11.160
mouth breathing at all is
actually quite damaging

02:10:11.160 --> 02:10:13.085
or can be, I should
say, quite damaging

02:10:13.085 --> 02:10:15.210
to some of the respiratory
functions of your lungs.

02:10:15.210 --> 02:10:17.760
That, of course, does not mean
that you shouldn't breathe hard

02:10:17.760 --> 02:10:19.380
through your mouth when
you're running or sprinting

02:10:19.380 --> 02:10:20.490
or exercising hard.

02:10:20.490 --> 02:10:22.380
But you don't want
mouth breathing

02:10:22.380 --> 02:10:25.650
to be the chronic default
pattern that you follow.

02:10:25.650 --> 02:10:27.480
Nasal breathing is
the best pattern

02:10:27.480 --> 02:10:30.330
of breathing to follow
as a default state.

02:10:30.330 --> 02:10:31.890
Another aspect of
nasal breathing

02:10:31.890 --> 02:10:34.860
that's really beneficial is
that the gas nitric oxide

02:10:34.860 --> 02:10:37.380
is actually created
in the nasal passages.

02:10:37.380 --> 02:10:40.710
It's a gas that can
cause relaxation

02:10:40.710 --> 02:10:44.880
of the smooth muscles that
relate to the vasculature not

02:10:44.880 --> 02:10:46.830
just of your nose
but of your brain

02:10:46.830 --> 02:10:48.790
and for all the
tissues of your body.

02:10:48.790 --> 02:10:52.260
This is why nasal breathing
and not mouth breathing

02:10:52.260 --> 02:10:54.660
is great for when you want
to relieve congestion.

02:10:54.660 --> 02:10:56.635
So a lot of these things
seem counterintuitive.

02:10:56.635 --> 02:10:57.510
Your nose is stuffed.

02:10:57.510 --> 02:11:00.058
So that mainly makes people
breathe through their mouth.

02:11:00.058 --> 02:11:02.100
But it turns out that
breathing through your nose

02:11:02.100 --> 02:11:06.480
will allow some dilation of the
vasculature, more blood flow,

02:11:06.480 --> 02:11:09.840
dilation of the nasal passages,
and delivery of nitric oxide

02:11:09.840 --> 02:11:11.910
to all the tissues of your body.

02:11:11.910 --> 02:11:14.760
And that dilation of
the small capillaries

02:11:14.760 --> 02:11:17.970
that innervate essentially
every organ of your body

02:11:17.970 --> 02:11:19.590
allow the delivery
of more nutrients

02:11:19.590 --> 02:11:23.760
and the removal of carbon
dioxide and other waste

02:11:23.760 --> 02:11:25.740
products from those
tissues more readily

02:11:25.740 --> 02:11:27.510
than if you're not
getting enough--

02:11:27.510 --> 02:11:29.920
excuse me-- nitric
oxide into your system.

02:11:29.920 --> 02:11:31.890
So a lot of reasons to
be a nasal breather.

02:11:31.890 --> 02:11:34.735
If you want to check out that
book Jaws, A Hidden Epidemic,

02:11:34.735 --> 02:11:35.610
it's a terrific read.

02:11:35.610 --> 02:11:38.310
And it also shows some
absolutely striking pictures,

02:11:38.310 --> 02:11:40.350
twin studies and so
forth, and some before

02:11:40.350 --> 02:11:43.170
and afters of people and
the aesthetic changes

02:11:43.170 --> 02:11:45.780
that they experienced when
they shifted from being a mouth

02:11:45.780 --> 02:11:48.000
breather to a nose breather.

02:11:48.000 --> 02:11:50.490
These are striking
examples that have been

02:11:50.490 --> 02:11:51.720
observed over and over again.

02:11:51.720 --> 02:11:53.280
When people mouth
breath, there's

02:11:53.280 --> 02:11:57.030
an elongation of the jaw,
drooping of the eyelids,

02:11:57.030 --> 02:11:58.830
and the entire jaw
structure really

02:11:58.830 --> 02:12:02.040
changes in ways that are
not aesthetically favorable.

02:12:02.040 --> 02:12:05.250
Fortunately, when people switch
to becoming nasal breathers--

02:12:05.250 --> 02:12:07.650
and, of course, that
takes some encouragement

02:12:07.650 --> 02:12:11.010
either by mouth taping or doing
their cardiovascular exercise

02:12:11.010 --> 02:12:13.882
with mouth closed or by
doing the sorts of exercises

02:12:13.882 --> 02:12:15.090
that we talked about earlier.

02:12:15.090 --> 02:12:18.210
When they switch to becoming
nasal breathers by default,

02:12:18.210 --> 02:12:21.600
the aesthetic changes that
occur are very dramatic

02:12:21.600 --> 02:12:25.080
and very favorable,
including elevation

02:12:25.080 --> 02:12:28.080
of the eyebrows, not
in an artificial sense

02:12:28.080 --> 02:12:30.600
or in a kind of outrageous
way, but elevation

02:12:30.600 --> 02:12:32.790
of the cheekbones,
sharpening of the jaw,

02:12:32.790 --> 02:12:36.330
and, most notably, improvements
of the teeth and the entire jaw

02:12:36.330 --> 02:12:36.910
structure.

02:12:36.910 --> 02:12:38.880
In fact, one simple
test of whether or not

02:12:38.880 --> 02:12:41.160
you can be an efficient
nasal breather

02:12:41.160 --> 02:12:43.710
and whether or not you've been
nasal breathing efficiently

02:12:43.710 --> 02:12:46.043
or most of the time in the
past or whether or not you've

02:12:46.043 --> 02:12:48.030
been relying more on
mouth breathing that

02:12:48.030 --> 02:12:49.560
was described in
the book Jaws is

02:12:49.560 --> 02:12:51.900
you should be able
to close your mouth

02:12:51.900 --> 02:12:54.090
and breathe only
through your nose.

02:12:54.090 --> 02:12:56.665
Again, this is at rest, not
during exercise necessarily,

02:12:56.665 --> 02:12:58.290
though you might do
it during exercise.

02:12:58.290 --> 02:13:00.210
But close your mouth,
put your tongue,

02:13:00.210 --> 02:13:02.490
on the roof of
your mouth, and it

02:13:02.490 --> 02:13:05.520
should fit behind your teeth.

02:13:05.520 --> 02:13:08.470
And you should be able to
nose breathe in that position.

02:13:08.470 --> 02:13:10.420
Now, many people won't
be able to do that.

02:13:10.420 --> 02:13:12.330
But fortunately, as
I mentioned earlier,

02:13:12.330 --> 02:13:15.510
if you nasal breathe, that is,
you deliberately nasal breathe

02:13:15.510 --> 02:13:18.250
when at rest for
some period of time,

02:13:18.250 --> 02:13:21.570
you will experience an increased
ability to nasal breathe.

02:13:21.570 --> 02:13:25.110
And you should also experience
some addition of space

02:13:25.110 --> 02:13:28.740
within the palate of your
mouth to allow your tongue

02:13:28.740 --> 02:13:30.990
to sit more completely on
the roof of your mouth.

02:13:30.990 --> 02:13:32.490
This is especially
true for children

02:13:32.490 --> 02:13:33.420
that perform this technique.

02:13:33.420 --> 02:13:35.730
Again, I refer you to the
book Jaws, A Hidden Epidemic.

02:13:35.730 --> 02:13:37.230
It's an absolutely
spectacular book.

02:13:37.230 --> 02:13:40.262
You can also just look online
"before and after Jaws, Hidden

02:13:40.262 --> 02:13:41.970
Epidemic" and look at
some of the changes

02:13:41.970 --> 02:13:43.920
in facial structure
that occur when people

02:13:43.920 --> 02:13:46.290
move from mouth to
nasal breathing,

02:13:46.290 --> 02:13:48.160
and it's really quite striking.

02:13:48.160 --> 02:13:51.090
So during today's
episode, per always, we

02:13:51.090 --> 02:13:52.330
covered a lot of information.

02:13:52.330 --> 02:13:54.630
First, we talked about
the mechanical aspects

02:13:54.630 --> 02:13:58.730
of breathing-- the lungs, the
diaphragm, the trachea, and so

02:13:58.730 --> 02:13:59.230
forth.

02:13:59.230 --> 02:14:01.522
We also talked about the
chemical aspects of breathing,

02:14:01.522 --> 02:14:04.020
that really breathing is
a way that we bring oxygen

02:14:04.020 --> 02:14:07.170
to our cells and that we
get the correct levels

02:14:07.170 --> 02:14:09.900
or, I should say, we maintain
the correct levels of carbon

02:14:09.900 --> 02:14:13.080
dioxide in our system, neither
too much nor too little,

02:14:13.080 --> 02:14:16.050
in order to allow
oxygen to do its magic

02:14:16.050 --> 02:14:18.393
and to allow carbon
dioxide to do its magic.

02:14:18.393 --> 02:14:20.310
Because as you learned
during today's episode,

02:14:20.310 --> 02:14:23.130
carbon dioxide is not
just a waste byproduct.

02:14:23.130 --> 02:14:25.800
It has very critical
physiological functions.

02:14:25.800 --> 02:14:27.630
You need to have
enough of it around.

02:14:27.630 --> 02:14:30.390
And therefore, you don't want
to overbreathe, especially

02:14:30.390 --> 02:14:31.410
at rest.

02:14:31.410 --> 02:14:33.000
We talked about
a tool to measure

02:14:33.000 --> 02:14:35.730
how well you manage carbon
dioxide, the so-called carbon

02:14:35.730 --> 02:14:39.450
dioxide tolerance test,
and various exercises

02:14:39.450 --> 02:14:41.700
that you can use
simply by breathing

02:14:41.700 --> 02:14:44.370
to decrease your
stress in real time,

02:14:44.370 --> 02:14:46.988
decrease your stress
chronically around the clock.

02:14:46.988 --> 02:14:49.530
Obviously, that's a good thing--
improve sleep, improve mood.

02:14:49.530 --> 02:14:51.960
How to increase
breath hold times

02:14:51.960 --> 02:14:53.610
and why you might
want to do that.

02:14:53.610 --> 02:14:55.680
Also how to eliminate hiccups.

02:14:55.680 --> 02:14:57.660
We talked about how
to breathe in order

02:14:57.660 --> 02:14:59.280
to eliminate the
side stitch or side

02:14:59.280 --> 02:15:01.380
cramp that you might
experience during exercise

02:15:01.380 --> 02:15:03.690
and how to breathe in
order to improve learning

02:15:03.690 --> 02:15:07.140
and memory, reaction time,
and various other aspects

02:15:07.140 --> 02:15:09.300
of cognitive and
physical function.

02:15:09.300 --> 02:15:11.110
I do realize it's a
lot of information.

02:15:11.110 --> 02:15:13.440
But as always, I
try and give you

02:15:13.440 --> 02:15:17.250
information that is clear,
hopefully interesting as well,

02:15:17.250 --> 02:15:20.470
and actionable toward a
number of different endpoints.

02:15:20.470 --> 02:15:22.470
So if you're somebody
that's just now starting

02:15:22.470 --> 02:15:24.360
to think about the
application of breathwork,

02:15:24.360 --> 02:15:27.785
I would encourage you to please,
yes, do that carbon dioxide

02:15:27.785 --> 02:15:28.410
tolerance test.

02:15:28.410 --> 02:15:30.420
That will give you some
window into how well

02:15:30.420 --> 02:15:32.190
or how poorly you're
managing breathing.

02:15:32.190 --> 02:15:33.750
And then here's the great news.

02:15:33.750 --> 02:15:35.790
The great news is
that breathwork,

02:15:35.790 --> 02:15:38.130
that is, deliberate
respiration practices,

02:15:38.130 --> 02:15:42.060
are very effective at
creating change very quickly.

02:15:42.060 --> 02:15:45.030
In some cases, such as the
use of the physiological sigh

02:15:45.030 --> 02:15:47.490
or cyclic hyperventilation,
those changes

02:15:47.490 --> 02:15:49.770
can be experienced the
first time and every time

02:15:49.770 --> 02:15:51.930
because, again,
these are not hacks.

02:15:51.930 --> 02:15:55.140
These are aspects of
your breathing apparati,

02:15:55.140 --> 02:15:57.390
including the mechanical
stuff and the neural stuff

02:15:57.390 --> 02:16:00.910
and the gas exchange stuff,
all of which you were born with

02:16:00.910 --> 02:16:03.040
and that are available
to you at any moment.

02:16:03.040 --> 02:16:06.630
So all you really have to do
is explore them and deploy them

02:16:06.630 --> 02:16:07.980
as you feel necessary.

